#: source/command-ref/nix-env/list-generations.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --list-generations` - list profile generations"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/list-generations.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` `--list-generations`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/list-generations.md:11
msgid ""
"This operation print a list of all the currently existing generations for "
"the active profile. These may be switched to using the `--switch-generation` "
"operation. It also prints the creation date of the generation, and indicates "
"the current generation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --query` - display information about packages"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:7
msgid ""
"`nix-env` {`--query` | `-q`} _names…_ \\[`--installed` | `--available` | `-"
"a`\\] \\[{`--status` | `-s`}\\] \\[{`--attr-path` | `-P`}\\] \\[`--no-"
"name`\\] \\[{`--compare-versions` | `-c`}\\] \\[`--system`\\] \\[`--drv-"
"path`\\] \\[`--out-path`\\] \\[`--description`\\] \\[`--meta`\\] \\[`--"
"xml`\\] \\[`--json`\\] \\[{`--prebuilt-only` | `-b`}\\] \\[{`--attr` | `-A`} "
"_attribute-path_\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:25
msgid ""
"The query operation displays information about either the store paths that "
"are installed in the current generation of the active profile (`--"
"installed`), or the derivations that are available for installation in the "
"active Nix expression (`--available`). It only prints information about "
"derivations whose symbolic name matches one of _names_."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:31
msgid "The derivations are sorted by their `name` attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:33
msgid "Source selection"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:35
msgid ""
"The following flags specify the set of things on which the query operates."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:38
msgid "`--installed`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:40
msgid ""
"The query operates on the store paths that are installed in the current "
"generation of the active profile. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:43
msgid "`--available` / `-a`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:45
msgid ""
"The query operates on the derivations that are available in the active Nix "
"expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:48
msgid "Queries"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:50
msgid ""
"The following flags specify what information to display about the selected "
"derivations. Multiple flags may be specified, in which case the information "
"is shown in the order given here. Note that the name of the derivation is "
"shown unless `--no-name` is specified."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:55
msgid "`--xml`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:57
msgid ""
"Print the result in an XML representation suitable for automatic processing "
"by other tools. The root element is called `items`, which contains a `item` "
"element for each available or installed derivation. The fields discussed "
"below are all stored in attributes of the `item` elements."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:63
msgid "`--json`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:65
msgid ""
"Print the result in a JSON representation suitable for automatic processing "
"by other tools."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:70
msgid ""
"Show only derivations for which a substitute is registered, i.e., there is a "
"pre-built binary available that can be downloaded in lieu of building the "
"derivation. Thus, this shows all packages that probably can be installed "
"quickly."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:75
msgid "`--status` / `-s`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:77
msgid ""
"Print the _status_ of the derivation. The status consists of three "
"characters. The first is `I` or `-`, indicating whether the derivation is "
"currently installed in the current generation of the active profile. This is "
"by definition the case for `--installed`, but not for `--available`. The "
"second is `P` or `-`, indicating whether the derivation is present on the "
"system. This indicates whether installation of an available derivation will "
"require the derivation to be built. The third is `S` or `-`, indicating "
"whether a substitute is available for the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:87
msgid "`--attr-path` / `-P`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:89
msgid ""
"Print the _attribute path_ of the derivation, which can be used to "
"unambiguously select it using the `--attr` option available in commands that "
"install derivations like `nix-env --install`. This option only works "
"together with `--available`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:94
msgid "`--no-name`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:96
msgid "Suppress printing of the `name` attribute of each derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:98
msgid "`--compare-versions` / `-c`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:100
msgid ""
"Compare installed versions to available versions, or vice versa (if `--"
"available` is given). This is useful for quickly seeing whether upgrades for "
"installed packages are available in a Nix expression. A column is added with "
"the following meaning:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:105
msgid "`<` _version_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:107
msgid "A newer version of the package is available or installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:109
msgid "`=` _version_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:111
msgid "At most the same version of the package is available or installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:114
msgid "`>` _version_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:116
msgid "Only older versions of the package are available or installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:118
msgid "`- ?`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:120
msgid "No version of the package is available or installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:122
msgid "`--system`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:124
msgid "Print the `system` attribute of the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:126
msgid "`--drv-path`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:128
msgid ""
"Print the [store path](../../glossary.md#gloss-store-path) to the [store "
"derivation](../../glossary.md#gloss-derivation)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:133
msgid "`--out-path`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:135
msgid "Print the output path of the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:137
msgid "`--description`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:139
msgid ""
"Print a short (one-line) description of the derivation, if available. The "
"description is taken from the `meta.description` attribute of the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:143
msgid "`--meta`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:145
msgid ""
"Print all of the meta-attributes of the derivation. This option is only "
"available with `--xml` or `--json`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:633
msgid "To show installed packages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:645
msgid "To show available packages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:656
msgid "To show the status of available packages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:667
msgid "To show available packages in the Nix expression `foo.nix`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:674
msgid "To compare installed versions to what’s available:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:685
msgid "To show all packages with “`zip`” in the name:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:695
msgid "To show all packages with “`firefox`” or “`chromium`” in the name:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:706
msgid "To show all packages in the latest revision of the Nixpkgs repository:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/query.md:708
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ nix-env --file https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/archive/master.tar.gz --"
"query --available\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/rollback.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --rollback` - set user environment to previous generation"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/rollback.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` `--rollback`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/rollback.md:11
msgid ""
"This operation switches to the “previous” generation of the active profile, "
"that is, the highest numbered generation lower than the current generation, "
"if it exists. It is just a convenience wrapper around `--list-generations` "
"and `--switch-generation`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --set-flag` - modify meta attributes of installed packages"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` `--set-flag` _name_ _value_ _drvnames_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:11
msgid ""
"The `--set-flag` operation allows meta attributes of installed packages to "
"be modified. There are several attributes that can be usefully modified, "
"because they affect the behaviour of `nix-env` or the user environment build "
"script:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:16
msgid ""
"`priority` can be changed to resolve filename clashes. The user environment "
"build script uses the `meta.priority` attribute of derivations to resolve "
"filename collisions between packages. Lower priority values denote a higher "
"priority. For instance, the GCC wrapper package and the Binutils package in "
"Nixpkgs both have a file `bin/ld`, so previously if you tried to install "
"both you would get a collision. Now, on the other hand, the GCC wrapper "
"declares a higher priority than Binutils, so the former’s `bin/ld` is "
"symlinked in the user environment."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:26
msgid ""
"`keep` can be set to `true` to prevent the package from being upgraded or "
"replaced. This is useful if you want to hang on to an older version of a "
"package."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:30
msgid ""
"`active` can be set to `false` to “disable” the package. That is, no "
"symlinks will be generated to the files of the package, but it remains part "
"of the profile (so it won’t be garbage-collected). It can be set back to "
"`true` to re-enable the package."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:511
msgid "To prevent the currently installed Firefox from being upgraded:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:517
msgid "After this, `nix-env --upgrade ` will ignore Firefox."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:519
msgid ""
"To disable the currently installed Firefox, then install a new Firefox while "
"the old remains part of the profile:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set-flag.md:544
msgid "To make files from `binutils` take precedence over files from `gcc`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --set` - set profile to contain a specified derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` `--set` _drvname_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set.md:11
msgid ""
"The `--set` operation modifies the current generation of a profile so that "
"it contains exactly the specified derivation, and nothing else."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/set.md:499
msgid ""
"The following updates a profile such that its current generation will "
"contain just Firefox:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-generation.md:3
msgid ""
"`nix-env --switch-generation` - set user environment to given profile "
"generation"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-generation.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` {`--switch-generation` | `-G`} _generation_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-generation.md:11
msgid ""
"This operation makes generation number _generation_ the current generation "
"of the active profile. That is, if the `profile` is the path to the active "
"profile, then the symlink `profile` is made to point to `profile-generation-"
"link`, which is in turn a symlink to the actual user environment in the Nix "
"store."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-generation.md:17
msgid "Switching will fail if the specified generation does not exist."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-profile.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --switch-profile` - set user environment to given profile"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-profile.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` {`--switch-profile` | `-S`} _path_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/switch-profile.md:11
msgid ""
"This operation makes _path_ the current profile for the user. That is, the "
"symlink `~/.nix-profile` is made to point to _path_."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/uninstall.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --uninstall` - remove packages from user environment"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/uninstall.md:7
msgid "`nix-env` {`--uninstall` | `-e`} _drvnames…_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/uninstall.md:11
msgid ""
"The uninstall operation creates a new user environment, based on the current "
"generation of the active profile, from which the store paths designated by "
"the symbolic names _drvnames_ are removed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:3
msgid "`nix-env --upgrade` - upgrade packages in user environment"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:7
msgid ""
"`nix-env` {`--upgrade` | `-u`} _args_ \\[`--lt` | `--leq` | `--eq` | `--"
"always`\\] \\[{`--prebuilt-only` | `-b`}\\] \\[{`--attr` | `-A`}\\] \\[`--"
"from-expression`\\] \\[`-E`\\] \\[`--from-profile` _path_\\] \\[`--preserve-"
"installed` | `-P`\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:17
msgid ""
"The upgrade operation creates a new user environment, based on the current "
"generation of the active profile, in which all store paths are replaced for "
"which there are newer versions in the set of paths described by _args_. "
"Paths for which there are no newer versions are left untouched; this is not "
"an error. It is also not an error if an element of _args_ matches no "
"installed derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:24
msgid ""
"For a description of how _args_ is mapped to a set of store paths, see [`--"
"install`](./install.md). If _args_ describes multiple store paths with the "
"same symbolic name, only the one with the highest version is installed."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:29
msgid "Flags"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:31
msgid "`--lt`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:33
msgid "Only upgrade a derivation to newer versions. This is the default."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:35
msgid "`--leq`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:37
msgid ""
"In addition to upgrading to newer versions, also “upgrade” to derivations "
"that have the same version. Version are not a unique identification of a "
"derivation, so there may be many derivations that have the same version. "
"This flag may be useful to force “synchronisation” between the installed and "
"available derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:43
msgid "`--eq`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:45
msgid ""
"_Only_ “upgrade” to derivations that have the same version. This may not "
"seem very useful, but it actually is, e.g., when there is a new release of "
"Nixpkgs and you want to replace installed applications with the same "
"versions built against newer dependencies (to reduce the number of "
"dependencies floating around on your system)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:51
msgid "`--always`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:53
msgid ""
"In addition to upgrading to newer versions, also “upgrade” to derivations "
"that have the same or a lower version. I.e., derivations may actually be "
"downgraded depending on what is available in the active Nix expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:564
msgid "When there are no updates available, nothing will happen:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:570
msgid ""
"Using `-A` is preferred when possible, as it is faster and unambiguous but "
"it is also possible to upgrade to a specific version by matching the "
"derivation name:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:578
msgid ""
"To try to upgrade everything (matching packages based on the part of the "
"derivation name without version):"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:587
msgid "Versions"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:589
msgid ""
"The upgrade operation determines whether a derivation `y` is an upgrade of a "
"derivation `x` by looking at their respective `name` attributes. The names "
"(e.g., `gcc-3.3.1` are split into two parts: the package name (`gcc`), and "
"the version (`3.3.1`). The version part starts after the first dash not "
"followed by a letter. `y` is considered an upgrade of `x` if their package "
"names match, and the version of `y` is higher than that of `x`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:597
msgid ""
"The versions are compared by splitting them into contiguous components of "
"numbers and letters. E.g., `3.3.1pre5` is split into `[3, 3, 1, \"pre\", "
"5]`. These lists are then compared lexicographically (from left to right). "
"Corresponding components `a` and `b` are compared as follows. If they are "
"both numbers, integer comparison is used. If `a` is an empty string and `b` "
"is a number, `a` is considered less than `b`. The special string component "
"`pre` (for _pre-release_) is considered to be less than other components. "
"String components are considered less than number components. Otherwise, "
"they are compared lexicographically (i.e., using case-sensitive string "
"comparison)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md:608
msgid "This is illustrated by the following examples:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:3
msgid "`nix-shell` - start an interactive shell based on a Nix expression"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:7
msgid ""
"`nix-shell` \\[`--arg` _name_ _value_\\] \\[`--argstr` _name_ _value_\\] \\"
"[{`--attr` | `-A`} _attrPath_\\] \\[`--command` _cmd_\\] \\[`--run` _cmd_\\] "
"\\[`--exclude` _regexp_\\] \\[`--pure`\\] \\[`--keep` _name_\\] {{`--"
"packages` | `-p`} {_packages_ | _expressions_} … | \\[_path_\\]}"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:20
msgid ""
"This man page describes the command `nix-shell`, which is distinct from `nix "
"shell`. For documentation on the latter, run `nix shell --help` or see `man "
"nix3-env-shell`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:26
msgid ""
"The command `nix-shell` will build the dependencies of the specified "
"derivation, but not the derivation itself. It will then start an interactive "
"shell in which all environment variables defined by the derivation _path_ "
"have been set to their corresponding values, and the script `$stdenv/setup` "
"has been sourced. This is useful for reproducing the environment of a "
"derivation for development."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:33
msgid ""
"If _path_ is not given, `nix-shell` defaults to `shell.nix` if it exists, "
"and `default.nix` otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:36
msgid ""
"If _path_ starts with `http://` or `https://`, it is interpreted as the URL "
"of a tarball that will be downloaded and unpacked to a temporary location. "
"The tarball must include a single top-level directory containing at least a "
"file named `default.nix`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:41
msgid ""
"If the derivation defines the variable `shellHook`, it will be run after "
"`$stdenv/setup` has been sourced. Since this hook is not executed by regular "
"Nix builds, it allows you to perform initialisation specific to `nix-shell`. "
"For example, the derivation attribute"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:46
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"shellHook =\n"
"  ''\n"
"    echo \"Hello shell\"\n"
"    export SOME_API_TOKEN=\"$(cat ~/.config/some-app/api-token)\"\n"
"  '';\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:54
msgid ""
"will cause `nix-shell` to print `Hello shell` and set the `SOME_API_TOKEN` "
"environment variable to a user-configured value."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:59
msgid ""
"All options not listed here are passed to `nix-store --realise`, except for "
"`--arg` and `--attr` / `-A` which are passed to `nix-instantiate`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:63
msgid "`--command` _cmd_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:65
msgid ""
"In the environment of the derivation, run the shell command _cmd_. This "
"command is executed in an interactive shell. (Use `--run` to use a non-"
"interactive shell instead.) However, a call to `exit` is implicitly added to "
"the command, so the shell will exit after running the command. To prevent "
"this, add `return` at the end; e.g.  `--command \"echo Hello; return\"` will "
"print `Hello` and then drop you into the interactive shell. This can be "
"useful for doing any additional initialisation."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:74
msgid "`--run` _cmd_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:76
msgid ""
"Like `--command`, but executes the command in a non-interactive shell. This "
"means (among other things) that if you hit Ctrl-C while the command is "
"running, the shell exits."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:80
msgid "`--exclude` _regexp_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:82
msgid ""
"Do not build any dependencies whose store path matches the regular "
"expression _regexp_. This option may be specified multiple times."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:85
msgid "`--pure`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:87
msgid ""
"If this flag is specified, the environment is almost entirely cleared before "
"the interactive shell is started, so you get an environment that more "
"closely corresponds to the “real” Nix build. A few variables, in particular "
"`HOME`, `USER` and `DISPLAY`, are retained.  Note that the shell used to run "
"commands is obtained from [`NIX_BUILD_SHELL`](#env-NIX_BUILD_SHELL) / "
"`<nixpkgs>` from `NIX_PATH`, and therefore not affected by `--pure`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:95
msgid "`--packages` / `-p` _packages_…"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:97
msgid ""
"Set up an environment in which the specified packages are present. The "
"command line arguments are interpreted as attribute names inside the Nix "
"Packages collection. Thus, `nix-shell --packages libjpeg openjdk` will start "
"a shell in which the packages denoted by the attribute names `libjpeg` and "
"`openjdk` are present."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:103
msgid "`-i` _interpreter_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:105
msgid ""
"The chained script interpreter to be invoked by `nix-shell`. Only applicable "
"in `#!`\\-scripts (described below)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:108
msgid "`--keep` _name_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:110
msgid ""
"When a `--pure` shell is started, keep the listed environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:350
msgid ""
"<span id=\"env-NIX_BUILD_SHELL\">[`NIX_BUILD_SHELL`](#env-NIX_BUILD_SHELL)</"
"span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:352
msgid ""
"Shell used to start the interactive environment. Defaults to the `bash` from "
"`bashInteractive` found in `<nixpkgs>`, falling back to the `bash` found in "
"`PATH` if not found."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:357
msgid ""
"The shell obtained using this method may not necessarily be the same as any "
"shells requested in _path_."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:361
msgid "\\*\\*Example"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:363
msgid ""
"Despite `--pure`, this invocation will not result in a fully reproducible "
"shell environment:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:365
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"#!/usr/bin/env -S nix-shell --pure\n"
"let\n"
"  pkgs = import (fetchTarball \"https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/archive/"
"854fdc68881791812eddd33b2fed94b954979a8e.tar.gz\") {};\n"
"in\n"
"pkgs.mkShell {\n"
"  buildInputs = pkgs.bashInteractive;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:576
msgid ""
"To build the dependencies of the package Pan, and start an interactive shell "
"in which to build it:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:589
msgid ""
"The reason we use form `eval ${configurePhase:-configurePhase}` here is "
"because those packages that override these phases do so by exporting the "
"overridden values in the environment variable of the same name. Here bash is "
"being told to either evaluate the contents of 'configurePhase', if it exists "
"as a variable, otherwise evaluate the configurePhase function."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:595
msgid ""
"To clear the environment first, and do some additional automatic "
"initialisation of the interactive shell:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:603
msgid ""
"Nix expressions can also be given on the command line using the `-E` and `-"
"p` flags. For instance, the following starts a shell containing the packages "
"`sqlite` and `libX11`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:607
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ nix-shell --expr 'with import <nixpkgs> { }; runCommand \"dummy\" "
"{ buildInputs = [ sqlite xorg.libX11 ]; } \"\"'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:611
msgid "A shorter way to do the same is:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:619
msgid ""
"Note that `-p` accepts multiple full nix expressions that are valid in the "
"`buildInputs = [ ... ]` shown above, not only package names. So the "
"following is also legal:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:627
msgid ""
"The `-p` flag looks up Nixpkgs in the Nix search path. You can override it "
"by passing `-I` or setting `NIX_PATH`. For example, the following gives you "
"a shell containing the Pan package from a specific revision of Nixpkgs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:632
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"$ nix-shell --packages pan -I nixpkgs=https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/"
"archive/8a3eea054838b55aca962c3fbde9c83c102b8bf2.tar.gz\n"
"\n"
"[nix-shell:~]$ pan --version\n"
"Pan 0.139\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:641
msgid ""
"You can use `nix-shell` as a script interpreter to allow scripts written in "
"arbitrary languages to obtain their own dependencies via Nix. This is done "
"by starting the script with the following lines:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:646 source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:701
msgid "#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:650
msgid ""
"where _real-interpreter_ is the “real” script interpreter that will be "
"invoked by `nix-shell` after it has obtained the dependencies and "
"initialised the environment, and _packages_ are the attribute names of the "
"dependencies in Nixpkgs."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:655
msgid ""
"The lines starting with `#! nix-shell` specify `nix-shell` options (see "
"above). Note that you cannot write `#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell -i ...` "
"because many operating systems only allow one argument in `#!` lines."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:663
msgid ""
"#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
"#! nix-shell -i python3 --packages python3 python3Packages.prettytable\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:674
msgid ""
"Similarly, the following is a Perl script that specifies that it requires "
"Perl and the `HTML::TokeParser::Simple`, `LWP` and `LWP::Protocol::Https` "
"packages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:679
msgid ""
"#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
"#! nix-shell -i perl \n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perl \n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perlPackages.HTMLTokeParserSimple \n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perlPackages.LWP\n"
"#! nix-shell --packages perlPackages.LWPProtocolHttps\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:689
msgid "'https://nixos.org/'"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:702
msgid "'terraform.withPlugins (plugins: [ plugins.openstack ])'"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:709
msgid ""
"You must use single or double quotes (`'`, `\"`) when passing a simple Nix "
"expression in a nix-shell shebang."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:712
msgid ""
"Finally, using the merging of multiple nix-shell shebangs the following "
"Haskell script uses a specific branch of Nixpkgs/NixOS (the 20.03 stable "
"branch):"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:718
msgid ""
"--packages 'haskellPackages.ghcWithPackages (ps: [ps.download-curl "
"ps.tagsoup])'\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:737
msgid ""
"    #! nix-shell -I nixpkgs=https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs/archive/"
"0672315759b3e15e2121365f067c1c8c56bb4722.tar.gz\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:739
msgid ""
"The examples above all used `-p` to get dependencies from Nixpkgs. You can "
"also use a Nix expression to build your own dependencies. For example, the "
"Python example could have been written as:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:744
msgid ""
"#! /usr/bin/env nix-shell\n"
"#! nix-shell deps.nix -i python\n"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:751
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"with import <nixpkgs> {};\n"
"\n"
"runCommand \"dummy\" { buildInputs = [ python3 "
"python3Packages.prettytable ]; } \"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:757
msgid ""
"The script's file name is passed as the first argument to the interpreter "
"specified by the `-i` flag."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-shell.md:759
msgid ""
"Aside from the very first line, which is a directive to the operating "
"system, the additional `#! nix-shell` lines do not need to be at the "
"beginning of the file. This allows wrapping them in block comments for "
"languages where `#` does not start a comment, such as ECMAScript, Erlang, or "
"PHP."
msgstr ""

