#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:3
msgid ""
"`nix-store --add-fixed` - add paths to store using given hashing algorithm"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:7
msgid "`nix-store` `--add-fixed` \\[`--recursive`\\] _algorithm_ _paths…_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:11
msgid ""
"The operation `--add-fixed` adds the specified paths to the Nix store. "
"Unlike `--add` paths are registered using the specified hashing algorithm, "
"resulting in the same output path as a fixed-output derivation. This can be "
"used for sources that are not available from a public url or broke since the "
"download expression was written."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:17
msgid "This operation has the following options:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:19
msgid "`--recursive`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:21
msgid ""
"Use recursive instead of flat hashing mode, used when adding directories to "
"the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:24
msgid ""
"_paths_ that refer to symlinks are not dereferenced, but added to the store "
"as symlinks with the same target."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:29
msgid ""
"The following options are allowed for all `nix-store` operations, but may "
"not always have an effect."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:31
msgid "<span id=\"opt-add-root\">[`--add-root`](#opt-add-root)</span> _path_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:33
msgid ""
"Causes the result of a realisation (`--realise` and `--force-realise`) to be "
"registered as a root of the garbage collector. _path_ will be created as a "
"symlink to the resulting store path. In addition, a uniquely named symlink "
"to _path_ will be created in `/nix/var/nix/gcroots/auto/`. For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:49
msgid ""
"Thus, when `/home/eelco/bla/result` is removed, the GC root in the `auto` "
"directory becomes a dangling symlink and will be ignored by the collector."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:55
msgid ""
"Note that it is not possible to move or rename GC roots, since the symlink "
"in the `auto` directory will still point to the old location."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add-fixed.md:59
msgid ""
"If there are multiple results, then multiple symlinks will be created by "
"sequentially numbering symlinks beyond the first one (e.g., `foo`, `foo-2`, "
"`foo-3`, and so on)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add.md:3
msgid "`nix-store --add` - add paths to Nix store"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add.md:7
msgid "`nix-store` `--add` _paths…_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/add.md:11
msgid ""
"The operation `--add` adds the specified paths to the Nix store. It prints "
"the resulting paths in the Nix store on standard output."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/delete.md:3
msgid "`nix-store --delete` - delete store paths"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/delete.md:7
msgid "`nix-store` `--delete` \\[`--ignore-liveness`\\] _paths…_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/delete.md:11
msgid ""
"The operation `--delete` deletes the store paths _paths_ from the Nix store, "
"but only if it is safe to do so; that is, when the path is not reachable "
"from a root of the garbage collector. This means that you can only delete "
"paths that would also be deleted by `nix-store --gc`. Thus, `--delete` is a "
"more targeted version of `--gc`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/delete.md:17
msgid ""
"With the option `--ignore-liveness`, reachability from the roots is ignored. "
"However, the path still won’t be deleted if there are other paths in the "
"store that refer to it (i.e., depend on it)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump-db.md:3
msgid "`nix-store --dump-db` - export Nix database"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump-db.md:7
msgid "`nix-store` `--dump-db` \\[_paths…_\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump-db.md:11
msgid ""
"The operation `--dump-db` writes a dump of the Nix database to standard "
"output. It can be loaded into an empty Nix store using `--load-db`. This is "
"useful for making backups and when migrating to different database schemas."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump-db.md:16
msgid ""
"By default, `--dump-db` will dump the entire Nix database. When one or more "
"store paths is passed, only the subset of the Nix database for those store "
"paths is dumped. As with `--export`, the user is responsible for passing all "
"the store paths for a closure. See `--export` for an example."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:3
msgid ""
"`nix-store --dump` - write a single path to a [Nix Archive](../../store/file-"
"system-object/content-address.md#serial-nix-archive)"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:7
msgid "`nix-store` `--dump` _path_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:11
msgid ""
"The operation `--dump` produces a [Nix archive](../../glossary.md#gloss-nar) "
"(NAR) file containing the contents of the file system tree rooted at _path_. "
"The archive is written to standard output."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:15
msgid ""
"A NAR archive is like a TAR or Zip archive, but it contains only the "
"information that Nix considers important. For instance, timestamps are "
"elided because all files in the Nix store have their timestamp set to 0 "
"anyway. Likewise, all permissions are left out except for the execute bit, "
"because all files in the Nix store have 444 or 555 permission."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:21
msgid ""
"Also, a NAR archive is _canonical_, meaning that “equal” paths always "
"produce the same NAR archive. For instance, directory entries are always "
"sorted so that the actual on-disk order doesn’t influence the result.  This "
"means that the cryptographic hash of a NAR dump of a path is usable as a "
"fingerprint of the contents of the path. Indeed, the hashes of store paths "
"stored in Nix’s database (see `nix-store --query --hash`) are SHA-256 hashes "
"of the NAR dump of each store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:29
msgid ""
"NAR archives support filenames of unlimited length and 64-bit file sizes. "
"They can contain regular files, directories, and symbolic links, but not "
"other types of files (such as device nodes)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/dump.md:33
msgid ""
"A Nix archive can be unpacked using [`nix-store --restore`](../../command-"
"ref/nix-store/restore.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:3
msgid ""
"`nix-store --export` - export store paths to a [Nix Archive](../../store/"
"file-system-object/content-address.md#serial-nix-archive)"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:7
msgid "`nix-store` `--export` _paths…_"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:11
msgid ""
"The operation `--export` writes a serialisation of the given [store objects]"
"(../../glossary.md#gloss-store-object) to standard output in a format that "
"can be imported into another [Nix store](../../store/index.md) with [`nix-"
"store --import`](./import.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:15
msgid ""
"This command _does not_ produce a [closure](../../glossary.md#gloss-closure) "
"of the specified store paths. Trying to import a store object that refers to "
"store paths not available in the target Nix store will fail."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:18
msgid ""
"Use [`nix-store --query`](../../command-ref/nix-store/query.md) to obtain "
"the closure of a store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:20
msgid ""
"This command is different from [`nix-store --dump`](./dump.md), which "
"produces a [Nix archive](../../glossary.md#gloss-nar) that _does not_ "
"contain the set of [references](../../glossary.md#gloss-reference) of a "
"given store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:24
msgid ""
"For efficient transfer of closures to remote machines over SSH, use [`nix-"
"copy-closure`](../../command-ref/nix-copy-closure.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:504
msgid "Deploy GNU Hello to an airgapped machine via USB stick."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:506
msgid ""
"Write the closure to the block device on a machine with internet connection:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/export.md:513
msgid ""
"Read the closure from the block device on the machine without internet "
"connection:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:3
msgid "`nix-store --gc` - run garbage collection"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:7
msgid ""
"`nix-store` `--gc` \\[`--print-roots` | `--print-live` | `--print-dead`\\] \\"
"[`--max-freed` _bytes_\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:11
msgid ""
"Without additional flags, the operation `--gc` performs a garbage collection "
"on the Nix store. That is, all paths in the Nix store not reachable via file "
"system references from a set of “roots”, are deleted."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:15
msgid "The following suboperations may be specified:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:17
msgid "`--print-roots`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:19
msgid ""
"This operation prints on standard output the set of roots used by the "
"garbage collector."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:22
msgid "`--print-live`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:24
msgid ""
"This operation prints on standard output the set of “live” store paths, "
"which are all the store paths reachable from the roots. Live paths should "
"never be deleted, since that would break consistency — it would become "
"possible that applications are installed that reference things that are no "
"longer present in the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:30
msgid "`--print-dead`"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:32
msgid ""
"This operation prints out on standard output the set of “dead” store paths, "
"which is just the opposite of the set of live paths: any path in the store "
"that is not live (with respect to the roots) is dead."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:36
msgid ""
"By default, all unreachable paths are deleted. The following options control "
"what gets deleted and in what order:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/nix-store/gc.md:39
