#: source/development/testing.md:19
msgid ""
"The unit tests are defined using the [googletest](https://google.github.io/"
"googletest/) and [rapidcheck](https://github.com/emil-e/rapidcheck) "
"frameworks."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:25
msgid "Source and header layout"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:27
msgid "An example of some files, demonstrating much of what is described below"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:62
msgid ""
"The tests for each Nix library (`libnixexpr`, `libnixstore`, etc..) live "
"inside a directory `src/${library_name_without-nix}-test`. Given an "
"interface (header) and implementation pair in the original library, say, "
"`src/libexpr/include/nix/expr/value/context.hh` and `src/libexpr/value/"
"context.cc`, we write tests for it in `src/libexpr-tests/value/context.cc`, "
"and (possibly) declare/define additional interfaces for testing purposes in "
"`src/libexpr-test-support/include/nix/expr/tests/value/context.hh` and `src/"
"libexpr-test-support/tests/value/context.cc`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:65
msgid ""
"Data for unit tests is stored in a `data` subdir of the directory for each "
"unit test executable. For example, `libnixstore` code is in `src/libstore`, "
"and its test data is in `src/libstore-tests/data`. The path to the `src/$"
"{library_name_without-nix}-test/data` directory is passed to the unit test "
"executable with the environment variable `_NIX_TEST_UNIT_DATA`. Note that "
"each executable only gets the data for its tests."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:70
msgid ""
"The unit test libraries are in `src/${library_name_without-nix}-test-"
"support`. All headers are in a `tests` subdirectory so they are included "
"with `#include \"nix/tests/\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:73
msgid ""
"The use of all these separate directories for the unit tests might seem "
"inconvenient, as for example the tests are not \"right next to\" the part of "
"the code they are testing. But organizing the tests this way has one big "
"benefit: there is no risk of any build-system wildcards for the library "
"accidentally picking up test code that should not built and installed as "
"part of the library."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:79
msgid ""
"You can run the whole testsuite with `meson test` from the Meson build "
"directory, or the tests for a specific component with `meson test nix-store-"
"tests`. The environment variables that Google Test accepts are also worth "
"knowing:"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:82
msgid ""
"[`GTEST_FILTER`](https://google.github.io/googletest/advanced.html#running-a-"
"subset-of-the-tests)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:84
msgid "This is used for finer-grained filtering of which tests to run."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:87
msgid ""
"[`GTEST_BRIEF`](https://google.github.io/googletest/"
"advanced.html#suppressing-test-passes)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:89
msgid "This is used to avoid logging passing tests."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:91
msgid ""
"[`GTEST_BREAK_ON_FAILURE`](https://google.github.io/googletest/"
"advanced.html#turning-assertion-failures-into-break-points)"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:93
msgid ""
"This is used to create a debugger breakpoint when an assertion failure "
"occurs."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/testing.md:95
