#: source/introduction.md:1
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "介紹"

#: source/introduction.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix is a _purely functional package manager_.  This means that it treats "
"packages like values in a purely functional programming language — packages "
"are built by functions that don’t have side-effects, and they never change "
"after they have been built.  Nix stores packages in the _Nix store_, usually "
"the directory `/nix/store`, where each package has its own unique "
"subdirectory such as"
msgstr ""
"Nix 是一個 _純粹功能性的軟體包管理器_ 。這表示它把軟體包當作純函數式程式語言"
"中的值來處理——軟體包是由沒有副作用的函數所建置，且建置完成後永遠不會改變。"
"Nix 將軟體包存放在 _Nix store_ ，通常是目錄中 `/nix/store`，每個軟體包都有其"
"獨特的子目錄，例如"

#: source/introduction.md:13
msgid ""
"where `q06x3jll2yfz…` is a unique identifier for the package that captures "
"all its dependencies (it’s a cryptographic hash of the package’s build "
"dependency graph).  This enables many powerful features."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:18
msgid "Multiple versions"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:20
msgid ""
"You can have multiple versions or variants of a package installed at the "
"same time.  This is especially important when different applications have "
"dependencies on different versions of the same package — it prevents the "
"“DLL hell”.  Because of the hashing scheme, different versions of a package "
"end up in different paths in the Nix store, so they don’t interfere with "
"each other."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:27
msgid ""
"An important consequence is that operations like upgrading or uninstalling "
"an application cannot break other applications, since these operations never "
"“destructively” update or delete files that are used by other packages."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:32
msgid "Complete dependencies"
msgstr "完整的依賴"

#: source/introduction.md:34
msgid ""
"Nix helps you make sure that package dependency specifications are "
"complete.  In general, when you’re making a package for a package management "
"system like RPM, you have to specify for each package what its dependencies "
"are, but there are no guarantees that this specification is complete.  If "
"you forget a dependency, then the package will build and work correctly on "
"_your_ machine if you have the dependency installed, but not on the end "
"user's machine if it's not there."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:43
msgid ""
"Since Nix on the other hand doesn’t install packages in “global” locations "
"like `/usr/bin` but in package-specific directories, the risk of incomplete "
"dependencies is greatly reduced.  This is because tools such as compilers "
"don’t search in per-packages directories such as `/nix/store/5lbfaxb722zp…-"
"openssl-0.9.8d/include`, so if a package builds correctly on your system, "
"this is because you specified the dependency explicitly. This takes care of "
"the build-time dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:51
msgid ""
"Once a package is built, runtime dependencies are found by scanning binaries "
"for the [hash parts](./store/store-path.md#digest) of Nix store paths (such "
"as `r8vvq9kq…`). This sounds risky, but it works extremely well."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:55
msgid "Multi-user support"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:57
msgid ""
"Nix has multi-user support.  This means that non-privileged users can "
"securely install software.  Each user can have a different _profile_, a set "
"of packages in the Nix store that appear in the user’s `PATH`. If a user "
"installs a package that another user has already installed previously, the "
"package won’t be built or downloaded a second time. At the same time, it is "
"not possible for one user to inject a Trojan horse into a package that might "
"be used by another user."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:65
msgid "Atomic upgrades and rollbacks"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:67
msgid ""
"Since package management operations never overwrite packages in the Nix "
"store but just add new versions in different paths, they are _atomic_.  So "
"during a package upgrade, there is no time window in which the package has "
"some files from the old version and some files from the new version — which "
"would be bad because a program might well crash if it’s started during that "
"period."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:74
msgid ""
"And since packages aren’t overwritten, the old versions are still there "
"after an upgrade.  This means that you can _roll back_ to the old version:"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:83
msgid "Garbage collection"
msgstr "垃圾回收"

#: source/introduction.md:85
msgid "When you uninstall a package like this…"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:91
msgid ""
"the package isn’t deleted from the system right away (after all, you might "
"want to do a rollback, or it might be in the profiles of other users).  "
"Instead, unused packages can be deleted safely by running the _garbage "
"collector_:"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:100
msgid ""
"This deletes all packages that aren’t in use by any user profile or by a "
"currently running program."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:103
msgid "Functional package language"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:105
msgid ""
"Packages are built from _Nix expressions_, which is a simple functional "
"language.  A Nix expression describes everything that goes into a package "
"build task (a “derivation”): other packages, sources, the build script, "
"environment variables for the build script, etc. Nix tries very hard to "
"ensure that Nix expressions are _deterministic_: building a Nix expression "
"twice should yield the same result."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:113
msgid ""
"Because it’s a functional language, it’s easy to support building variants "
"of a package: turn the Nix expression into a function and call it any number "
"of times with the appropriate arguments.  Due to the hashing scheme, "
"variants don’t conflict with each other in the Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:119
msgid "Transparent source/binary deployment"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:121
msgid ""
"Nix expressions generally describe how to build a package from source, so an "
"installation action like"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:128
msgid ""
"_could_ cause quite a bit of build activity, as not only Firefox but also "
"all its dependencies (all the way up to the C library and the compiler) "
"would have to be built, at least if they are not already in the Nix store.  "
"This is a _source deployment model_.  For most users, building from source "
"is not very pleasant as it takes far too long. However, Nix can "
"automatically skip building from source and instead use a _binary cache_, a "
"web server that provides pre-built binaries. For instance, when asked to "
"build `/nix/store/b6gvzjyb2pg0…-firefox-33.1` from source, Nix would first "
"check if the file `https://cache.nixos.org/b6gvzjyb2pg0….narinfo` exists, "
"and if so, fetch the pre-built binary referenced from there; otherwise, it "
"would fall back to building from source."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:141
msgid "Nix Packages collection"
msgstr "Nix 軟體包集合"

#: source/introduction.md:143
msgid ""
"We provide a large set of Nix expressions containing more than 140,000 "
"existing Unix packages, the _Nix Packages collection_ (Nixpkgs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:146
msgid "Managing build environments"
msgstr "管理建置環境"

#: source/introduction.md:148
msgid ""
"Nix is extremely useful for developers as it makes it easy to automatically "
"set up the build environment for a package. Given a Nix expression that "
"describes the dependencies of your package, the command `nix-shell` will "
"build or download those dependencies if they’re not already in your Nix "
"store, and then start a Bash shell in which all necessary environment "
"variables (such as compiler search paths) are set."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:156
msgid ""
"For example, the following command gets all dependencies of the Pan "
"newsreader, as described by [its Nix expression](https://github.com/NixOS/"
"nixpkgs/blob/master/pkgs/applications/networking/newsreaders/pan/"
"default.nix):"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:164
msgid ""
"You’re then dropped into a shell where you can edit, build and test the "
"package:"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:175
msgid "Portability"
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:177
msgid "Nix runs on Linux, macOS and FreeBSD."
msgstr ""

#: source/introduction.md:179
msgid "NixOS"
msgstr "NixOS"

#: source/introduction.md:181
msgid ""
"NixOS is a Linux distribution based on Nix.  It uses Nix not just for "
"package management but also to manage the system configuration (e.g., to "
"build configuration files in `/etc`).  This means, among other things, that "
"it is easy to roll back the entire configuration of the system to an earlier "
"state.  Also, users can install software without root privileges.  For more "
"information and downloads, see the [NixOS homepage](https://nixos.org/)."
msgstr ""

