#: source/language/builtins.md:2302
msgid ""
"Return the output path of a derivation, literally or using an [input "
"placeholder string](../store/derivation/index.md#input-placeholder) if "
"needed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2306
msgid ""
"If the derivation has a statically-known output path (i.e. the derivation "
"output is input-addressed, or fixed content-addressed), the output path is "
"returned. But if the derivation is content-addressed or if the derivation is "
"itself not-statically produced (i.e. is the output of another derivation), "
"an input placeholder is returned instead."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2309
msgid ""
"_`derivation reference`_ must be a string that may contain a regular store "
"path to a derivation, or may be an input placeholder reference. If the "
"derivation is produced by a derivation, you must explicitly select "
"`drv.outPath`. This primop can be chained arbitrarily deeply. For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2314
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.outputOf\n"
"  (builtins.outputOf myDrv \"out\")\n"
"  \"out\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2320
msgid "returns an input placeholder for the output of the output of `myDrv`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2322
msgid ""
"This primop corresponds to the `^` sigil for [deriving paths](../"
"glossary.md#gloss-deriving-path), e.g. as part of installable syntax on the "
"command line."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2331
msgid ""
"Split the string _s_ into a package name and version. The package name is "
"everything up to but not including the first dash not followed by a letter, "
"and the version is everything following that dash. The result is returned in "
"a set `{ name, version }`. Thus, `builtins.parseDrvName "
"\"nix-0.12pre12876\"` returns `{ name = \"nix\"; version = "
"\"0.12pre12876\"; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2355
msgid "Parse a flake reference, and return its exploded form."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2359
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.parseFlakeRef \"github:NixOS/nixpkgs/23.05?dir=lib\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2363
msgid "evaluates to:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2365
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ dir = \"lib\"; owner = \"NixOS\"; ref = \"23.05\"; repo = \"nixpkgs\"; "
"type = \"github\"; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2376
msgid ""
"Given a predicate function _pred_, this function returns an attrset "
"containing a list named `right`, containing the elements in _list_ for which "
"_pred_ returned `true`, and a list named `wrong`, containing the elements "
"for which it returned `false`. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2392
msgid "Runs in linear time in the size of the _list_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2401
msgid ""
"An enrichment of the built-in path type, based on the attributes present in "
"_args_. All are optional except `path`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2404
msgid ""
"path  \n"
"The underlying path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2407
msgid ""
"name  \n"
"The name of the path when added to the store. This can used to reference "
"paths that have nix-illegal characters in their names, like `@`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2412
msgid ""
"filter  \n"
"A function of the type expected by [`builtins.filterSource`](#builtins-"
"filterSource), with the same semantics."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2416
msgid ""
"recursive  \n"
"When `false`, when `path` is added to the store it is with a [flat hash](../"
"store/file-system-object/content-address.md#serial-flat), rather than a hash "
"of the [NAR serialization](../store/file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-nix-archive) of the file. Thus, `path` must refer to a "
"regular file, not a directory. This allows similar behavior to `fetchurl`. "
"Defaults to `true`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2425
msgid ""
"sha256  \n"
"When provided, this is the expected [content hash](../store/file-system-"
"object/content-address.md) of the path. Evaluation fails if the hash is "
"incorrect, and providing a hash allows `builtins.path` to be used even when "
"the `pure-eval` nix config option is on."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2439
msgid ""
"Return `true` if the path _path_ exists at evaluation time, and `false` "
"otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2449
msgid ""
"Return an [output placeholder string](../store/derivation/index.md#output-"
"placeholder) for the specified _output_ that will be substituted by the "
"corresponding [output path](../glossary.md#gloss-output-path) at build time."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2455
msgid "Typical outputs would be `\"out\"`, `\"bin\"` or `\"dev\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2464
msgid ""
"Return the contents of the directory _path_ as a set mapping directory "
"entries to the corresponding file type. For instance, if directory `A` "
"contains a regular file `B` and another directory `C`, then "
"`builtins.readDir ./A` returns the set"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2469
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ B = \"regular\"; C = \"directory\"; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2473
msgid ""
"The possible values for the file type are `\"regular\"`, `\"directory\"`, "
"`\"symlink\"` and `\"unknown\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2483
msgid "Return the contents of the file _path_ as a string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2492
msgid ""
"Determine the directory entry type of a filesystem node, being one of "
"`\"directory\"`, `\"regular\"`, `\"symlink\"`, or `\"unknown\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2502
msgid ""
"Remove the attributes listed in _list_ from _set_. The attributes don’t have "
"to exist in _set_. For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2505
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"removeAttrs { x = 1; y = 2; z = 3; } [ \"a\" \"x\" \"z\" ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2509
msgid "evaluates to `{ y = 2; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2511
msgid ""
"Has `O(n + k log k)` time complexity, where `n` is number of attributes in "
"the _set_ and `k` is the size of _list_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2520
msgid ""
"Given string _s_, replace every occurrence of the strings in _from_ with the "
"corresponding string in _to_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2523
msgid ""
"The argument _to_ is lazy, that is, it is only evaluated when its "
"corresponding pattern in _from_ is matched in the string _s_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2527
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.replaceStrings [\"oo\" \"a\"] [\"a\" \"i\"] \"foobar\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2531
msgid "evaluates to `\"fabir\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2533
msgid ""
"Has `O(n k)` time complexity, where `n` is the length of _s_ and `k` is the "
"number of replacements."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2542
msgid ""
"Load, parse, and return the Nix expression in the file _path_, with the "
"attributes from _scope_ available as variables in the lexical scope of the "
"imported file."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2544
msgid ""
"This function is similar to [`import`](#builtins-import), but allows you to "
"provide additional variables that will be available in the scope of the "
"imported expression. The _scope_ argument must be an attribute set; each "
"attribute becomes a variable available in the imported file. Built-in "
"functions and values remain accessible unless shadowed by _scope_ attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2550
msgid ""
"Variables from _scope_ shadow built-ins with the same name, allowing you to "
"override built-ins for the imported expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2554
msgid ""
"Unlike [`import`](#builtins-import), `scopedImport` does not memoize "
"evaluation results. While the parsing result may be reused, each call "
"produces a distinct value. This is observable through performance and side "
"effects such as [`builtins.trace`](#builtins-trace)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2564
msgid "Create a file `greet.nix`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2566
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"# greet.nix\n"
"\"${greeting}, ${name}!\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2571
msgid "Import it with additional variables in scope:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2573
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"scopedImport { greeting = \"Hello\"; name = \"World\"; } ./greet.nix\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2577
msgid ""
"    \"Hello, World!\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2579
msgid ""
"Evaluation aborts if the file doesn't exist or contains an invalid Nix "
"expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2588
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_, then evaluate and return _e2_. This ensures that a "
"computation is strict in the value of _e1_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2598
msgid ""
"Return _list_ in sorted order. It repeatedly calls the function _comparator_ "
"with two elements. The comparator should return `true` if the first element "
"is less than the second, and `false` otherwise. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2607
msgid "produces the list `[ 42 77 147 249 483 526 ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2609
msgid ""
"This is a stable sort: it preserves the relative order of elements deemed "
"equal by the comparator."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2612
msgid ""
"_comparator_ must impose a strict weak ordering on the set of values in the "
"_list_. This means that for any elements _a_, _b_ and _c_ from the _list_, "
"_comparator_ must satisfy the following relations:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2616
msgid "Transitivity"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2618
msgid ""
"If a is less than b and b is less than c, then it follows that a is less "
"than c."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2624
msgid "Irreflexivity"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2630
msgid "Transitivity of equivalence"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2632
msgid ""
"First, two values a and b are considered equivalent with respect to the "
"comparator if:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2638
msgid "In other words, neither is considered \"less than\" the other."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2640
msgid "Transitivity of equivalence means:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2642
msgid ""
"If a is equivalent to b, and b is equivalent to c, then a must also be "
"equivalent to c."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2651
msgid ""
"If the _comparator_ violates any of these properties, then `builtins.sort` "
"reorders elements in an unspecified manner."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2654
msgid ""
"Runs in `O(n log n)` time on average, where `n` is the size of the _list_. "
"Uses an adaptive sort that exploits existing sorted runs in the input, down "
"to `O(n)` when the list is already sorted."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2664
msgid ""
"Returns a list composed of non matched strings interleaved with the lists of "
"the [extended POSIX regular expression](http://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/"
"9699919799/basedefs/V1_chap09.html#tag_09_04) _regex_ matches of _str_. Each "
"item in the lists of matched sequences is a regex group."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2670
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.split \"(a)b\" \"abc\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2674
msgid "Evaluates to `[ \"\" [ \"a\" ] \"c\" ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2676
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.split \"([ac])\" \"abc\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2680
msgid "Evaluates to `[ \"\" [ \"a\" ] \"b\" [ \"c\" ] \"\" ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2682
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.split \"(a)|(c)\" \"abc\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2686
msgid "Evaluates to `[ \"\" [ \"a\" null ] \"b\" [ null \"c\" ] \"\" ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2688
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.split \"([[:upper:]]+)\" \" FOO \"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2692
msgid "Evaluates to `[ \" \" [ \"FOO\" ] \" \" ]`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2701
msgid ""
"Split a string representing a version into its components, by the same "
"version splitting logic underlying the version comparison in [`nix-env -u`]"
"(../command-ref/nix-env/upgrade.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2712
msgid ""
"Logical file system location of the [Nix store](../glossary.md#gloss-store) "
"currently in use."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2714
msgid ""
"This value is determined by the `store` parameter in [Store URLs](../store/"
"types/index.md#store-url-format):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2716
msgid ""
"```shell-session\n"
"$ nix-instantiate --store 'dummy://?store=/blah' --eval --expr "
"builtins.storeDir\n"
"\"/blah\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2728
msgid ""
"This function allows you to define a dependency on an already existing store "
"path. For example, the derivation attribute `src = builtins.storePath /nix/"
"store/f1d18v1y…-source` causes the derivation to depend on the specified "
"path, which must exist or be substitutable. Note that this differs from a "
"plain path (e.g. `src = /nix/store/f1d18v1y…-source`) in that the latter "
"causes the path to be _copied_ again to the Nix store, resulting in a new "
"path (e.g. `/nix/store/ld01dnzc…-source-source`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2739
msgid "See also [`builtins.fetchClosure`](#builtins-fetchClosure)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2748
msgid ""
"Return the number of bytes of the string _e_. If _e_ is not a string, "
"evaluation is aborted."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2758
msgid "Return the difference between the numbers _e1_ and _e2_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2767
msgid ""
"Return the substring of _s_ from byte position _start_ (zero-based) up to "
"but not including _start + len_. If _start_ is greater than the length of "
"the string, an empty string is returned. If _start + len_ lies beyond the "
"end of the string or _len_ is `-1`, only the substring up to the end of the "
"string is returned. _start_ must be non-negative. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2775
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.substring 0 3 \"nixos\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2779
msgid "evaluates to `\"nix\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2788
msgid ""
"Return the list without its first item; abort evaluation if the argument "
"isn’t a list or is an empty list."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2793
msgid ""
"This function should generally be avoided since it's inefficient: unlike "
"Haskell's `tail`, it takes O(n) time, so recursing over a list by repeatedly "
"calling `tail` takes O(n^2) time."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2804
msgid ""
"Throw an error message _s_. This usually aborts Nix expression evaluation, "
"but in `nix-env -qa` and other commands that try to evaluate a set of "
"derivations to get information about those derivations, a derivation that "
"throws an error is silently skipped (which is not the case for `abort`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2817
msgid ""
"Store the string _s_ in a file in the Nix store and return its path.  The "
"file has suffix _name_. This file can be used as an input to derivations. "
"One application is to write builders “inline”. For instance, the following "
"Nix expression combines the Nix expression for GNU Hello and its build "
"script into one file:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2823
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ stdenv, fetchurl, perl }:\n"
"\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation {\n"
"  name = \"hello-2.1.1\";\n"
"\n"
"  builder = builtins.toFile \"builder.sh\" \"\n"
"    source $stdenv/setup\n"
"\n"
"    PATH=$perl/bin:$PATH\n"
"\n"
"    tar xvfz $src\n"
"    cd hello-*\n"
"    ./configure --prefix=$out\n"
"    make\n"
"    make install\n"
"  \";\n"
"\n"
"  src = fetchurl {\n"
"    url = \"http://ftp.nluug.nl/pub/gnu/hello/hello-2.1.1.tar.gz\";\n"
"    sha256 = \"1md7jsfd8pa45z73bz1kszpp01yw6x5ljkjk2hx7wl800any6465\";\n"
"  };\n"
"  inherit perl;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2849
msgid "It is even possible for one file to refer to another, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2851
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builder = let\n"
"  configFile = builtins.toFile \"foo.conf\" \"\n"
"    # This is some dummy configuration file.\n"
"    ...\n"
"  \";\n"
"in builtins.toFile \"builder.sh\" \"\n"
"  source $stdenv/setup\n"
"  ...\n"
"  cp ${configFile} $out/etc/foo.conf\n"
"\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2864
msgid ""
"Note that `${configFile}` is a [string interpolation](../language/"
"types.md#type-string), so the result of the expression `configFile` (i.e., a "
"path like `/nix/store/m7p7jfny445k...-foo.conf`) will be spliced into the "
"resulting string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2870
msgid ""
"It is however _not_ allowed to have files mutually referring to each other, "
"like so:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2873
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  foo = builtins.toFile \"foo\" \"...${bar}...\";\n"
"  bar = builtins.toFile \"bar\" \"...${foo}...\";\n"
"in foo\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2880
msgid ""
"This is not allowed because it would cause a cyclic dependency in the "
"computation of the cryptographic hashes for `foo` and `bar`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2883
msgid ""
"It is also not possible to reference the result of a derivation. If you are "
"using Nixpkgs, the `writeTextFile` function is able to do that."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2894
msgid ""
"Return a string containing a JSON representation of _e_. Strings, integers, "
"floats, booleans, nulls and lists are mapped to their JSON equivalents. Sets "
"(except derivations) are represented as objects. Derivations are translated "
"to a JSON string containing the derivation’s output path. Paths are copied "
"to the store and represented as a JSON string of the resulting store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2908
msgid ""
"**DEPRECATED.** Use `/. + \"/path\"` to convert a string into an absolute "
"path. For relative paths, use `./. + \"/path\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2918
msgid "Convert the expression _e_ to a string. _e_ can be:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2920
msgid "A string (in which case the string is returned unmodified)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2922
msgid "A path (e.g., `toString /foo/bar` yields `\"/foo/bar\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2924
msgid "A set containing `{ __toString = self: ...; }` or `{ outPath = ...; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2926
msgid "An integer."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2928
msgid ""
"A list, in which case the string representations of its elements are joined "
"with spaces."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2931
msgid "A Boolean (`false` yields `\"\"`, `true` yields `\"1\"`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2933
msgid "`null`, which yields the empty string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2942
msgid ""
"Return a string containing an XML representation of _e_. The main "
"application for `toXML` is to communicate information with the builder in a "
"more structured format than plain environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2947
msgid "Here is an example where this is the case:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2949
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ stdenv, fetchurl, libxslt, jira, uberwiki }:\n"
"\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation (rec {\n"
"  name = \"web-server\";\n"
"\n"
"  buildInputs = [ libxslt ];\n"
"\n"
"  builder = builtins.toFile \"builder.sh\" \"\n"
"    source $stdenv/setup\n"
"    mkdir $out\n"
"    echo \"$servlets\" | xsltproc ${stylesheet} - > $out/server-conf.xml ①\n"
"  \";\n"
"\n"
"  stylesheet = builtins.toFile \"stylesheet.xsl\" ②\n"
"   \"<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>\n"
"    <xsl:stylesheet xmlns:xsl='http://www.w3.org/1999/XSL/Transform' "
"version='1.0'>\n"
"      <xsl:template match='/'>\n"
"        <Configure>\n"
"          <xsl:for-each select='/expr/list/attrs'>\n"
"            <Call name='addWebApplication'>\n"
"              <Arg><xsl:value-of select=\\\"attr[@name = 'path']/string/"
"@value\\\" /></Arg>\n"
"              <Arg><xsl:value-of select=\\\"attr[@name = 'war']/path/@value\\"
"\" /></Arg>\n"
"            </Call>\n"
"          </xsl:for-each>\n"
"        </Configure>\n"
"      </xsl:template>\n"
"    </xsl:stylesheet>\n"
"  \";\n"
"\n"
"  servlets = builtins.toXML [ ③\n"
"    { path = \"/bugtracker\"; war = jira + \"/lib/atlassian-jira.war\"; }\n"
"    { path = \"/wiki\"; war = uberwiki + \"/uberwiki.war\"; }\n"
"  ];\n"
"})\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:2986
msgid ""
"The builder is supposed to generate the configuration file for a [Jetty "
"servlet container](http://jetty.mortbay.org/). A servlet container contains "
"a number of servlets (`*.war` files) each exported under a specific URI "
"prefix. So the servlet configuration is a list of sets containing the `path` "
"and `war` of the servlet (①). This kind of information is difficult to "
"communicate with the normal method of passing information through an "
"environment variable, which just concatenates everything together into a "
"string (which might just work in this case, but wouldn’t work if fields are "
"optional or contain lists themselves). Instead the Nix expression is "
"converted to an XML representation with `toXML`, which is unambiguous and "
"can easily be processed with the appropriate tools. For instance, in the "
"example an XSLT stylesheet (at point ②) is applied to it (at point ①) to "
"generate the XML configuration file for the Jetty server. The XML "
"representation produced at point ③ by `toXML` is as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3004
msgid "'1.0'"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3004
msgid "'utf-8'"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3008 source/language/builtins.md:3016
msgid "\"path\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3009
msgid "\"/bugtracker\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3011 source/language/builtins.md:3019
msgid "\"war\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3012
msgid "\"/nix/store/d1jh9pasa7k2...-jira/lib/atlassian-jira.war\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3017
msgid "\"/wiki\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3020
msgid "\"/nix/store/y6423b1yi4sx...-uberwiki/uberwiki.war\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3027
msgid ""
"Note that we used the `toFile` built-in to write the builder and the "
"stylesheet “inline” in the Nix expression. The path of the stylesheet is "
"spliced into the builder using the syntax `xsltproc ${stylesheet}`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3039
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_ and print its abstract syntax representation on standard "
"error. Then return _e2_. This function is useful for debugging."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3043
msgid ""
"If the [`debugger-on-trace`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-debugger-on-"
"trace) option is set to `true` and the `--debugger` flag is given, the "
"interactive debugger is started when `trace` is called (like [`break`](../"
"language/builtins.md#builtins-break))."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3056
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_ and print its abstract syntax representation on standard error "
"if `--trace-verbose` is enabled. Then return _e2_. This function is useful "
"for debugging."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3074
msgid "The name `true` is not special, and can be shadowed:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3088
msgid ""
"Try to shallowly evaluate _e_. Return a set containing the attributes "
"`success` (`true` if _e_ evaluated successfully, `false` if an error was "
"thrown) and `value`, equalling _e_ if successful and `false` otherwise. "
"`tryEval` only prevents errors created by `throw` or `assert` from being "
"thrown. Errors `tryEval` doesn't catch are, for example, those created by "
"`abort` and type errors generated by builtins. Also note that this doesn't "
"evaluate _e_ deeply, so `let e = { x = throw \"\"; }; in (builtins.tryEval "
"e).success` is `true`. Using `builtins.deepSeq` one can get the expected "
"result: `let e = { x = throw \"\"; }; in (builtins.tryEval (builtins.deepSeq "
"e e)).success` is `false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3102
msgid ""
"`tryEval` intentionally does not return the error message, because that "
"risks bringing non-determinism into the evaluation result, and it would "
"become very difficult to improve error reporting without breaking existing "
"expressions. Instead, use [`builtins.addErrorContext`](../language/"
"builtins.md#builtins-addErrorContext) to add context to the error message, "
"and use a Nix unit testing tool for testing."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3112
msgid ""
"Return a string representing the type of the value _e_, namely `\"int\"`, "
"`\"bool\"`, `\"string\"`, `\"path\"`, `\"null\"`, `\"set\"`, `\"list\"`, "
"`\"lambda\"` or `\"float\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3123
msgid ""
"Create a copy of the given string where every [derivation deep](../language/"
"string-context.md#string-context-element-derivation-deep) string context "
"element is turned into a [constant](../language/string-context.md#string-"
"context-constant) string context element."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3129
msgid ""
"This is the opposite of [`builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies`](#builtins-"
"addDrvOutputDependencies)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3131
msgid ""
"This is unsafe because it allows us to \"forget\" store objects we would "
"have otherwise referred to with the string context, whereas Nix normally "
"tracks all dependencies consistently. Safe operations \"grow\" but never "
"\"shrink\" string contexts. [`builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies`](#builtins-"
"addDrvOutputDependencies) in contrast is safe because \"derivation deep\" "
"string context element always refers to the underlying derivation (among "
"many more things). Replacing a constant string context element with a "
"\"derivation deep\" element is a safe operation that just enlargens the "
"string context without forgetting anything."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3146
msgid ""
"Discard the [string context](../language/string-context.md) from a value "
"that can be coerced to a string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3155
msgid ""
"`unsafeGetAttrPos` returns the position of the attribute named _s_ from "
"_set_. This is used by Nixpkgs to provide location information in error "
"messages."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3166
msgid ""
"Evaluate _e1_, which must be a string, and print it on standard error as a "
"warning. Then return _e2_. This function is useful for non-critical "
"situations where attention is advisable."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3170
msgid ""
"If the [`debugger-on-trace`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-debugger-on-"
"trace) or [`debugger-on-warn`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-debugger-on-"
"warn) option is set to `true` and the `--debugger` flag is given, the "
"interactive debugger will be started when `warn` is called (like [`break`]"
"(../language/builtins.md#builtins-break))."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3177
msgid ""
"If the [`abort-on-warn`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-abort-on-warn) "
"option is set, the evaluation is aborted after the warning is printed. This "
"is useful to reveal the stack trace of the warning, when the context is non-"
"interactive and a debugger can not be launched."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3189
msgid ""
"Transpose a list of attribute sets into an attribute set of lists, then "
"apply `mapAttrs`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3192
msgid ""
"`f` receives two arguments: the attribute name and a non-empty list of all "
"values encountered for that attribute name."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3195
msgid ""
"The result is an attribute set where the attribute names are the union of "
"the attribute names in each element of `list`. The attribute values are the "
"return values of `f`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3199
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.zipAttrsWith\n"
"  (name: values: { inherit name values; })\n"
"  [ { a = \"x\"; } { a = \"y\"; b = \"z\"; } ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3207
msgid ""
"```\n"
"{\n"
"  a = { name = \"a\"; values = [ \"x\" \"y\" ]; };\n"
"  b = { name = \"b\"; values = [ \"z\" ]; };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/builtins.md:3214
msgid ""
"Has `O(n log n)` time complexity, where `n` is the number of attributes "
"across all sets."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:3
msgid ""
"The most important built-in function is `derivation`, which is used to "
"describe a single store-layer [store derivation](../glossary.md#gloss-store-"
"derivation). Consult the [store chapter](../store/derivation/index.md) for "
"what a store derivation is; this section just concerns how to create one "
"from the Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:7
msgid ""
"This builtin function takes as input an attribute set, the attributes of "
"which specify the inputs to the process. It outputs an attribute set, and "
"produces a [store derivation](../glossary.md#gloss-store-derivation) as a "
"side effect of evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:12
msgid "Input attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:14
msgid "Required"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:16
msgid "\\[`name`\\]{#attr-name} ([String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:18
msgid ""
"A symbolic name for the derivation. See [derivation outputs](../store/"
"derivation/outputs/index.md#outputs) for what this is affects."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:25
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  name = \"hello\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:32
msgid ""
"The derivation's path will be `/nix/store/<hash>-hello.drv`. The [output]"
"(#attr-outputs) paths will be of the form `/nix/store/<hash>-hello[-"
"<output>]`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:35
msgid ""
"\\[`system`\\]{#attr-system} ([String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:37
msgid "See [system](../store/derivation/index.md#system)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:41
msgid "Declare a derivation to be built on a specific system type:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:43
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  system = \"x86_64-linux\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:53
msgid ""
"Declare a derivation to be built on the system type that evaluates the "
"expression:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:63
msgid ""
"[`builtins.currentSystem`](../language/builtins.md#builtins-currentSystem) "
"has the value of the \\[`system` configuration option\\], and defaults to "
"the system type of the current Nix installation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:65
msgid ""
"\\[`builder`\\]{#attr-builder} ([Path](../language/types.md#type-path) | "
"[String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:67
msgid "See [builder](../store/derivation/index.md#builder)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:71
msgid "Use the file located at `/bin/bash` as the builder executable:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:73
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  builder = \"/bin/bash\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:85
msgid "Copy a local file to the Nix store for use as the builder executable:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:99
msgid "Use a file from another derivation as the builder executable:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:101
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {}; in\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  builder = \"${pkgs.python}/bin/python\";\n"
"  # ...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:110
msgid "Optional"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:112
msgid ""
"\\[`args`\\]{#attr-args} ([List](../language/types.md#type-list) of [String]"
"(../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:114
msgid "Default: `[ ]`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:116
msgid "See [args](../store/derivation/index.md#args)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:120
msgid "Pass arguments to Bash to interpret a shell command:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:122
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  builder = \"/bin/bash\";\n"
"  args = [ \"-c\" \"echo hello world > $out\" ];\n"
"  # ...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:131
msgid ""
"\\[`outputs`\\]{#attr-outputs} ([List](../language/types.md#type-list) of "
"[String](../language/types.md#type-string))"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:133
msgid "Default: `[ \"out\" ]`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:135
msgid ""
"Symbolic outputs of the derivation. Each output name is passed to the "
"[`builder`](#attr-builder) executable as an environment variable with its "
"value set to the corresponding [store path](../store/store-path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:138
msgid ""
"By default, a derivation produces a single output called `out`. However, "
"derivations can produce multiple outputs. This allows the associated [store "
"objects](../store/store-object.md) and their [closures](../glossary.md#gloss-"
"closure) to be copied or garbage-collected separately."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:144
msgid ""
"Imagine a library package that provides a dynamic library, header files, and "
"documentation. A program that links against such a library doesn’t need the "
"header files and documentation at runtime, and it doesn’t need the "
"documentation at build time. Thus, the library package could specify:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:148
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  # ...\n"
"  outputs = [ \"lib\" \"dev\" \"doc\" ];\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:156
msgid ""
"This will cause Nix to pass environment variables `lib`, `dev`, and `doc` to "
"the builder containing the intended store paths of each output. The builder "
"would typically do something like"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:166
msgid "for an Autoconf-style package."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:168
msgid ""
"The name of an output is combined with the name of the derivation to create "
"the [name part](../store/store-path.md#name) of the output's store path, "
"unless it is `out`, in which case just the name of the derivation is used."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:173
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  name = \"example\";\n"
"  outputs = [ \"lib\" \"dev\" \"doc\" \"out\" ];\n"
"  # ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:181
msgid ""
"The store derivation path will be `/nix/store/<hash>-example.drv`. The "
"output paths will be"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:183
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example-lib`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:184
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example-dev`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:185
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example-doc`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:186
msgid "`/nix/store/<hash>-example`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:188
msgid ""
"You can refer to each output of a derivation by selecting it as an "
"attribute. The first element of `outputs` determines the _default output_ "
"and ends up at the top-level."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:193
msgid "Select an output by attribute name:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:195
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  myPackage = derivation {\n"
"    name = \"example\";\n"
"    outputs = [ \"lib\" \"dev\" \"doc\" \"out\" ];\n"
"    # ...\n"
"  };\n"
"in myPackage.dev\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:205
msgid ""
"Since `lib` is the first output, `myPackage` is equivalent to "
"`myPackage.lib`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:209
msgid ""
"See [Advanced Attributes](./advanced-attributes.md) for more, infrequently "
"used, optional attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:213
msgid ""
"Every other attribute is passed as an environment variable to the builder. "
"Attribute values are translated to environment variables as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:216
msgid "Strings are passed unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:218
msgid "Integral numbers are converted to decimal notation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:220
msgid ""
"Floating point numbers are converted to simple decimal or scientific "
"notation with a preset precision."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:222
msgid ""
"A _path_ (e.g., `../foo/sources.tar`) causes the referenced file to be "
"copied to the store; its location in the store is put in the environment "
"variable. The idea is that all sources should reside in the Nix store, since "
"all inputs to a derivation should reside in the Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:228
msgid ""
"A _derivation_ causes that derivation to be built prior to the present "
"derivation. The environment variable is set to the [store path](../store/"
"store-path.md) of the derivation's default [output](#attr-outputs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:231
msgid ""
"Lists of the previous types are also allowed. They are simply concatenated, "
"separated by spaces."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/derivations.md:234
msgid ""
"`true` is passed as the string `1`, `false` and `null` are passed as an "
"empty string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:1
msgid "Advanced Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:3
msgid "Derivations can declare some infrequently used optional attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:5
msgid "Inputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:7
msgid ""
"\\[`exportReferencesGraph`\\]{#adv-attr-exportReferencesGraph}  \n"
"Specifies the [_export references graph_](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#export-references-graph) option. The value of this attribute should "
"be a list of pairs `[ name1 path1 name2 path2 ...  ]`. For example, when the "
"following derivation is built:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:12
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"derivation {\n"
"  ...\n"
"  exportReferencesGraph = [ \"libfoo-graph\" libfoo ];\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:19
msgid ""
"the references graph of `libfoo` is placed in the file `libfoo-graph` in the "
"temporary build directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:22
msgid ""
"\\[`passAsFile`\\]{#adv-attr-passAsFile}  \n"
"A list of names of attributes whose environment variables should be marked "
"[_pass as file_](../store/derivation/index.md#pass-as-file). For example, if "
"you have"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:27
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"passAsFile = [\"big\"];\n"
"big = \"a very long string\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:32
msgid ""
"then when the builder runs, the environment variable `bigPath` will contain "
"the absolute path to a temporary file containing `a very long string`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:36
msgid ""
"\\[`__structuredAttrs`\\]{#adv-attr-structuredAttrs}  \n"
"If the special attribute `__structuredAttrs` is set to `true`, the other "
"derivation attributes are serialised into a file in JSON format."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:40
msgid ""
"See the [corresponding section in the derivation page](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#structured-attrs) for further details."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:44
msgid ""
"If set to `true`, the top-level attributes [`allowedReferences`](#adv-attr-"
"allowedReferences), [`allowedRequisites`](#adv-attr-allowedRequisites), "
"[`disallowedReferences`](#adv-attr-disallowedReferences) and "
"[`disallowedRequisites`](#adv-attr-disallowedRequisites) will have no "
"effect; use [`outputChecks`](#adv-attr-outputChecks) instead."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:48
msgid "Output checks"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:50
msgid ""
"These attributes specify [checks on the derivation's outputs](../store/"
"derivation/outputs/index.md#output-checks). The concepts behind them are "
"documented there; this section gives the syntax for specifying them from the "
"Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:53
msgid ""
"Store paths in these lists may be given as strings with context (i.e. by "
"interpolating a derivation), and outputs of the derivation itself are "
"denoted by their plain output names, e.g. `\"out\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:55
msgid ""
"\\[`allowedReferences`\\]{#adv-attr-allowedReferences}  \n"
"Specifies the [_allowed references_](../store/derivation/outputs/"
"index.md#allowed-references) check. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:62
msgid ""
"enforces that the output of a derivation cannot have any runtime "
"dependencies on its inputs. To allow an output to have a runtime dependency "
"on itself, use `\"out\"` as a list item."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:66
msgid ""
"\\[`allowedRequisites`\\]{#adv-attr-allowedRequisites}  \n"
"Specifies the [_allowed requisites_](../store/derivation/outputs/"
"index.md#allowed-requisites) check. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:73
msgid ""
"enforces that the output of a derivation cannot have any other runtime "
"dependency than `foobar`, and in addition it enforces that `foobar` itself "
"doesn't introduce any other dependency itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:77
msgid ""
"\\[`disallowedReferences`\\]{#adv-attr-disallowedReferences}  \n"
"Specifies the [_disallowed references_](../store/derivation/outputs/"
"index.md#disallowed-references) check. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:84
msgid ""
"enforces that the output of a derivation cannot have a direct runtime "
"dependency on the derivation `foo`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:87
msgid ""
"\\[`disallowedRequisites`\\]{#adv-attr-disallowedRequisites}  \n"
"Specifies the [_disallowed requisites_](../store/derivation/outputs/"
"index.md#disallowed-requisites) check. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:94
msgid ""
"enforces that the output of a derivation cannot have any runtime dependency "
"on `foobar` or any other derivation depending recursively on `foobar`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:98
msgid ""
"\\[`outputChecks`\\]{#adv-attr-outputChecks}  \n"
"When using [structured attributes](#adv-attr-structuredAttrs), the "
"`outputChecks` attribute allows defining checks per-output."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:102
msgid ""
"In addition to [`allowedReferences`](#adv-attr-allowedReferences), "
"[`allowedRequisites`](#adv-attr-allowedRequisites), [`disallowedReferences`]"
"(#adv-attr-disallowedReferences) and [`disallowedRequisites`](#adv-attr-"
"disallowedRequisites), the following attributes are available:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:107
msgid ""
"`maxSize` specifies the [_max size_](../store/derivation/outputs/"
"index.md#max-size) check."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:108
msgid ""
"`maxClosureSize` specifies the [_max closure size_](../store/derivation/"
"outputs/index.md#max-closure-size) check."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:109
msgid ""
"`ignoreSelfRefs` controls the [_ignore self references_](../store/derivation/"
"outputs/index.md#ignore-self-refs) behavior."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:113
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"__structuredAttrs = true;\n"
"\n"
"outputChecks.out = {\n"
"  # The closure of 'out' must not be larger than 256 MiB.\n"
"  maxClosureSize = 256 * 1024 * 1024;\n"
"\n"
"  # It must not refer to the C compiler or to the 'dev' output.\n"
"  disallowedRequisites = [ stdenv.cc \"dev\" ];\n"
"};\n"
"\n"
"outputChecks.dev = {\n"
"  # The 'dev' output must not be larger than 128 KiB.\n"
"  maxSize = 128 * 1024;\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:130
msgid "Other output modifications"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:132
msgid ""
"\\[`unsafeDiscardReferences`\\]{#adv-attr-unsafeDiscardReferences}  \n"
"When using [structured attributes](#adv-attr-structuredAttrs), the attribute "
"`unsafeDiscardReferences` is an attribute set with a boolean value for each "
"output name. If set to `true`, it enables the [_unsafe discard references_]"
"(../store/derivation/outputs/index.md#unsafe-discard-references) behavior "
"for that output."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:146
msgid "Build scheduling"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:148
msgid ""
"\\[`preferLocalBuild`\\]{#adv-attr-preferLocalBuild}  \n"
"If set to `true`, enables the [_prefer local build_](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#prefer-local-build) option. This is useful for derivations that are "
"cheapest to build locally."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:152
msgid ""
"\\[`allowSubstitutes`\\]{#adv-attr-allowSubstitutes}  \n"
"If set to `false`, disables the [_allow substitutes_](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#allow-substitutes) option, so Nix will always build this derivation "
"(locally or remotely) rather than substituting its outputs. This is useful "
"for derivations that are cheaper to build than to substitute."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:156
msgid ""
"\\[`requiredSystemFeatures`\\]{#adv-attr-requiredSystemFeatures}  \n"
"Specifies the [_required system features_](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#required-system-features) option: Nix will only build the "
"derivation on a machine that has the corresponding features set in its "
"[`system-features` configuration](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-system-"
"features)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:160
msgid "For example, setting"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:162
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"requiredSystemFeatures = [ \"kvm\" ];\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:166
msgid ""
"ensures that the derivation can only be built on a machine with the `kvm` "
"feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:168
msgid "Impure builder configuration"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:170
msgid ""
"\\[`impureEnvVars`\\]{#adv-attr-impureEnvVars}  \n"
"Specifies the [_impure environment variables_](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#impure-env-vars) option: a list of environment variables that "
"should be passed from the environment of the calling user to the builder. "
"For example, `fetchurl` in Nixpkgs has the line"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:175
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"impureEnvVars = [ \"http_proxy\" \"https_proxy\" ... ];\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:179
msgid ""
"to make it use the proxy server configuration specified by the user in the "
"environment variables `http_proxy` and friends."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:182
msgid "Setting the derivation type"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:184
msgid ""
"As discussed in [Derivation Outputs and Types of Derivations](../store/"
"derivation/outputs/index.md), there are multiples kinds of derivations / "
"kinds of derivation outputs. The choice of the following attributes "
"determines which kind of derivation we are making."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:187
msgid "[`__contentAddressed`](#adv-attr-__contentAddressed)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:189
msgid "[`outputHash`](#adv-attr-outputHash)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:191
msgid "[`outputHashAlgo`](#adv-attr-outputHashAlgo)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:193
msgid "[`outputHashMode`](#adv-attr-outputHashMode)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:195
msgid ""
"The three types of derivations are chosen based on the following "
"combinations of these attributes. All other combinations are invalid."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:198
msgid ""
"[Input-addressing derivations](../store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:200
msgid ""
"This is the default for `builtins.derivation`. Nix only currently supports "
"one kind of input-addressing, so no other information is needed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:203
msgid ""
"`__contentAddressed = false;` may also be included, but is not needed, and "
"will trigger the experimental feature check."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:205
msgid ""
"[Fixed-output derivations](../glossary.md#gloss-fixed-output-derivation)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:207
msgid ""
"All of [`outputHash`](#adv-attr-outputHash), [`outputHashAlgo`](#adv-attr-"
"outputHashAlgo), and [`outputHashMode`](#adv-attr-outputHashMode)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:217
msgid ""
"[(Floating) content-addressing derivations](../store/derivation/outputs/"
"content-address.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:219
msgid ""
"Both [`outputHashAlgo`](#adv-attr-outputHashAlgo) and [`outputHashMode`]"
"(#adv-attr-outputHashMode), `__contentAddressed = true;`, and _not_ "
"`outputHash`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:221
msgid ""
"If an output hash was given, then the derivation output would be \"fixed\" "
"not \"floating\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:223
msgid ""
"Here is more information on the `output*` attributes, and what values they "
"may be set to:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:225
msgid "[`outputHashMode`](#adv-attr-outputHashMode){#adv-attr-outputHashMode}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:227
msgid ""
"This specifies how the files of a content-addressing derivation output are "
"digested to produce a content address."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:229
msgid ""
"This works in conjunction with [`outputHashAlgo`](#adv-attr-outputHashAlgo). "
"Specifying one without the other is an error (unless \\[`outputHash` is also "
"specified and includes its own hash algorithm as described below)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:232
msgid ""
"The `outputHashMode` attribute determines how the hash is computed. It must "
"be one of the following values:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:235
msgid "[`\"flat\"`](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-flat)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:237
msgid "This is the default."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:239
msgid ""
"[`\"recursive\"` or `\"nar\"`](../store/store-object/content-"
"address.md#method-nix-archive)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:241
msgid "**Compatibility**"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:243
msgid ""
"`\"recursive\"` is the traditional way of indicating this, and is supported "
"since 2005 (virtually the entire history of Nix). `\"nar\"` is more clear, "
"and consistent with other parts of Nix (such as the CLI), however support "
"for it is only added in Nix version 2.21."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:248
msgid "[`\"text\"`](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-text)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:252
msgid ""
"The use of this method for derivation outputs is part of the [`dynamic-"
"derivations`](../development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-dynamic-"
"derivations) experimental feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:254
msgid "[`\"git\"`](../store/store-object/content-address.md#method-git)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:258
msgid ""
"This method is part of the [`git-hashing`](../development/experimental-"
"features.md#xp-feature-git-hashing) experimental feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:260
msgid ""
"See [content-addressing store objects](../store/store-object/content-"
"address.md) for more information about the process this flag controls."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:262
msgid "[`outputHashAlgo`](#adv-attr-outputHashAlgo){#adv-attr-outputHashAlgo}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:264
msgid ""
"This specifies the hash algorithm used to digest the [file system object](../"
"store/file-system-object.md) data of a content-addressing derivation output."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:266
msgid ""
"This works in conjunction with [`outputHashMode`](#adv-attr-outputHashAlgo). "
"Specifying one without the other is an error (unless `outputHash` is also "
"specified and includes its own hash algorithm as described below)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:269
msgid ""
"The `outputHashAlgo` attribute specifies the hash algorithm used to compute "
"the hash. It can currently be `\"blake3\"`, `\"sha1\"`, `\"sha256\"`, "
"`\"sha512\"`, or `null`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:272
msgid ""
"`outputHashAlgo` can only be `null` when `outputHash` follows the SRI "
"format, because in that case the choice of hash algorithm is determined by "
"`outputHash`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:274
msgid "[`outputHash`](#adv-attr-outputHash){#adv-attr-outputHash}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:276
msgid ""
"This will specify the output hash of the single output of a [fixed-output "
"derivation](../glossary.md#gloss-fixed-output-derivation)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:278
msgid ""
"The `outputHash` attribute must be a string containing the hash in either "
"hexadecimal or \"nix32\" encoding, or following the format for integrity "
"metadata as defined by [SRI](../glossary.md#gloss-sri). The [\"nix32\" "
"encoding](../protocols/nix32.md) is Nix's variant of Base32 encoding."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:283
msgid ""
"The [`convertHash`](../language/builtins.md#builtins-convertHash) function "
"shows how to convert between different encodings. The [`nix-hash` command]"
"(../command-ref/nix-hash.md) has information about obtaining the hash for "
"some contents, as well as converting to and from encodings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:286
msgid ""
"[`__contentAddressed`](#adv-attr-__contentAddressed){#adv-attr-"
"\\_\\_contentAddressed}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:290
msgid ""
"This attribute is part of an [experimental feature](../development/"
"experimental-features.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:292
msgid ""
"To use this attribute, you must enable the \\[`ca-derivations`\\]\\[xp-"
"feature-ca-derivations\\] experimental feature. For example, in [nix.conf]"
"(../command-ref/conf-file.md) you could add:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/advanced-attributes.md:300
msgid ""
"This is a boolean with a default of `false`. It determines whether the "
"derivation is floating content-addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:1
msgid "Import From Derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:3
msgid ""
"The value of a Nix expression can depend on the contents of a [store object]"
"(../store/store-object.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:7
msgid ""
"Passing an expression `expr` that evaluates to a [store path](../store/store-"
"path.md) to any built-in function which reads from the filesystem "
"constitutes Import From Derivation (IFD):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:9
msgid "[`import`](./builtins.md#builtins-import)` expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:10
msgid "[`builtins.readFile`](./builtins.md#builtins-readFile)` expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:11
msgid "[`builtins.readFileType`](./builtins.md#builtins-readFileType)` expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:12
msgid "[`builtins.readDir`](./builtins.md#builtins-readDir)` expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:13
msgid "[`builtins.pathExists`](./builtins.md#builtins-pathExists)` expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:14
msgid "[`builtins.filterSource`](./builtins.md#builtins-filterSource)` f expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:15
msgid "[`builtins.path`](./builtins.md#builtins-path)` { path = expr; }`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:16
msgid "[`builtins.hashFile`](./builtins.md#builtins-hashFile)` t expr`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:17
msgid "`builtins.scopedImport x drv`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:19
msgid ""
"When the store path needs to be accessed, evaluation will be paused, the "
"corresponding store object [realised](../glossary.md#gloss-realise), and "
"then evaluation resumed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:23
msgid ""
"This has performance implications: Evaluation can only finish when all "
"required store objects are realised. Since the Nix language evaluator is "
"sequential, it only finds store paths to read from one at a time. While "
"realisation is always parallel, in this case it cannot be done for all "
"required store paths at once, and is therefore much slower than otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:28
msgid ""
"Realising store objects during evaluation can be disabled by setting [`allow-"
"import-from-derivation`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-allow-import-from-"
"derivation) to `false`. Without IFD it is ensured that evaluation is "
"complete and Nix can produce a build plan before starting any realisation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:33
msgid ""
"In the following Nix expression, the inner derivation `drv` produces a file "
"with contents `hello`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:35
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"# IFD.nix\n"
"let\n"
"  drv = derivation {\n"
"    name = \"hello\";\n"
"    builder = \"/bin/sh\";\n"
"    args = [ \"-c\" \"echo -n hello > $out\" ];\n"
"    system = builtins.currentSystem;\n"
"  };\n"
"in \"${builtins.readFile drv} world\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:51
msgid ""
"```\n"
"building '/nix/store/348q1cal6sdgfxs8zqi9v8llrsn4kqkq-hello.drv'...\n"
"\"hello world\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:56
msgid ""
"The contents of the derivation's output have to be [realised](../"
"glossary.md#gloss-realise) before they can be read with [`readFile`](./"
"builtins.md#builtins-readFile). Only then evaluation can continue to produce "
"the final result."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:59
msgid "Illustration"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:61
msgid ""
"As a first approximation, the following data flow graph shows how evaluation "
"and building are interleaved, if the value of a Nix expression depends on "
"realising a [store object](../store/store-object.md). Boxes are data "
"structures, arrow labels are transformations."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:95
msgid ""
"In more detail, the following sequence diagram shows how the expression is "
"evaluated step by step, and where evaluation is blocked to wait for the "
"build output to appear."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/import-from-derivation.md:97
msgid ""
"```\n"
".-------.     .-------------.                        .---------.\n"
"|Nix CLI|     |Nix evaluator|                        |Nix store|\n"
"'-------'     '-------------'                        '---------'\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |evaluate IFD.nix|                                    |\n"
"    |--------------->|                                    |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |  evaluate `\"${readFile drv} world\"`                 |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |    evaluate `readFile drv`                          |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |   evaluate `drv` as string                          |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |                |instantiate /nix/store/...-hello.drv|\n"
"    |                |----------------------------------->|\n"
"    |                :                                    |\n"
"    |                :  realise /nix/store/...-hello.drv  |\n"
"    |                :----------------------------------->|\n"
"    |                :                                    |\n"
"    |                                                     |--------.\n"
"    |                :                                    |        |\n"
"    |      (evaluation blocked)                           |  echo hello > "
"$out\n"
"    |                :                                    |        |\n"
"    |                                                     |<-------'\n"
"    |                :        /nix/store/...-hello        |\n"
"    |                |<-----------------------------------|\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |  resume `readFile /nix/store/...-hello`             |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |                |   readFile /nix/store/...-hello    |\n"
"    |                |----------------------------------->|\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |                |               hello                |\n"
"    |                |<-----------------------------------|\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |      resume `\"${\"hello\"} world\"`                    |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    |        resume `\"hello world\"`                       |\n"
"    |                |                                    |\n"
"    | \"hello world\"  |                                    |\n"
"    |<---------------|                                    |\n"
".-------.     .-------------.                        .---------.\n"
"|Nix CLI|     |Nix evaluator|                        |Nix store|\n"
"'-------'     '-------------'                        '---------'\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:1
msgid "Data Types"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:3
msgid "Every value in the Nix language has one of the following types:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:5
msgid "[Integer](#type-int)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:6
msgid "[Float](#type-float)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:7
msgid "[Boolean](#type-bool)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:8
msgid "[String](#type-string)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:9
msgid "[Path](#type-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:10
msgid "[Null](#type-null)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:11
msgid "[Attribute set](#type-attrs)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:12
msgid "[List](#type-list)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:13
msgid "[Function](#type-function)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:14
msgid "[External](#type-external)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:16
msgid "Primitives"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:18
msgid "Integer"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:20
msgid "An _integer_ in the Nix language is a signed 64-bit integer."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:22
msgid ""
"Non-negative integers can be expressed as [integer literals]"
"(syntax.md#number-literal). Negative integers are created with the "
"[arithmetic negation operator](./operators.md#arithmetic). The function "
"[`builtins.isInt`](builtins.md#builtins-isInt) can be used to determine if a "
"value is an integer."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:26
msgid "Float"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:28
msgid ""
"A _float_ in the Nix language is a 64-bit [IEEE 754](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754) floating-point number."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:30
msgid ""
"Most non-negative floats can be expressed as [float literals]"
"(syntax.md#number-literal). Negative floats are created with the [arithmetic "
"negation operator](./operators.md#arithmetic). The function "
"[`builtins.isFloat`](builtins.md#builtins-isFloat) can be used to determine "
"if a value is a float."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:34
msgid "Boolean"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:36
msgid "A _boolean_ in the Nix language is one of _true_ or _false_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:40
msgid ""
"These values are available as attributes of [`builtins`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-builtins) as [`builtins.true`](builtins.md#builtins-"
"true) and [`builtins.false`](builtins.md#builtins-false). The function "
"[`builtins.isBool`](builtins.md#builtins-isBool) can be used to determine if "
"a value is a boolean."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:43
msgid "String"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:45
msgid ""
"A _string_ in the Nix language is an immutable, finite-length sequence of "
"bytes, along with a [string context](string-context.md). Nix does not assume "
"or support working natively with character encodings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:48
msgid ""
"String values without string context can be expressed as [string literals]"
"(string-literals.md). The function [`builtins.isString`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-isString) can be used to determine if a value is a "
"string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:51
msgid "Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:53
msgid ""
"A _path_ in the Nix language is an immutable, finite-length sequence of "
"bytes starting with `/`, representing a POSIX-style, canonical file system "
"path. Path values are distinct from string values, even if they contain the "
"same sequence of bytes. Operations that produce paths will simplify the "
"result as the standard C function [`realpath`](https://pubs.opengroup.org/"
"onlinepubs/9699919799/functions/realpath.html) would, except that there is "
"no symbolic link resolution."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:59
msgid ""
"Paths are suitable for referring to local files, and are often preferable "
"over strings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:60
msgid "Path values do not contain trailing or duplicate slashes, `.`, or `..`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:61
msgid ""
"Relative path literals are automatically resolved relative to their [base "
"directory](../glossary.md#gloss-base-directory)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:62
msgid ""
"Tooling can recognize path literals and provide additional features, such as "
"autocompletion, refactoring automation and jump-to-file."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:66
msgid ""
"A file is not required to exist at a given path in order for that path value "
"to be valid, but a path that is converted to a string with [string "
"interpolation](string-interpolation.md#interpolated-expression) or [string-"
"and-path concatenation](operators.md#string-and-path-concatenation) must "
"resolve to a readable file or directory which will be copied into the Nix "
"store. For instance, evaluating `\"${./foo.txt}\"` will cause `foo.txt` from "
"the same directory to be copied into the Nix store and result in the string "
"`\"/nix/store/<hash>-foo.txt\"`. Operations such as [`import`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-import) can also expect a path to resolve to a "
"readable file or directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:76
msgid ""
"The Nix language assumes that all input files will remain _unchanged_ while "
"evaluating a Nix expression. For example, assume you used a file path in an "
"interpolated string during a `nix repl` session. Later in the same session, "
"after having changed the file contents, evaluating the interpolated string "
"with the file path again might not return a new [store path](../store/store-"
"path.md), since Nix might not re-read the file contents. Use `:r` to reset "
"the repl as needed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:83
msgid ""
"Path values can be expressed as [path literals](syntax.md#path-literal). The "
"function [`builtins.isPath`](builtins.md#builtins-isPath) can be used to "
"determine if a value is a path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:86
msgid "Null"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:88
msgid "There is a single value of type _null_ in the Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:92
msgid ""
"This value is available as an attribute on the [`builtins`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-builtins) attribute set as [`builtins.null`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-null)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:94
msgid "Compound values"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:96
msgid "Attribute set"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:100
msgid ""
"An attribute set can be constructed with an [attribute set literal]"
"(syntax.md#attrs-literal). The function [`builtins.isAttrs`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-isAttrs) can be used to determine if a value is an "
"attribute set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:103
msgid "List"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:107
msgid ""
"A list can be constructed with a [list literal](syntax.md#list-literal). The "
"function [`builtins.isList`](builtins.md#builtins-isList) can be used to "
"determine if a value is a list."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:110
msgid "Function"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:114
msgid ""
"A function can be constructed with a [function expression]"
"(syntax.md#functions). The function [`builtins.isFunction`]"
"(builtins.md#builtins-isFunction) can be used to determine if a value is a "
"function."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:117
msgid "External"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/types.md:119
msgid ""
"An _external_ value is an opaque value created by a Nix [plugin](../command-"
"ref/conf-file.md#conf-plugin-files). Such a value can be substituted in Nix "
"expressions but only created and used by plugin code."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:1
msgid "String context"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:5
msgid ""
"This is an advanced topic. The Nix language is designed to be used without "
"the programmer consciously dealing with string contexts or even knowing what "
"they are."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:8
msgid ""
"A string in the Nix language is not just a sequence of characters like "
"strings in other languages. It is actually a pair of a sequence of "
"characters and a _string context_. The string context is an (unordered) set "
"of _string context elements_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:12
msgid ""
"The purpose of string contexts is to collect non-string values attached to "
"strings via [string concatenation](./operators.md#string-concatenation), "
"[string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md), and similar operations. "
"The idea is that a user can reference other files when creating text files "
"through Nix expressions, without manually keeping track of the exact paths. "
"Nix will ensure that the all referenced files are accessible – that all "
"[store paths](../glossary.md#gloss-store-path) are [valid](../"
"glossary.md#gloss-validity)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:21
msgid ""
"String contexts are _not_ explicitly manipulated in idiomatic Nix language "
"code."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:23
msgid "String context elements come in different forms:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:25
msgid ""
"[deriving path](../glossary.md#gloss-deriving-path){#string-context-element-"
"derived-path}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:27
msgid ""
"A string context element of this type is a [deriving path](../"
"glossary.md#gloss-deriving-path). They can be either of type [constant]"
"(#string-context-constant) or [output](#string-context-output), which "
"correspond to the types of deriving paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:30
msgid "\\[Constant string context elements\\]{#string-context-constant}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:34
msgid ""
"[`builtins.storePath`](./builtins.md#builtins-storePath) creates a string "
"with a single constant string context element:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:36
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.getContext (builtins.storePath \"/nix/store/"
"ikwkxz4wwlp2g1428n7dy729cg1d9hin-hello-2.10\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:40
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  \"/nix/store/ikwkxz4wwlp2g1428n7dy729cg1d9hin-hello-2.10\" = {\n"
"    path = true;\n"
"  };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:52
msgid "\\[Output string context elements\\]{#string-context-output}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:56
msgid ""
"The behavior of string contexts are best demonstrated with a built-in "
"function that is still experimental: [`builtins.outputOf`](./"
"builtins.md#builtins-outputOf). This example will _not_ work with stable Nix!"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:59
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.getContext\n"
"  (builtins.outputOf\n"
"    (builtins.storePath \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-"
"hello-2.12.drv\")\n"
"    \"out\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:66
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-hello-2.12.drv\" = {\n"
"    outputs = [ \"out\" ];\n"
"  };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:76
msgid "\\[_derivation deep_\\]{#string-context-element-derivation-deep}"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:78
msgid ""
"_derivation deep_ is an advanced feature intended to be used with the "
"[`exportReferencesGraph` derivation attribute](./advanced-"
"attributes.html#adv-attr-exportReferencesGraph). A _derivation deep_ string "
"context element is a derivation path, and refers to both its outputs and the "
"entire build closure of that derivation: all its outputs, all the other "
"derivations the given derivation depends on, and all the outputs of those."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:85
msgid ""
"The best way to illustrate _derivation deep_ string contexts is with "
"[`builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies`](./builtins.md#builtins-"
"addDrvOutputDependencies). Take a regular constant string context element "
"pointing to a derivation, and transform it into a \"Derivation deep\" string "
"context element."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:88
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"builtins.getContext\n"
"  (builtins.addDrvOutputDependencies\n"
"    (builtins.storePath \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-"
"hello-2.12.drv\"))\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:94
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  \"/nix/store/fvchh9cvcr7kdla6n860hshchsba305w-hello-2.12.drv\" = {\n"
"    allOutputs = true;\n"
"  };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:105
msgid "Inspecting string contexts"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:107
msgid ""
"Most basically, [`builtins.hasContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-hasContext) "
"will tell whether a string has a non-empty context."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:109
msgid ""
"When more granular information is needed, [`builtins.getContext`](./"
"builtins.md#builtins-getContext) can be used. It creates an [attribute set]"
"(./types.md#type-attrs) representing the string context, which can be "
"inspected as usual."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:116
msgid "Clearing string contexts"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:118
msgid ""
"[`builtins.unsafeDiscardStringContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-"
"unsafeDiscardStringContext) will make a copy of a string, but with an empty "
"string context. The returned string can be used in more ways, e.g. by "
"operators that require the string context to be empty. The requirement to "
"explicitly discard the string context in such use cases helps ensure that "
"string context elements are not lost by mistake. The \"unsafe\" marker is "
"only there to remind that Nix normally guarantees that dependencies are "
"tracked, whereas the returned string has lost them."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:123
msgid "Constructing string contexts"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:125
msgid ""
"[`builtins.appendContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-appendContext) will create "
"a copy of a string, but with additional string context elements. The context "
"is specified explicitly by an [attribute set](./types.md#type-attrs) in the "
"format that [`builtins.hasContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-hasContext) "
"produces. A string with arbitrary contexts can be made like this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:129
msgid ""
"Create a string with the desired string context elements. (The contents of "
"the string do not matter.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:131
msgid ""
"Dump its context with [`builtins.getContext`](./builtins.md#builtins-"
"getContext)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-context.md:132
msgid ""
"Combine it with a base string and repeated [`builtins.appendContext`](./"
"builtins.md#builtins-appendContext) calls."
msgstr ""

