msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:196
msgid ""
"Comparison of distinct [functions](./syntax.md#functions) returns `false`, "
"but identical functions may be subject to [value identity optimization]"
"(#value-identity-optimization)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:197
msgid "Numbers are type-compatible, see [arithmetic](#arithmetic) operators."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:198
msgid "Floating point numbers only differ up to a limited precision."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:200
msgid ""
"The `==` operator is [strict](../language/evaluation.md#strictness) in both "
"arguments; when comparing composite types ([attribute sets](./types.md#type-"
"attrs) and [lists](./types.md#type-list)), it is partially strict in their "
"contained values: they are evaluated until a difference is found. <!-- this "
"is woefully underspecified, affecting which expressions evaluate correctly; "
"not just \"ordering\" or error messages. -->"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:202
msgid "Value identity optimization"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:204
msgid ""
"Nix performs equality comparisons of nested values by pointer equality or "
"more abstractly, _identity_. Nix semantics ideally do not assign a unique "
"identity to values as they are created, but equality is an exception to this "
"rule. The disputable benefit of this is that it is more efficient, and it "
"allows cyclical structures to be compared, e.g. `let x = { x = x; }; in x == "
"x` evaluates to `true`. However, as a consequence, it makes a function equal "
"to itself when the comparison is made in a list or attribute set, in "
"contradiction to a simple direct comparison."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:213
msgid "Logical conjunction"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:217
msgid "_bool1_ `&&` _bool2_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:219
msgid "Logical AND. Equivalent to `if` _bool1_ `then` _bool2_ `else false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:221 source/language/operators.md:272
msgid ""
"This operator is [strict](../language/evaluation.md#strictness) in _bool1_, "
"but only evaluates _bool2_ if _bool1_ is `true`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:225
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"true && false\n"
"=> false\n"
"\n"
"false && throw \"never evaluated\"\n"
"=> false\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:235
msgid "Logical disjunction"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:239
msgid "_bool1_ `||` _bool2_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:241
msgid "Logical OR. Equivalent to `if` _bool1_ `then true` `else` _bool2_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:243
msgid ""
"This operator is [strict](../language/evaluation.md#strictness) in _bool1_, "
"but only evaluates _bool2_ if _bool1_ is `false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:247
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"true || false\n"
"=> true\n"
"\n"
"true || throw \"never evaluated\"\n"
"=> true\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:257
msgid "Precedence and disjunctive normal form"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:259
msgid ""
"The precedence of `&&` and `||` aligns with disjunctive normal form. Without "
"parentheses, an expression describes multiple \"permissible situations\" "
"(connected by `||`), where each situation consists of multiple simultaneous "
"conditions (connected by `&&`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:262
msgid ""
"For example, `A || B && C || D && E` is parsed as `A || (B && C) || (D && "
"E)`, describing three permissible situations: A holds, or both B and C hold, "
"or both D and E hold."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:264
msgid "Logical implication"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:268
msgid "_bool1_ `->` _bool2_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:270
msgid ""
"Logical implication. Equivalent to `!`_bool1_ `||` _bool2_ (or `if` _bool1_ "
"`then` _bool2_ `else true`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:276
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"true -> false\n"
"=> false\n"
"\n"
"false -> throw \"never evaluated\"\n"
"=> true\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:286
msgid "Pipe operators"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:288
msgid "_a_ `|>` _b_ is equivalent to _b_ _a_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:289
msgid "_a_ `<|` _b_ is equivalent to _a_ _b_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:303
msgid ""
"This syntax is part of an [experimental feature](../development/experimental-"
"features.md) and may change in future releases."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/operators.md:307
msgid ""
"To use this syntax, make sure the [`pipe-operators` experimental feature](../"
"development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-pipe-operators) is enabled. "
"For example, include the following in [`nix.conf`](../command-ref/conf-"
"file.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:1
msgid "Language Constructs"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:3
msgid "This section covers syntax and semantics of the Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:5
msgid "Basic Literals"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:9
msgid "See [String literals](string-literals.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:11
msgid "Number"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:15
msgid ""
"Numbers, which can be _integers_ (like `123`) or _floating point_ (like "
"`123.43` or `.27e13`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:18
msgid ""
"Integers in the Nix language are 64-bit [two's complement](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two%27s_complement) signed integers, with a range of "
"-9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807, inclusive."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:22
msgid ""
"Note that negative numeric literals are actually parsed as unary negation of "
"positive numeric literals. This means that the minimum integer "
"`-9223372036854775808` cannot be written as-is as a literal, since the "
"positive number `9223372036854775808` is one past the maximum range."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:25
msgid ""
"See [arithmetic](./operators.md#arithmetic) and [comparison](./"
"operators.md#comparison) operators for semantics."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:32
msgid "_Paths_ can be expressed by path literals such as `./builder.sh`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:34
msgid ""
"A path literal must contain at least one slash to be recognised as such. For "
"instance, `builder.sh` is not a path: it's parsed as an expression that "
"selects the attribute `sh` from the variable `builder`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:38
msgid ""
"Path literals are resolved relative to their [base directory](../"
"glossary.md#gloss-base-directory). Path literals may also refer to absolute "
"paths by starting with a slash."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:43
msgid ""
"Absolute paths make expressions less portable. In the case where a function "
"translates a path literal into an absolute path string for a configuration "
"file, it is recommended to write a string literal instead. This avoids some "
"confusion about whether files at that location will be used during "
"evaluation. It also avoids unintentional situations where some function "
"might try to copy everything at the location into the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:48
msgid ""
"If the first component of a path is a `~`, it is interpreted such that the "
"rest of the path were relative to the user's home directory. For example, `~/"
"foo` would be equivalent to `/home/edolstra/foo` for a user whose home "
"directory is `/home/edolstra`. Path literals that start with `~` are not "
"allowed in [pure](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-pure-eval) evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:52
msgid ""
"Path literals can also include [string interpolation](./string-"
"interpolation.md), besides being [interpolated into other expressions](./"
"string-interpolation.md#interpolated-expression)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:57
msgid ""
"At least one slash (`/`) must appear _before_ any interpolated expression "
"for the result to be recognized as a path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:59
msgid ""
"`a.${foo}/b.${bar}` is a syntactically valid number division operation. `./a."
"${foo}/b.${bar}` is a path."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:62
msgid ""
"[Lookup path](./constructs/lookup-path.md) literals such as `<nixpkgs>` also "
"resolve to path values."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:66
msgid ""
"Lists are formed by enclosing a whitespace-separated list of values between "
"square brackets. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:69
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"[ 123 ./foo.nix \"abc\" (f { x = y; }) ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:73
msgid ""
"defines a list of four elements, the last being the result of a call to the "
"function `f`. Note that function calls have to be enclosed in parentheses. "
"If they had been omitted, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:77
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"[ 123 ./foo.nix \"abc\" f { x = y; } ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:81
msgid ""
"the result would be a list of five elements, the fourth one being a function "
"and the fifth being a set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:84
msgid "Note that lists are only lazy in values, and they are strict in length."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:86
msgid ""
"Elements in a list can be accessed using [`builtins.elemAt`](./"
"builtins.md#builtins-elemAt)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:88
msgid "Attribute Set"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:90
msgid ""
"An attribute set is a collection of name-value-pairs called _attributes_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:92
msgid ""
"Attribute sets are written enclosed in curly brackets (`{ }`). Attribute "
"names and attribute values are separated by an equal sign (`=`). Each value "
"can be an arbitrary expression, terminated by a semicolon (`;`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:96
msgid ""
"An attribute name is a string without context, and is denoted by a [name](./"
"identifiers.md#names) (an [identifier](./identifiers.md#identifiers) or "
"[string literal](string-literals.md))."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:102
msgid "_attrset_ → `{` { _name_ `=` _expr_ `;` } `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:104
msgid ""
"Attributes can appear in any order. An attribute name may only occur once in "
"each attribute set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:109
msgid "This defines an attribute set with attributes named:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:110
msgid "`x` with the value `123`, an integer"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:111
msgid "`text` with the value `\"Hello\"`, a string"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:112
msgid ""
"`y` where the value is the result of applying the function `f` to the "
"attribute set `{ bla = 456; }`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:114
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{\n"
"  x = 123;\n"
"  text = \"Hello\";\n"
"  y = f { bla = 456; };\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:122
msgid ""
"Attributes in nested attribute sets can be written using _attribute paths_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:126
msgid "_attrset_ → `{` { _attrpath_ `=` _expr_ `;` } `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:133
msgid "_attrpath_ = _name_ { `.` _name_ }"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:152
msgid ""
"Attribute names can also be set implicitly by using the [`inherit` keyword]"
"(#inheriting-attributes)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:162
msgid ""
"Attributes can be accessed with the [`.` operator](./operators.md#attribute-"
"selection)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:166
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ a = \"Foo\"; b = \"Bar\"; }.a\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:170
msgid "This evaluates to `\"Foo\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:172
msgid ""
"It is possible to provide a default value in an attribute selection using "
"the `or` keyword."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:176
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ a = \"Foo\"; b = \"Bar\"; }.c or \"Xyzzy\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:180
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ a = \"Foo\"; b = \"Bar\"; }.c.d.e.f.g or \"Xyzzy\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:184
msgid ""
"will both evaluate to `\"Xyzzy\"` because there is no `c` attribute in the "
"set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:186
msgid "You can use arbitrary double-quoted strings as attribute names:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:188
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ \"$!@#?\" = 123; }.\"$!@#?\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:192
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let bar = \"bar\"; in\n"
"{ \"foo ${bar}\" = 123; }.\"foo ${bar}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:197 source/language/syntax.md:211
msgid "Both will evaluate to `123`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:199
msgid ""
"Attribute names support [string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:201
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let bar = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ foo = 123; }.${bar}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:206
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let bar = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ ${bar} = 123; }.foo\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:213
msgid ""
"In the special case where an attribute name inside of a set declaration "
"evaluates to `null` (which is normally an error, as `null` cannot be coerced "
"to a string), that attribute is simply not added to the set:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:217
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ ${if foo then \"bar\" else null} = true; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:221
msgid "This will evaluate to `{}` if `foo` evaluates to `false`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:223
msgid ""
"A set that has a \\[`__functor`\\]{#attr-\\_\\_functor} attribute whose "
"value is callable (i.e. is itself a function or a set with a `__functor` "
"attribute whose value is callable) can be applied as if it were a function, "
"with the set itself passed in first , e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:228
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let add = { __functor = self: x: x + self.x; };\n"
"    inc = add // { x = 1; }; # inc is { x = 1; __functor = (...) }\n"
"in inc 1 # equivalent of `add.__functor add 1` i.e. `1 + self.x`\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:234
msgid ""
"evaluates to `2`. This can be used to attach metadata to a function without "
"the caller needing to treat it specially, or to implement a form of object-"
"oriented programming, for example."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:238
msgid "Recursive sets"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:240
msgid ""
"Recursive sets are like normal [attribute sets](./types.md#type-attrs), but "
"the attributes can refer to each other."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:242
msgid "_rec-attrset_ = `rec {` \\[ _name_ `=` _expr_ `;` `]`... `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:253
msgid "This evaluates to `123`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:255
msgid ""
"Note that without `rec` the binding `x = y;` would refer to the variable `y` "
"in the surrounding scope, if one exists, and would be invalid if no such "
"variable exists. That is, in a normal (non-recursive) set, attributes are "
"not added to the lexical scope; in a recursive set, they are."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:261
msgid ""
"Recursive sets of course introduce the danger of infinite recursion. For "
"example, the expression"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:271
msgid "will crash with an `infinite recursion encountered` error message."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:273
msgid "Let-expressions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:275
msgid ""
"A let-expression allows you to define local variables for an expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:277
msgid "_let-in_ = `let` \\[ _identifier_ = _expr_ `;` \\]... `in` _expr_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:281
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  x = \"foo\";\n"
"  y = \"bar\";\n"
"in x + y\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:288
msgid "This evaluates to `\"foobar\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:290
msgid ""
"There is also another, older, syntax for let expressions that should not be "
"used in new code:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:292
msgid ""
"_let_ = `let` `{` _identifier_ = _expr_ `;` \\[ _identifier_ = _expr_ "
"`;`\\]...  `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:294
msgid "In this form, the attribute set between the `{` `}` is recursive."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:296
msgid ""
"One of the attributes must have the special name `body`, which is the result "
"of the expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:301
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let {\n"
"  foo = bar;\n"
"  bar = \"baz\";\n"
"  body = foo;\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:309
msgid "This evaluates to \"baz\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:311
msgid "Inheriting attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:313
msgid ""
"When defining an [attribute set](./types.md#type-attrs) or in a [let-"
"expression](#let-expressions) it is often convenient to copy variables from "
"the surrounding lexical scope (e.g., when you want to propagate attributes). "
"This can be shortened using the `inherit` keyword."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:336
msgid "and both evaluate to `{ x = 123; y = 456; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:340
msgid ""
"This works because `x` is added to the lexical scope by the `let` construct."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:342
msgid "It is also possible to inherit attributes from another attribute set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:346
msgid "In this fragment from `all-packages.nix`,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:365
msgid ""
"the set used in the function call to the function defined in `../tools/"
"graphics/graphviz` inherits a number of variables from the surrounding scope "
"(`fetchurl` ... `yacc`), but also inherits `libXaw` (the X Athena Widgets) "
"from the `xorg` set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:370
msgid "Summarizing the fragment"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:388
msgid ""
"when used while defining local variables in a let-expression or while "
"defining a set."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:391
msgid ""
"In a `let` expression, `inherit` can be used to selectively bring specific "
"attributes of a set into scope. For example"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:415
msgid "both evaluate to `{ names = [ \"a\" \"b\" ]; }`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:417
msgid "Functions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:419
msgid "Functions have the following form:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:425
msgid ""
"The pattern specifies what the argument of the function must look like, and "
"binds variables in the body to (parts of) the argument. There are three "
"kinds of patterns:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:429
msgid ""
"If a pattern is a single identifier, then the function matches any argument. "
"Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:432
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let negate = x: !x;\n"
"    concat = x: y: x + y;\n"
"in if negate true then concat \"foo\" \"bar\" else \"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:438
msgid ""
"Note that `concat` is a function that takes one argument and returns a "
"function that takes another argument. This allows partial parameterisation "
"(i.e., only filling some of the arguments of a function); e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:443
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"map (concat \"foo\") [ \"bar\" \"bla\" \"abc\" ]\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:449
msgid ""
"A _set pattern_ of the form `{ name1, name2, …, nameN }` matches a set "
"containing the listed attributes, and binds the values of those attributes "
"to variables in the function body. For example, the function"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:458
msgid ""
"can only be called with a set containing exactly the attributes `x`, `y` and "
"`z`. No other attributes are allowed. If you want to allow additional "
"arguments, you can use an ellipsis (`...`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:466
msgid ""
"This works on any set that contains at least the three named attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:469
msgid ""
"It is possible to provide _default values_ for attributes, in which case "
"they are allowed to be missing. A default value is specified by writing "
"`name ?  e`, where _e_ is an arbitrary expression. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:474
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ x, y ? \"foo\", z ? \"bar\" }: z + y + x\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:478
msgid ""
"specifies a function that only requires an attribute named `x`, but "
"optionally accepts `y` and `z`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:481
msgid ""
"An `@`\\-pattern provides a means of referring to the whole value being "
"matched:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:488
msgid "but can also be written as:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:494
msgid ""
"Here `args` is bound to the argument _as passed_, which is further matched "
"against the pattern `{ x, y, z, ... }`. The `@`\\-pattern makes mainly sense "
"with an ellipsis(`...`) as you can access attribute names as `a`, using "
"`args.a`, which was given as an additional attribute to the function."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:502
msgid ""
"`args@` binds the name `args` to the attribute set that is passed to the "
"function. In particular, `args` does _not_ include any default values "
"specified with `?` in the function's set pattern."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:505
msgid "For instance"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:523
msgid "and both expressions will evaluate to:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:529
msgid ""
"All bindings introduced by the function are in scope in the entire function "
"expression; not just in the body. It can therefore be used in default values."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:534
msgid ""
"A parameter (`x`), is used in the default value for another parameter (`y`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:543 source/language/syntax.md:562
msgid "This evaluates to:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:553
msgid ""
"The binding of an `@` pattern, `args`, is used in the default value for a "
"parameter, `x`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:568
msgid ""
"Note that functions do not have names. If you want to give them a name, you "
"can bind them to an attribute, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:571
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let concat = { x, y }: x + y;\n"
"in concat { x = \"foo\"; y = \"bar\"; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:576
msgid "Conditionals"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:578
msgid "Conditionals look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:584
msgid ""
"where _e1_ is an expression that should evaluate to a Boolean value (`true` "
"or `false`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:587
msgid "Assertions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:589
msgid ""
"Assertions are generally used to check that certain requirements on or "
"between features and dependencies hold. They look like this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:596
msgid ""
"where _e1_ is an expression that should evaluate to a Boolean value. If it "
"evaluates to `true`, _e2_ is returned; otherwise expression evaluation is "
"aborted and a backtrace is printed."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:600
msgid ""
"Here is a Nix expression for the Subversion package that shows how "
"assertions can be used:."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:603
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"{ localServer ? false\n"
", httpServer ? false\n"
", sslSupport ? false\n"
", pythonBindings ? false\n"
", javaSwigBindings ? false\n"
", javahlBindings ? false\n"
", stdenv, fetchurl\n"
", openssl ? null, httpd ? null, db4 ? null, expat, swig ? null, j2sdk ? "
"null\n"
"}:\n"
"\n"
"assert localServer -> db4 != null; ①\n"
"assert httpServer -> httpd != null && httpd.expat == expat; ②\n"
"assert sslSupport -> openssl != null && (httpServer -> httpd.openssl == "
"openssl); ③\n"
"assert pythonBindings -> swig != null && swig.pythonSupport;\n"
"assert javaSwigBindings -> swig != null && swig.javaSupport;\n"
"assert javahlBindings -> j2sdk != null;\n"
"\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation {\n"
"  name = \"subversion-1.1.1\";\n"
"  ...\n"
"  openssl = if sslSupport then openssl else null; ④\n"
"  ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:629
msgid "The points of interest are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:631
msgid ""
"This assertion states that if Subversion is to have support for local "
"repositories, then Berkeley DB is needed. So if the Subversion function is "
"called with the `localServer` argument set to `true` but the `db4` argument "
"set to `null`, then the evaluation fails."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:636
msgid ""
"Note that `->` is the [logical implication](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Truth_table#Logical_implication) Boolean operation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:640
msgid ""
"This is a more subtle condition: if Subversion is built with Apache "
"(`httpServer`) support, then the Expat library (an XML library) used by "
"Subversion should be same as the one used by Apache. This is because in this "
"configuration Subversion code ends up being linked with Apache code, and if "
"the Expat libraries do not match, a build- or runtime link error or "
"incompatibility might occur."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:647
msgid ""
"This assertion says that in order for Subversion to have SSL support (so "
"that it can access `https` URLs), an OpenSSL library must be passed. "
"Additionally, it says that _if_ Apache support is enabled, then Apache's "
"OpenSSL should match Subversion's. (Note that if Apache support is not "
"enabled, we don't care about Apache's OpenSSL.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:654
msgid ""
"The conditional here is not really related to assertions, but is worth "
"pointing out: it ensures that if SSL support is disabled, then the "
"Subversion derivation is not dependent on OpenSSL, even if a non-`null` "
"value was passed. This prevents an unnecessary rebuild of Subversion if "
"OpenSSL changes."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:660
msgid "With-expressions"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:662
msgid "A _with-expression_,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:668
msgid ""
"introduces the set _e1_ into the lexical scope of the expression _e2_. For "
"instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:671
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let as = { x = \"foo\"; y = \"bar\"; };\n"
"in with as; x + y\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:676
msgid ""
"evaluates to `\"foobar\"` since the `with` adds the `x` and `y` attributes "
"of `as` to the lexical scope in the expression `x + y`. The most common use "
"of `with` is in conjunction with the `import` function. E.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:684
msgid ""
"makes all attributes defined in the file `definitions.nix` available as if "
"they were defined locally in a `let`\\-expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:687
msgid ""
"The bindings introduced by `with` do not shadow bindings introduced by other "
"means, e.g."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:694
msgid "establishes the same scope as"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:700
msgid "Variables coming from outer `with` expressions _are_ shadowed:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:702
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"with { a = \"outer\"; };\n"
"with { a = \"inner\"; };\n"
"a\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:708
msgid "Does evaluate to `\"inner\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:710
msgid "Comments"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:712
msgid "Inline comments start with `#` and run until the end of the line."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:725
msgid ""
"Block comments start with `/*` and run until the next occurrence of `*/`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:729
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"/*\n"
"Block comments\n"
"can span multiple lines.\n"
"*/ \"hello\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:736
msgid ""
"```console\n"
"\"hello\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:740
msgid "This means that block comments cannot be nested."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/syntax.md:757
msgid ""
"Consider escaping nested comments and unescaping them in post-processing."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:1
msgid "Evaluation"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:3
msgid ""
"Evaluation is the process of turning a Nix expression into a [Nix value]"
"(types.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:5
msgid "This happens by a number of rules, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:6
msgid ""
"Constructing values from literals. For example the number literal `1` is "
"turned into the number value `1`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:8
msgid ""
"Applying operators For example the addition operator `+` is applied to two "
"number values to produce a new number value."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:10
msgid ""
"Applying built-in functions For example the expression `builtins.isInt 1` is "
"evaluated to `true`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:12
msgid ""
"Applying user-defined functions For example the expression `(x: x + 1) 10` "
"can[\\*](#laziness) be thought of rewriting `x` in the function body to the "
"argument, `10 + 1`, which is then evaluated to `11`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:15
msgid ""
"These rules are applied as needed, driven by the specific use of the "
"expression. For example, this can occur in the Nix command line interface or "
"interactively with the [repl (read-eval-print loop)](../command-ref/new-cli/"
"nix3-repl.md), which is a useful tool when learning about evaluation."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:17
msgid "Details"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:19
msgid "Values"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:21
msgid ""
"Nix values can be thought of as a subset of Nix expressions. For example, "
"the expression `1 + 2` is not a value, because it can be reduced to `3`. The "
"expression `3` is a value, because it cannot be reduced any further."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:24
msgid ""
"Evaluation normally happens by applying rules to the \"head\" of the "
"expression, which is the outermost part of the expression. The head of an "
"expression like `[ 1 2 ]` is the list literal (`[ a1 a2 ]`), for `1 + 2` it "
"is the addition operator (`+`), and for `f 1` it is the function application "
"\"operator\" (` `)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:26
msgid ""
"After applying all possible rules to the head until no rules can be applied, "
"the expression is in \"weak head normal form\" (WHNF). This means that the "
"outermost constructor of the expression is evaluated, but the inner values "
"may or may not be. \"Weak\" only signifies that the expression may be a "
"function. This is an historical or academic artifact, and Nix has no use for "
"the non-weak \"head normal form\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:28
msgid "Laziness and thunks"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:30
msgid ""
"The Nix language implements _call by need_ (as opposed to _call by value_ or "
"_call by reference_). <!-- No wikipedia link, which would be a huge "
"distraction. --> Call by need is commonly known as laziness in functional "
"programming, as it is a specific implementation of the concept where "
"evaluation is deferred until the result is required, aiming to only evaluate "
"the parts of an expression that are needed to produce the final result."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:32
msgid ""
"Furthermore, the result of evaluation is preserved, in values, in `let` "
"bindings, in function _parameters_, which behave a lot like `let` bindings, "
"but with the notable exception of function _calls_. Results of function "
"calls rely on being put into `let` bindings, etc to be reused. <!-- which "
"would be prohibitively expensive and too strict, or we wouldn't have a cache "
"key for the argument -->"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:34
msgid ""
"When discussing the process of evaluation in lower level terms, we may "
"define values not as a subset of expressions, but separately, where each "
"\"value\" is either a data constructor, a function or a _thunk_. A thunk is "
"a delayed computation, represented by an expression reference and a "
"\"closure\" – the values for the lexical scope around the delayed expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:36
msgid ""
"As a user of the language, you generally don't have to think about thunks, "
"as they are not part of the language semantics, but you may encounter them "
"in the repl, in the [C API](../c-api.md) or in discussions."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:38
msgid "Strictness"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:40
msgid ""
"Instead of thinking about thunks, it is often more productive to think in "
"terms of _strictness_. This term is used in functional programming to refer "
"to the opposite of laziness, i.e. not just for something like error "
"propagation. It refers to the need to evaluate certain expressions before "
"evaluation can produce any result."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:43
msgid ""
"Statements about strictness usually implicitly refer to weak head normal "
"form. For example, we can say that the following function is strict in its "
"argument:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:50
msgid ""
"The above function must be strict in its argument `x` because determining "
"its type requires evaluating `x` to at least some degree."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:52
msgid "The following function is not strict in its argument:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:58
msgid ""
"It is not strict, because it can return the attribute set before evaluating "
"`x`. The attribute value for `isOk` _is_ strict in `x`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:61
msgid ""
"A function with a _set pattern_ is always strict in its argument, as a "
"consequence of checking the argument's type and/or attribute names:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:63
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let f = { ... }: \"ok\";\n"
"in f (throw \"kablam\")\n"
"=> error: kablam\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:69
msgid ""
"However, a set pattern does not add any strictness beyond WHNF of the "
"attribute set argument."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:71
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let f = orig@{ x, ... }: \"ok\";\n"
"in f { x = throw \"error\"; y = throw \"error\"; }\n"
"=> \"ok\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:77
msgid "Evaluation order"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:79
msgid ""
"The order in which expressions are evaluated is generally unspecified, "
"because it does not affect successful evaluation outcomes. This allows more "
"freedom for the evaluator to evolve and to evaluate efficiently."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:82
msgid ""
"Data dependencies naturally impose some ordering constraints: a value cannot "
"be used before it is computed. Beyond these constraints, the evaluator is "
"free to choose any order."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:85
msgid ""
"The order in which side effects such as [`builtins.trace`](../language/"
"builtins.md#builtins-trace) output occurs is not defined, but may be "
"expected to follow data dependencies. <!-- we may want to be more specific "
"about this. -->"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:87
msgid ""
"In a lazy language, evaluation order is often opposite to expectations from "
"strict languages. For example, in `let wrap = x: { wrapped = x; }; in wrap "
"(1 + 2)`, the function body produces a result (`{ wrapped = ...; }`) "
"_before_ evaluating `x`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:90
msgid "Infinite recursion and stack overflow"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:92
msgid ""
"During evaluation, two types of errors can occur when expressions reference "
"themselves or call functions too deeply:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:94
msgid "Infinite recursion"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:96
msgid ""
"This error occurs when a value depends on itself through a cycle, making it "
"impossible to compute."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:103
msgid ""
"Infinite recursion happens at the value level when evaluating an expression "
"requires evaluating the same expression again."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:105
msgid ""
"Despite the name, infinite recursion is cheap to compute and does not "
"involve a stack overflow. The cycle is finite and fairly easy to detect."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:108
msgid "Stack overflow"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:110
msgid ""
"This error occurs when the call depth exceeds the maximum allowed limit."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/evaluation.md:118
msgid ""
"Stack overflow happens when too many function calls are nested without "
"returning. The maximum call depth is controlled by the [`max-call-depth` "
"setting](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-max-call-depth)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:1
msgid "Identifiers"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:3
msgid ""
"An _identifier_ is an [ASCII](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASCII) character "
"sequence that:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:4
msgid "Starts with a letter (`a-z`, `A-Z`) or underscore (`_`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:5
msgid "Can contain any number of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:6
msgid "Letters (`a-z`, `A-Z`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:7
msgid "Digits (`0-9`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:8
msgid "Underscores (`_`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:9
msgid "Apostrophes (`'`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:10
msgid "Hyphens (`-`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:11
msgid "Is not one of the [keywords](#keywords)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:15
msgid "_identifier_ ~ `[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_'-]*`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:17
msgid "Names"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:19
msgid ""
"A _name_ can be written as an [identifier](#identifiers) or a [string "
"literal](./string-literals.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:23
msgid "_name_ → _identifier_ | _string_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:25
msgid ""
"Names are used in [attribute sets](./syntax.md#attrs-literal), [`let` "
"bindings](./syntax.md#let-expressions), and [`inherit`](./"
"syntax.md#inheriting-attributes). Two names are the same if they represent "
"the same sequence of characters, regardless of whether they are written as "
"identifiers or strings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:28
msgid "Keywords"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:30
msgid ""
"These keywords are reserved and cannot be used as [identifiers]"
"(#identifiers):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:32
msgid "[`assert`](./syntax.md#assertions)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:33
msgid "[`else`](./syntax.md#conditionals)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:34
msgid "[`if`](./syntax.md#conditionals)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:35
msgid "[`in`](./syntax.md#let-expressions)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:36
msgid "[`inherit`](./syntax.md#inheriting-attributes)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:37
msgid "[`let`](./syntax.md#let-expressions)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:38
msgid "[`or`](./operators.md#attribute-selection) (see note)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:39
msgid "[`rec`](./syntax.md#recursive-sets)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:40
msgid "[`then`](./syntax.md#conditionals)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:41
msgid "[`with`](./syntax.md#with-expressions)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:48
msgid ""
"The Nix language evaluator currently allows `or` to be used as a name in "
"some contexts, for backwards compatibility reasons. Users are advised not to "
"rely on this."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/identifiers.md:51
msgid ""
"There are long-standing issues with how `or` is parsed as a name, which "
"can't be resolved without making a breaking change to the language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/constructs/lookup-path.md:1
msgid "Lookup path"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/constructs/lookup-path.md:5
msgid "_lookup-path_ = `<` _identifier_ \\[ `/` _identifier_ \\]... `>`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/constructs/lookup-path.md:7
msgid ""
"A lookup path is an identifier with an optional path suffix that resolves to "
"a [path value](../../language/types.md#type-path) if the identifier matches "
"a search path entry in [`builtins.nixPath`](../../language/"
"builtins.md#builtins-nixPath). The algorithm for lookup path resolution is "
"described in the documentation on [`builtins.findFile`](../../language/"
"builtins.md#builtins-findFile)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:1
msgid "Scoping rules"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:3
msgid ""
"A _scope_ in the Nix language is a dictionary keyed by [name](./"
"identifiers.md#names), mapping each name to an expression and a _definition "
"type_. The definition type is either _explicit_ or _implicit_. Each entry in "
"this dictionary is a _definition_."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:7
msgid "Explicit definitions are created by the following expressions:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:8
msgid "[let-expressions](syntax.md#let-expressions)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:9
msgid "[recursive attribute set literals](syntax.md#recursive-sets) (`rec`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:10
msgid "[function literals](syntax.md#functions)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:12
msgid ""
"Implicit definitions are only created by [with-expressions](./syntax.md#with-"
"expressions)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:14
msgid ""
"Every expression is _enclosed_ by a scope. The outermost expression is "
"enclosed by the [built-in, global scope](./builtins.md), which contains only "
"explicit definitions. The expressions listed above _extend_ their enclosing "
"scope by adding new definitions, or replacing existing ones with the same "
"name. An explicit definition can replace a definition of any type; an "
"implicit definition can only replace another implicit definition."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:19
msgid ""
"Each of the above expressions defines which of its subexpressions are "
"enclosed by the extended scope. In all other cases, the same scope that "
"encloses an expression is the enclosing scope for its subexpressions."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:22
msgid ""
"The Nix language is [statically scoped](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Scope_(computer_science)#Lexical_scope); the value of a variable is "
"determined only by the variable's enclosing scope, and not by the dynamic "
"context in which the variable is evaluated."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/scope.md:27
msgid ""
"Expressions entered into the [Nix REPL](../command-ref/new-cli/nix3-repl.md) "
"are enclosed by a scope that can be extended by command line arguments or "
"previous REPL commands. These ways of extending scope are not, strictly "
"speaking, part of the Nix language."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:1
msgid "String interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:3
msgid ""
"String interpolation is a language feature where a [string](./types.md#type-"
"string), [path](./types.md#type-path), or [attribute name](./types.md#type-"
"attrs) can contain expressions enclosed in `${ }` (dollar-sign with curly "
"brackets)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:5
msgid ""
"Such a construct is called _interpolated string_, and the expression inside "
"is an [interpolated expression](#interpolated-expression)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:13
msgid "_interpolation_element_ → `${` _expression_ `}`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:19
#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:93
msgid "Rather than writing"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:21
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"--with-freetype2-library=\" + freetype + \"/lib\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:25
msgid ""
"(where `freetype` is a [derivation expression](../glossary.md#gloss-"
"derivation-expression)), you can instead write"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:29
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"--with-freetype2-library=${freetype}/lib\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:33
msgid "The latter is automatically translated to the former."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:35
msgid ""
"A more complicated example (from the Nix expression for [Qt](http://"
"www.trolltech.com/products/qt)):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:37
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"configureFlags = \"\n"
"  -system-zlib -system-libpng -system-libjpeg\n"
"  ${if openglSupport then \"-dlopen-opengl\n"
"    -L${mesa}/lib -I${mesa}/include\n"
"    -L${libXmu}/lib -I${libXmu}/include\" else \"\"}\n"
"  ${if threadSupport then \"-thread\" else \"-no-thread\"}\n"
"\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:47
msgid ""
"Note that Nix expressions and strings can be arbitrarily nested; in this "
"case the outer string contains various interpolated expressions that "
"themselves contain strings (e.g., `\"-thread\"`), some of which in turn "
"contain interpolated expressions (e.g., `${mesa}`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:50
msgid ""
"To write a literal `${` in an regular string, escape it with a backslash "
"(`\\`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:54
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"echo \\${PATH}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:58
msgid ""
"    \"echo ${PATH}\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:60
msgid ""
"To write a literal `${` in an indented string, escape it with two single "
"quotes (`''`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:70
msgid ""
"    \"echo ${PATH}\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:72
msgid "`$${` can be written literally in any string."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:76
msgid ""
"In Make, `$` in file names or recipes is represented as `$$`, see [GNU "
"`make`: Basics of Variable Reference](https://www.gnu.org/software/make/"
"manual/html_node/Reference.html#Basics-of-Variable-References). This can be "
"expressed directly in the Nix language strings:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:87
msgid ""
"    \"MAKEVAR = Hello\\nall:\\n\\t@export BASHVAR=world; echo $(MAKEVAR) $\\$"
"{BASHVAR}\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:89
msgid "See the [documentation on strings](./types.md#type-string) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:95
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"./. + \"/\" + foo + \"-\" + bar + \".nix\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:101
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"./. + \"/${foo}-${bar}.nix\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:105
msgid "you can instead write"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:111
msgid "Attribute name"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:118
msgid "Attribute names can be interpolated strings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:122
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let name = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ ${name} = 123; }\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:129
msgid "Attributes can be selected with interpolated strings."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:133
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let name = \"foo\"; in\n"
"{ foo = 123; }.${name}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:140
msgid "Interpolated expression"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:142
msgid ""
"An expression that is interpolated must evaluate to one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:144
msgid "a [string](./types.md#type-string)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:145
msgid "a [path](./types.md#type-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:146
msgid ""
"an [attribute set](./types.md#type-attrs) that has a `__toString` attribute "
"or an `outPath` attribute"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:148
msgid ""
"`__toString` must be a function that takes the attribute set itself and "
"returns a string"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:149
msgid "`outPath` must be a string"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:151
msgid ""
"This includes [derivation expressions](./derivations.md) or [flake inputs]"
"(../command-ref/new-cli/nix3-flake.md#flake-inputs) (experimental)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:153
msgid "A string interpolates to itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:155
msgid ""
"A path in an interpolated expression is first copied into the Nix store, and "
"the resulting string is the [store path](../store/store-path.md) of the "
"newly created [store object](../store/store-object.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:165
msgid "Reference the empty directory in an interpolated expression:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:167
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"${./foo}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:171
msgid ""
"    \"/nix/store/2hhl2nz5v0khbn06ys82nrk99aa1xxdw-foo\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:173
msgid ""
"A derivation interpolates to the [store path](../store/store-path.md) of its "
"first [output](./derivations.md#attr-outputs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:177
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  pkgs = import <nixpkgs> {};\n"
"in\n"
"\"${pkgs.hello}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:184
msgid ""
"    \"/nix/store/qnlr7906z0mrl2syrkdbpicffq02nw07-hello-2.12.1\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:186
msgid ""
"An attribute set interpolates to the return value of the function in the "
"`__toString` applied to the attribute set itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:190
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = {\n"
"    value = 1;\n"
"    __toString = self: toString (self.value + 1);\n"
"  };\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:200
msgid ""
"    \"2\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:202
msgid ""
"An attribute set also interpolates to the value of its `outPath` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:206
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = { outPath = \"foo\"; };\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:213
msgid ""
"    \"foo\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:215
msgid ""
"If both `__toString` and `outPath` are present in an attribute set, "
"`__toString` takes precedence."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:219
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = { __toString = _: \"yes\"; outPath = throw \"no\"; };\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:226
msgid ""
"    \"yes\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:228
msgid "If neither is present, an error is thrown."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:232
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"let\n"
"  a = {};\n"
"in\n"
"\"${a}\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-interpolation.md:239
msgid ""
"    error: cannot coerce a set to a string: { }\n"
"    \n"
"           at «string»:4:2:\n"
"    \n"
"                3| in\n"
"                4| \"${a}\"\n"
"                 |  ^\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:1
msgid "String literals"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:3
msgid "A _string literal_ represents a [string](types.md#type-string) value."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:7
msgid "_expression_ → _string_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:9
msgid ""
"_string_ → `\"` ( _string_char_\\* [_interpolation_element_](./string-"
"interpolation.md) )\\* _string_char_\\* `\"`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:11
msgid ""
"_string_ → `''` ( _indented_string_char_\\* [_interpolation_element_](./"
"string-interpolation.md) )\\* _indented_string_char_\\* `''`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:13
msgid "_string_ → _uri_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:15
msgid "_string_char_ ~ `[^\"$\\\\]|\\$(?!\\{)|\\\\.`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:17
msgid ""
"_indented_string_char_ ~ `[^$']|\\$\\$|\\$(?!\\{)|''[$']|''\\\\.|'(?!')`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:19
msgid "_uri_ ~ `[A-Za-z][+\\-.0-9A-Za-z]*:[!$%&'*+,\\-./0-9:=?@A-Z_a-z~]+`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:21
msgid "Strings can be written in three ways."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:23
msgid ""
"The most common way is to enclose the string between double quotes, e.g., "
"`\"foo bar\"`. Strings can span multiple lines. The results of other "
"expressions can be included into a string by enclosing them in `${ }`, a "
"feature known as [string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:29
msgid ""
"The following must be escaped to represent them within a string, by "
"prefixing with a backslash (`\\`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:31
msgid "Double quote (`\"`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:35
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"\\\"\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:39
msgid ""
"    \"\\\"\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:41
msgid "Backslash (`\\`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:45
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"\\\\\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:49
msgid ""
"    \"\\\\\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:51
msgid ""
"Dollar sign followed by an opening curly bracket (`${`) – \"dollar-curly\""
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:55
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"\\${\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:59
msgid ""
"    \"\\${\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:61
msgid ""
"The newline, carriage return, and tab characters can be written as `\\n`, "
"`\\r` and `\\t`, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:63 source/language/string-literals.md:168
msgid "A \"double-dollar-curly\" (`$${`) can be written literally."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:67
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"$${\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:71
msgid ""
"    \"$\\${\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:73
msgid ""
"String values are output on the terminal with Nix-specific escaping. Strings "
"written to files will contain the characters encoded by the escaping."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:76
msgid ""
"The second way to write string literals is as an _indented string_, which is "
"enclosed between pairs of _double single-quotes_ (`''`), like so:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:86
msgid ""
"This kind of string literal intelligently strips indentation from the start "
"of each line. To be precise, it strips from each line a number of spaces "
"equal to the minimal indentation of the string as a whole (disregarding the "
"indentation of empty lines). For instance, the first and second line are "
"indented two spaces, while the third line is indented four spaces. Thus, two "
"spaces are stripped from each line, so the resulting string is"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:94
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"\"This is the first line.\\nThis is the second line.\\n  This is the third "
"line.\\n\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:100
msgid ""
"Whitespace and newline following the opening `''` is ignored if there is no "
"non-whitespace text on the initial line."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:104
msgid "Prefixed tab characters are not stripped."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:108
msgid "The following indented string is prefixed with tabs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:116
msgid ""
"    \"\\tall:\\n\\t\\t@echo hello\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:118
msgid ""
"Indented strings support [string interpolation](./string-interpolation.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:120
msgid "The following must be escaped to represent them in an indented string:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:122
msgid "`$` is escaped by prefixing it with two single quotes (`''`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:132
msgid ""
"    \"$\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:134
msgid "`''` is escaped by prefixing it with one single quote (`'`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:144
msgid ""
"    \"''\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:146
msgid "These special characters are escaped as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:147
msgid "Linefeed (`\\n`): `''\\n`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:148
msgid "Carriage return (`\\r`): `''\\r`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:149
msgid "Tab (`\\t`): `''\\t`"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:151
msgid "`''\\` escapes any other character."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:153
msgid "A \"dollar-curly\" (`${`) can be written as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:166
msgid ""
"This differs from the syntax for escaping a dollar-curly within double "
"quotes (`\"\\${\"`). Be aware of which one is needed at a given moment."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:178
msgid ""
"    \"$\\${\\n\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:180
msgid ""
"Indented strings are primarily useful in that they allow multi-line string "
"literals to follow the indentation of the enclosing Nix expression, and that "
"less escaping is typically necessary for strings representing languages such "
"as shell scripts and configuration files because `''` is much less common "
"than `\"`. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:187
msgid ""
"```nix\n"
"stdenv.mkDerivation {\n"
"...\n"
"postInstall =\n"
"  ''\n"
"    mkdir $out/bin $out/etc\n"
"    cp foo $out/bin\n"
"    echo \"Hello World\" > $out/etc/foo.conf\n"
"    ${if enableBar then \"cp bar $out/bin\" else \"\"}\n"
"  '';\n"
"...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/string-literals.md:201
msgid ""
"Finally, as a convenience, _URIs_ as defined in appendix B of [RFC 2396]"
"(http://www.ietf.org/rfc/rfc2396.txt) can be written _as is_, without "
"quotes. For instance, the string `\"http://example.org/foo.tar.bz2\"` can "
"also be written as `http://example.org/foo.tar.bz2`."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/variables.md:1
msgid "Variables"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/variables.md:3
msgid "A _variable_ is an [identifier](identifiers.md) used as an expression."
msgstr ""

#: source/language/variables.md:7
msgid "_expression_ → _identifier_"
msgstr ""

#: source/language/variables.md:9
msgid ""
"A variable must have the same name as a definition in the [scope](./"
"scope.md) that encloses it. The value of a variable is the value of the "
"corresponding expression in the enclosing scope."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:1
msgid "Nix Store"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:3
msgid ""
"The _Nix store_ is an abstraction to store immutable file system data (such "
"as software packages) that can have dependencies on other such data."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:5
msgid ""
"Concretely, albeit using concepts that are only defined in the rest of the "
"chapter, a store consists of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:7
msgid ""
"A set of [store objects](./store-object.md), the immutable file system data."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:9
msgid ""
"This can also be looked at as a map from [store paths](./store-path.md) to "
"store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:11
msgid ""
"A set of [derivations](./derivation/index.md), instructions for building "
"store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:13
msgid ""
"This can also be looked at as a map from [store paths](./store-path.md) to "
"derivations. Since store paths to derivations always end in `.drv`, and "
"store paths to other store objects never do, the two maps can also be "
"combined into one. Derivations can also be encoded as store objects too."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:17
msgid ""
"A [build trace](./build-trace.md), a record of which derivations have been "
"built and what they produced."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:21
msgid ""
"The concept of a build trace is currently [**experimental**](../development/"
"experimental-features.md#xp-feature-ca-derivations) and subject to change."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/index.md:25
msgid ""
"There are [multiple types of Nix stores](./types/index.md) with different "
"capabilities, such as the default one on the [local file system](./types/"
"local-store.md) (`/nix/store`) or [binary caches](./types/http-binary-cache-"
"store.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/math-notation.md:1
msgid "Appendix: Math notation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/math-notation.md:3
msgid ""
"A few times in this manual, formal \"proof trees\" are used for [natural "
"deduction](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_deduction)\\-style "
"definition of various [relations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Relation_(mathematics))."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/math-notation.md:5
msgid ""
"The following grammar and assignment of metavariables to syntactic "
"categories is used in these sections."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/math-notation.md:7
msgid ""
"\\\\begin{align} s, t &\\in \\text{store-path} \\\\\\\\ o &\\in "
"\\text{output-name} \\\\\\\\ i, p &\\in \\text{deriving-path} \\\\\\\\ d "
"&\\in \\text{derivation} \\\\end{align}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/math-notation.md:14
msgid ""
"\\\\begin{align} \\\\text{deriving-path} \\quad p &::= s \\mid (p, o) \\"
"\\end{align}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:5
msgid ""
"This entire concept is currently [**experimental**](../development/"
"experimental-features.md#xp-feature-ca-derivations) and subject to change."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:9
msgid ""
"The _build trace_ is a [memoization table](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Memoization) for builds. It maps the inputs of builds to the outputs of "
"builds. Each _\\[entry\\]{#entry}_ in the build trace maps a [derivation](./"
"derivation/index.md) to a map of [output](./derivation/outputs/index.md) "
"names to [store objects](../store/store-object.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:13
msgid ""
"In general the derivations used as a key should be [_resolved_](./"
"resolution.md). A build trace with all-resolved-derivation keys is also "
"called a _base build trace_ for extra clarity. If all the resolved inputs of "
"a derivation are content-addressed, that means the inputs will be fully "
"determined, leaving no ambiguity for what build was performed. (Input-"
"addressed inputs however are still ambiguous. They too should be locked "
"down, but this is left as future work.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:18
msgid ""
"Accordingly, to look up an unresolved derivation, one must first resolve it "
"to get a resolved derivation. Resolving itself involves looking up entries "
"in the build trace, so this is a mutually recursive process that will end up "
"inspecting possibly many entries."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:21
msgid ""
"Except for the issue with input-addressed paths called out above, base build "
"traces are trivially _coherent_ -- incoherence is not possible. That means "
"that the claims that each key-value base build try entry makes are "
"independent, and no mapping invalidates another mapping."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:24
msgid ""
"Whether the mappings are _true_, i.e. the faithful recording of actual "
"builds performed, is another matter. Coherence is about the multiple claims "
"of the build trace being mutually consistent, not about whether the claims "
"are individually true or false."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:27
msgid ""
"In general, there is no way to audit a build trace entry except for by "
"performing the build again from scratch. And even in that case, a different "
"result doesn't mean the original entry was a \"lie\", because the derivation "
"being built may be non-deterministic. As such, the decision of whether to "
"trust a counterparty's build trace is a fundamentally subject policy choice. "
"Build trace entries are typically _signed_ in order to enable arbitrary "
"public-key-based trust polices."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:32
msgid "Derived build traces"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:34
msgid ""
"Implementations that wish to memoize the above may also keep additional "
"_derived_ build trace entries that do map unresolved derivations. But if "
"they do so, they _must_ also keep the underlying base entries with resolved "
"derivation keys around. Firstly, this ensures that the derived entries are "
"merely cache, which could be recomputed from scratch. Secondly, this ensures "
"the coherence of the derived build trace."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:39
msgid ""
"Unlike with base build traces, incoherence with derived build traces is "
"possible. The key ingredient is that derivation resolution is only "
"deterministic with respect to a fixed base build trace. Without fixing the "
"base build trace, it inherits the subjectivity of base build traces "
"themselves."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:43
msgid ""
"Concretely, suppose there are three derivations \\\\(a\\\\), \\\\(b\\\\), "
"and \\\\(c\\\\). Let \\\\(a\\\\) be a resolved derivation, but let \\\\(b\\"
"\\) and \\\\(c\\\\) be unresolved and both take as an input an output of \\\\"
"(a\\\\). Now suppose that derived entries are made for \\\\(b\\\\) and \\\\"
"(c\\\\) based on two different entries of \\\\(a\\\\). (This could happen if "
"\\\\(a\\\\) is non-deterministic, \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\) are built in "
"one store, \\\\(a\\\\) and \\\\(c\\\\) are built in another store, and then "
"a third store substitutes from both of the first two stores.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/build-trace.md:48
msgid ""
"If trusting the derived build trace entries for \\\\(b\\\\) and \\\\(c\\\\) "
"requires that each's underlying entry for \\\\(a\\\\) be also trusted, the "
"two different mappings for \\\\(a\\\\) will be caught. However, if \\\\(b\\"
"\\) and \\\\(c\\\\)'s entries can be combined in isolation, there will be "
"nothing to catch the contradiction in their hidden assumptions about \\\\(a\\"
"\\)'s output."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:1
msgid "Building"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:3
msgid "As discussed in the [main page on derivations](./derivation/index.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:5
msgid ""
"A derivation is a specification for running an executable on precisely "
"defined input to produce one or more [store objects](./store-object.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:7
msgid ""
"This page describes _building_ a derivation, which is to say following the "
"instructions in the derivation to actually run the executable. Some elements "
"of derivations are self-explanatory. For example, the arguments specified in "
"the derivation really are the arguments passed to the executable. In other "
"cases, however, there is additional common steps performed by Nix for all "
"derivations --- mostly for setting up the build environment and collecting "
"the built outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:12
msgid ""
"The chief design consideration for the building process is _determinism_. "
"Conventional operating systems are typically not designed with determinism "
"in mind. But determinism is needed to make Nix's build caching a transparent "
"abstraction."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:16 source/store/building.md:46
msgid "**Explanation**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:18
msgid ""
"For example, no one wants to slightly modify a derivation, and then find "
"that it no longer builds for an unrelated reason, because the original "
"derivation _also_ doesn't build anymore, but the cache hit on the original "
"derivation was hiding this. We want builds that succeed once to continue "
"succeeding, to encourage fearless modification of old build recipes. "
"Determinism is what enables things that once worked to keep working."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:22
msgid "The life cycle of a build can be broken down into 3 parts:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:24
msgid ""
"Spawn the builder process with the proper environment, including the correct "
"process arguments, environment variables, and file system state."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:26
msgid ""
"Wait for the builder process to exit and collect its exit status. Exit code "
"0 means success; anything else is a build failure. (Strictly speaking, Nix "
"detects process exit by waiting for the standard output and error streams to "
"close. If a builder explicitly closes these streams without exiting, Nix "
"will kill it, and deem the build a failure. Processes should therefore exit "
"_without_ explicitly closing those standard streams, and let the exiting of "
"the process close them implicitly.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:32
msgid ""
"Nix also logs the standard output and error of the process, but this is just "
"for human convenience and does not influence the behavior of the system. "
"(Builder processes have no idea what the consumer of their standard output "
"and error does with the pseudo-terminal master, only that they are indeed "
"consumed so buffers do not fill up etc. and writes to each output standard "
"stream will continue to succeed. In practice, Nix will store the log in `/"
"nix/var/log/nix`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:36
msgid "Processing the outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:38
msgid ""
"Traditionally, this happens only after the builder has exited: the builder "
"process should have left behind files for each output the derivation is "
"supposed to produce, and those files are processed to turn them into bona "
"fide store objects. Alternatively, the builder may send messages to Nix "
"while it's running, creating filesystem objects and linking them to an "
"output name. This allows outputs to be processed concurrently during the "
"build, allows outputs to depend on other newly created store objects, and "
"also resolves some tricky issues with content-addressing and output-to-"
"output references. If the processing succeeds, the resulting store objects "
"are associated with the derivation as (the results of) a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:43
msgid ""
"Step (3) is done by Nix, either externally to the build (in the traditional "
"case, operating on the inert data left behind after the builder has exited "
"or been killed) or concurrently with it (in the IPC case). Step (1) however "
"is best described not from Nix's perspective, but from the build process's "
"perspective."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:48
msgid ""
"Ultimately, what matters for determinism is what the build process can "
"observe: what resources (files, networking, etc.) it can see, what syscalls "
"succeed or fail, etc. Nix can achieve this through many different sandboxing "
"strategies (namespaces, VMs, chroots, ...), but the process shouldn't be "
"able to tell them apart. We therefore specify building from the process's "
"perspective, not Nix's perspective, to focus on _what_, not _how_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:52
msgid "What derivations can be built"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:54
msgid ""
"Actually only some derivations are ready to be built. In particular, only "
"[_resolved_](./resolution.md) derivations can be built. That is to say, a "
"derivation that depends on other derivations is not ready yet to be built, "
"because some of those other derivations might not have yet been built. If "
"the other derivations are indeed all built, we can witness this fact by "
"resolving the derivation, and converting all the derivation's input "
"references into plain store paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:61
msgid ""
"Note that [input-addressing](derivation/outputs/input-address.md) "
"derivations are improperly resolved. As discussed on the linked page, the "
"current input-addressing algorithm does not respect resolution-equivalence "
"of derivations (\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\)). That means that if Nix "
"properly resolved an input-addressed derivation, the resolved derivation "
"would have different input addresses, violating expectations. Nix therefore "
"improperly resolves the derivation, keeping its original input-addressed "
"output paths, creating an invalid derivation that is both resolved and "
"instructed to create the outputs at the originally expected paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:66
msgid "Environment of the builder process"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:68
msgid ""
"This section describes how the [`builder`](./derivation/index.md#builder) is "
"executed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:70 source/store/building.md:221
#: source/store/building.md:231
msgid "**Implementation detail**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:72
msgid ""
"Nix prevents multiple [Nix instances](../glossary.md#gloss-nix-instance) "
"from performing the same build at the same time, for example by acquiring "
"exclusive file locks."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:74
msgid "File system"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:76
msgid ""
"The builder should have access to a limited file system where only certain "
"objects are available. The most important exposed files are the inputs "
"(other store objects) of the (resolved) derivation. Additionally, some other "
"files are exposed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:80
msgid "Store inputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:82
msgid ""
"The builder will be run against a file system in which the [store directory]"
"(./store-path.md#store-directory-path) contains the [closure](../"
"glossary.md#gloss-closure) of the inputs. In particular, consider a store "
"that just contains this closure. That store is exposed to the file system "
"according to the rules specified in the [Exposing Store Objects in OS File "
"Systems](./store-path.md#exposing) documentation. This precisely defines the "
"file system layout of the store that should be visible to the builder "
"process."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:89
msgid ""
"Historically, Nix exposed _at least_ the following store contents to the "
"builder, but also arbitrarily other store objects, due to limitations around "
"operating systems' file system virtualization capabilities, and wanting to "
"avoid copying or moving files. It still can do this in so-called "
"_unsandboxed_ builds."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:92
msgid ""
"Such builds should be considered discouraged, but one that works less badly "
"against non-mischievous derivations than might be expected. This is because "
"store paths are relatively unpredictable, so a well-behaved program is "
"unlikely to stumble upon a store object it wasn't supposed to know about."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:95
msgid ""
"As operating systems developed better file system primitives, the need for "
"disabling sandboxing has lessened greatly over the years, and this trend "
"should continue into the future."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:97
msgid ""
"The outputs are expected to be created in that store directory as if they "
"were valid store objects. (They are just files during builder execution, but "
"during [processing outputs](#processing-outputs) they will be turned into "
"proper store objects.) The [environment variables](#env-vars) for each "
"output indicate where the builder should write them; Nix ensures that those "
"paths do not yet exist when the builder is run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:104
msgid ""
"In sandboxed builds, ensuring that the outputs do not exist in the store "
"directory is trivial. In unsandboxed builds, it is harder in general. In the "
"worst case, the derivation is in fact rewritten so different output paths "
"are used instead, and then the outputs are rewritten back to the intended "
"output paths after. In the content-addressing case rewriting would be needed "
"either way, but in the input-addressing case, this is a significant "
"degradation, as the point of input addressing is to avoid rewrites by "
"knowing output paths in advance."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:113
msgid "Other file system state"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:115
msgid ""
"The current working directory of the builder process will be a fresh "
"temporary directory. It is initially empty when the process starts except "
"for a few input files:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:118
msgid ""
"If [`__structuredAttrs`](../language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-"
"structuredAttrs) is enabled: `.attrs.json` (the derivation attributes as "
"JSON) and `.attrs.sh` (a Bash-compatible rendering of the same). The "
"environment variables `NIX_ATTRS_JSON_FILE` and `NIX_ATTRS_SH_FILE` point to "
"these files, respectively."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:121
msgid ""
"If [`passAsFile`](../language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-passAsFile) is "
"used (only without `__structuredAttrs`): for each attribute name listed, a "
"file `.attr-<hash>` where `<hash>` is the [Nix32](../protocols/nix32.md)\\-"
"encoded SHA-256 hash of the attribute name. The environment variable "
"`<name>Path` points to the file containing the attribute's value."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:124
msgid ""
"In sandboxed builds, this directory is at a deterministic path inside the "
"sandbox (controlled by the [`sandbox-build-dir`](../command-ref/conf-"
"file.md#conf-sandbox-build-dir) setting, default `/build`). See also the per-"
"store [`build-dir`](../store/types/local-store.md#store-local-store-build-"
"dir) setting for the host-side location."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:127
msgid ""
"Basic device nodes for essential operations (null device, random number "
"generation, standard streams as a pseudo terminal)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:129
msgid ""
"(A pseudo terminal would not be strictly necessary since the standard "
"streams are passively logging, not there to facilitate interaction. But it "
"is still useful to entice programs to do nicer logging with e.g. colors etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:132
msgid "On Linux: Process information via `/proc`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:134
msgid "Minimal user and group identity information"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:136
msgid "A loopback-only network configuration with hostname set to `localhost`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:140
msgid ""
"Fixed-output derivations have access to additional operating system state to "
"facilitate communication with the outside world, such as network name "
"resolution and TLS certificate verification. This is necessary because these "
"derivations are allowed to access the network, unlike regular derivations "
"which are fully sandboxed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:145
msgid ""
"The environment is cleared and set to the derivation attributes, as "
"specified above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:148
msgid "For most derivations types this must contain at least:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:150
msgid ""
"For each output declared in `outputs`, the corresponding environment "
"variable is set to point to the intended path in the Nix store for that "
"output. Each output path is a concatenation of the cryptographic hash of all "
"build inputs, the `name` attribute and the output name. (The output name is "
"omitted if it's `out`.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:154
msgid "In addition, the following variables are set:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:156
msgid ""
"`NIX_BUILD_TOP` contains the path of the temporary directory for this build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:158
msgid ""
"Also, `TMPDIR`, `TEMPDIR`, `TMP`, `TEMP` are set to point to the temporary "
"directory. This is to prevent the builder from accidentally writing "
"temporary files anywhere else. Doing so might cause interference by other "
"processes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:162
msgid ""
"`PATH` is set to `/path-not-set` to prevent shells from initialising it to "
"their built-in default value."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:164
msgid ""
"`HOME` is set to `/homeless-shelter`. (Without sandboxing, this discourages "
"programs from using `/etc/passwd` or the like to find the user's home "
"directory, which could cause impurity.) Usually, when `HOME` is set, it is "
"used as the location of the home directory, even if it points to a non-"
"existent path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:168
msgid ""
"`NIX_STORE` is set to the path of the top-level Nix [store directory path](./"
"store-path.md#store-directory-path) (typically, `/nix/store`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:170
msgid ""
"`NIX_ATTRS_JSON_FILE` & `NIX_ATTRS_SH_FILE` if `__structuredAttrs` is set to "
"`true` for the derivation. A detailed explanation of this behavior can be "
"found in the [section about structured attrs](../language/advanced-"
"attributes.md#adv-attr-structuredAttrs)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:173
msgid "Arguments"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:175
msgid ""
"The builder is passed the arguments specified by the derivation attribute "
"`args`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:177
msgid "Processing outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:179
msgid ""
"There are two methods for processing outputs. But first, let us cover the "
"requirements common to both methods."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:182
msgid ""
"Regardless of which method is used, each output must be turned into a valid "
"store object. This involves two steps:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:185
msgid "**Normalize the file permissions**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:187
msgid ""
"The files must conform to the model described in the [Exposing in OS file "
"systems](./file-system-object/os-file-system.md) section. For example, "
"timestamps and permissions are canonicalised."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:190
msgid "\\[**Calculate the references**\\]{#scanning-for-references}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:192
msgid ""
"Nix scans each output path for [references](./store-object.md#references) to "
"input store objects by looking for the [digest](./store-path.md#digest) of "
"each input. (The name part and the [store directory path](./store-"
"path.md#store-directory-path) are ignored when scanning; an input's hash "
"part that is neither followed by a `-` nor proceeded by a `/` still scans as "
"a reference.) Since these are potential runtime dependencies, Nix will "
"register them as references of the output store object they occur in."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:196
msgid ""
"This scanning can be disabled per output with the [_unsafe discard "
"references_](derivation/outputs/index.md#unsafe-discard-references) setting."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:198
msgid "Traditional (post-build) processing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:200
msgid ""
"With the traditional method, the builder process on exit should have left "
"behind files for each output the derivation is supposed to produce. The "
"files must be processed to turn them into bona fide store objects. If the "
"processing succeeds, those store objects are associated with the derivation "
"as (the results of) a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:204
msgid ""
"Nix also scans for references from one output to another in the same way, "
"because outputs are allowed to refer to each other. The outputs' references "
"must form a [directed acyclic graph](../glossary.md#gloss-directed-acyclic-"
"graph). (This is not a special restriction for outputs; it is true for the "
"references of all store objects in general.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:208
msgid ""
"In the case of derivations with output paths that are fixed in advance (i.e. "
"[input-addressing](./derivation/outputs/input-address.md) derivations, or "
"[fixed content-addressing](./derivation/outputs/content-address.md#fixed) "
"derivations), the actual final store path to each output is used during the "
"build if possible. For [floating content-addressing](./derivation/outputs/"
"content-address.md#floating) derivations, however, the final store path is "
"not known in advance by definition. Scratch store paths must therefore be "
"used instead. Scanning will use those scratch paths, but then any output-to-"
"be that contains such a scanned scratch path must be rewritten to instead "
"use the final (content-addressed) path of the output in question."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:213
msgid ""
"In addition to output-to-output references, rewriting is also needed to "
"support self-references in the content-addressing case. An output may "
"contain its own store path digest, which is a self-reference. Hash functions "
"which are secure cannot allow the easy calculation of the quasi-fixed points "
"needed to support self-references \"natively\", so instead we replace all "
"would-be self-references with a sentinel value, and then rewrite the "
"sentinel value to be the final store path digest. Superficially, this post-"
"hashing rewriting breaks the content address, but as the self-references are "
"easily identified, the rewriting can be inverted to yield the original "
"hashed data, allowing verifying the content address after all."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:218
msgid ""
"At this point, the file system data is in the proper form, and the valid "
"acyclic reference data for each output is also calculated, so the outputs "
"are added to the store as proper store objects. Additionally, those store "
"objects (at least in the case that they are [content-addressed](./derivation/"
"outputs/content-address.md)) can be associated with the derivation in the "
"[build trace](./build-trace.md) in the record for a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:223
msgid ""
"Nix will normally clean up and remove the temporary build directory after "
"every build, successful or unsuccessful. The builder doesn't know whether "
"Nix does or not, however, as it will have exited before the build directory "
"is cleaned up, and it will not see any old build directory if (after a "
"failed build) it is run again. The [`--keep-failed`](../command-ref/opt-"
"common.md#opt-keep-failed) option can be specified to keep the build "
"directory in the case of a failing build."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:227
msgid "Concurrent processing via IPC"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:229
msgid ""
"With this method, the builder communicates with Nix during the build using "
"inter-process communication (IPC)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:233
msgid ""
"The current implementation, `builder-rpc-v0`, exposes its interface over a "
"limited form of the Nix daemon socket. Builders may use it either with their "
"own implementation of the Nix protocol, or with the `nix store add` and `nix "
"store submit-output` commands."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:236
msgid ""
"Derivations with `builder-rpc-v0` in their set of [`requiredSystemFeatures`]"
"(../language/advanced-attributes.md#adv-attr-requiredSystemFeatures) will "
"not receive output paths in their environment, and are expected to submit "
"all outputs with the aforementioned commands or protocol."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:239
msgid ""
"Instead of leaving files behind for Nix to process after exit, the builder "
"explicitly requests the daemon create store objects one at a time, then "
"sends commands assigning output names to the just-created store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:241
msgid ""
"Scanning for references proceeds as usual for each store object creation "
"request, but the set of potential references to be scanned is greater: it "
"includes both all inputs (as before) and also all previously-added store "
"objects. This means, if output `bar` is supposed to reference output `foo`, "
"`foo` should be created first, and `bar` second."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:244
msgid ""
"All store objects being created are content-addressed (there is no support "
"for input-addressed outputs with the IPC approach). When a store object is "
"created, its content address store path will be calculated by Nix and then "
"returned in the IPC response message. The builder then knows what store path "
"to use in subsequent store objects in order for reference scanning to pick "
"them up."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:248
msgid "This overall approach has several advantages:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:250
msgid "**No Nix-side rewriting**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:252
msgid ""
"For content-addressed outputs, the builder is responsible for adding outputs "
"in reference order, using the store paths from earlier adds in later ones. "
"This avoids the fragile rewriting that would otherwise be needed to fix up "
"output-to-output references described above. The builder, unlike Nix itself, "
"is free to leverage domain-specific knowledge to do a better job. For "
"example it can"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:256
msgid ""
"uncompress, rewrite, and then recompress man pages, to not miss references "
"hidden by compression."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:258
msgid ""
"make sure to rewrite data that is to be signed, like Apple binaries, before "
"signing that data, so as not to invalidate any signatures by mistake."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:260
msgid "**Pipelining**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:262
msgid ""
"Downstream builds that only need some outputs (e.g., a \"dev\" or "
"\"headers\" output) can start without waiting for all outputs to be ready. "
"Nix doesn't yet implement this, but it could and should."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/building.md:265
msgid ""
"The major _disadvantage_ of this approach is that it doesn't yet support "
"self-references. Unlike acyclic output-to-output references, self-references "
"fundamentally do require rewriting. The output-to-output case was only a "
"challenge in the traditional case because all the outputs were submitted "
"simultaneously, whereas the self-reference case is fundamentally challenging "
"because of what it means for a hash function to be secure, as described "
"above. Neither batched (traditional) nor serial (IPC) submission of outputs "
"can avoid this fundamental property of secure hash functions. We could add "
"support for such rewriting just for self-references, as is done for the "
"traditional post-build processing, but we haven't yet done so as the very "
"point of the IPC approach is to free Nix from any obligation to rewrite "
"black-box data in unsound ways."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:1
msgid "Derivation Resolution"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:3
msgid ""
"_See [this chapter's appendix](../store/math-notation.md) on grammar and "
"metavariable conventions._"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:5
msgid ""
"To _resolve_ a derivation is to replace its [inputs](../store/derivation/"
"index.md#inputs) with the simplest inputs — plain store paths — that denote "
"the same store objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:7
msgid ""
"Derivations that only have store paths as inputs are likewise called "
"_resolved derivations_. (They are called that whether they are in fact the "
"output of derivation resolution, or just made that way without non-store-"
"path inputs to begin with.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:10
msgid "Input Content Equivalence of Derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:12
msgid ""
"[Deriving paths](../store/derivation/index.md#deriving-path) intentionally "
"make it possible to refer to the same [store object](../store/store-"
"object.md) in multiple ways. This is a consequence of content-addressing, "
"since different derivations can produce the same outputs, and the same data "
"can also be manually added to the store. This is also a consequence even of "
"input-addressing, as an output can be referred to by derivation and output "
"name, or directly by its [computed](./derivation/outputs/input-address.md) "
"store path. Since dereferencing deriving paths is thus not injective, it "
"induces an equivalence relation on deriving paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:17
msgid ""
"Let's call this equivalence relation \\\\(\\\\sim\\\\), where \\\\(p_1 \\"
"\\sim p_2\\\\) means that deriving paths \\\\(p_1\\\\) and \\\\(p_2\\\\) "
"refer to the same store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:19
msgid ""
"**Content Equivalence**: Two deriving paths are equivalent if they refer to "
"the same store object:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:21
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{${}^\\*p_1 = {}^\\*p_2$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$p_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{DP}\\\\, p_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} "
"\\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:28
msgid ""
"where \\\\({}^\\*p\\\\) denotes the store object that deriving path \\\\(p\\"
"\\) refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:30
msgid "This also induces an equivalence relation on sets of deriving paths:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:32
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\{ {}^\\*p | p \\\\in P_1 \\\\} "
"= \\\\{ {}^\\*p | p \\\\in P_2 \\\\}$} \\\\UnaryInfC{$P_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{DP})}\\\\, P_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:39
msgid ""
"**Input Content Equivalence**: This, in turn, induces an equivalence "
"relation on derivations: two derivations are equivalent if their inputs are "
"equivalent, and they are otherwise equal:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:41
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\mathrm{inputs}(d_1) \\\\,\\"
"\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{DP})}\\\\, \\\\mathrm{inputs}(d_2)$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$ d\\_1\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputs} := \\\\{\\\\}\\right\\] = "
"d\\_2\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputs} := \\\\{\\\\}\\right\\] $} \\"
"\\BinaryInfC{$d_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\, d_2$} \\"
"\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:53
msgid ""
"Derivation resolution always maps derivations to input-content-equivalent "
"derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:55
msgid "Resolution relation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:57
msgid ""
"Dereferencing a derived path — \\\\({}^\\*p\\\\) above — was just introduced "
"as a black box. But actually it is a multi-step process of looking up build "
"results in the [build trace](../store/build-trace.md) that itself depends on "
"resolving the lookup keys. Resolution is thus a recursive multi-step process "
"that is worth diagramming formally."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:61
msgid ""
"We can do this with a small-step binary transition relation; let's call it \\"
"\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\). We can then conclude dereferenced equality like "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:64
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$p\\_1 \\\\rightsquigarrow^\\* p$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$p\\_2 \\\\rightsquigarrow^\\* p$} \\\\BinaryInfC{${}^\\*p\\_1 = {}"
"^\\*p\\_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:72
msgid ""
"I.e. by showing that both original items resolve (over 0 or more small "
"steps, hence the \\\\({}^\\*\\\\)) to the same exact item."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:74
msgid ""
"With this motivation, let's now formalize a [small-step](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operational_semantics#Small-step_semantics) system of "
"reduction rules for resolution."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:76
msgid "Formal rules"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:78
msgid "\\\\(\\text{resolved}\\\\) unary relation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:80
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$s \\in \\text{store-path}$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$s$ resolved} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:87
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\forall i \\in \\mathrm{inputs}"
"(d). i \\text{ resolved}$} \\\\UnaryInfC{$d$ resolved} \\\\end{prooftree} \\"
"\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:94
msgid "\\\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\) binary relation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:96 source/store/resolution.md:148
msgid "**Remark**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:98
msgid ""
"Actually, to be completely formal we would need to keep track of the build "
"trace we are choosing to resolve against."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:100
msgid ""
"We could do that by making \\\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\) a ternary relation, "
"which would pass the build trace to itself until it finally uses it in that "
"one rule. This would add clutter more than insight, so we didn't bother to "
"write it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:103
msgid ""
"There are other options too, like saying the whole reduction rule system is "
"parameterized on the build trace, essentially [currying](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Currying) the ternary \\\\(\\rightsquigarrow\\\\) into "
"a function from build traces to the binary relation written above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:105
msgid "Core build trace lookup rule"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:107
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$s \\in \\text{store-path}$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{${}^\\*s \\in \\text{derivation}$} \\\\AxiomC{${}^\\*s$ resolved} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$\\mathrm{build\\text{-}trace}\\[s\\]\\[o\\] = t$} \\"
"\\QuaternaryInfC{$(s, o) \\rightsquigarrow t$} \\\\RightLabel{\\\\scriptsize "
"output path resolution} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:118
msgid "Inductive rules"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:120
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$i \\\\rightsquigarrow i'$} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$i \\\\in \\\\mathrm{inputs}(d)$} \\\\BinaryInfC{$d \\"
"\\rightsquigarrow d\\[i \\\\mapsto i'\\]$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:128
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$d \\\\rightsquigarrow d'$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$(\\\\mathrm{path}(d), o) \\\\rightsquigarrow (\\\\mathrm{path}"
"(d'), o)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:135
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$p \\\\rightsquigarrow p'$} \\"
"\\UnaryInfC{$(p, o) \\\\rightsquigarrow (p', o)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:142
msgid "Properties"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:144
msgid ""
"Like all well-behaved evaluation relations, partial resolution is "
"[_confluent_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Confluence_(abstract_rewriting)). Also, if we take the symmetric closure of "
"\\\\(\\\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\), we end up with the equivalence relations "
"of the previous section. Resolution respects content equivalence for "
"deriving paths, and input content equivalence for derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:150
msgid ""
"We chose to define from scratch an \"resolved\" unary relation explicitly "
"above. But it can also be defined as the normal forms of the \\\\(\\"
"\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\) relation:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:153
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ a \\text{ resolved} \\Leftrightarrow \\forall b. b "
"\\rightsquigarrow^\\* a \\Rightarrow b = a\\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:155
msgid ""
"In prose, resolved terms are terms which \\\\(\\\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\) "
"only relates on the left side to the same term on the right side; they are "
"the terms which can be resolved no further."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:157
msgid "Partial versus Complete Resolution"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:159
msgid ""
"Similar to evaluation, we can also speak of _partial_ versus _complete_ "
"derivation resolution. Partial derivation resolution is what we've actually "
"formalized above with \\\\(\\\\rightsquigarrow^\\*\\\\). Complete resolution "
"is resolution ending in a resolved term (deriving path or derivation). "
"(Which is a normal form of the relation, per the remark above.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:164
msgid ""
"With partial resolution, a derivation is related to equivalent derivations "
"with the same or simpler inputs, but not all those inputs will be plain "
"store paths. This is useful when the input refers to a floating content "
"addressed output we have not yet built — we don't know what (content-"
"address) store path will used for that derivation, so we are \"stuck\" "
"trying to resolve the deriving path in question. (In the above "
"formalization, this happens when the build trace is missing the keys we wish "
"to look up in it.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:168
msgid ""
"Complete resolution is a _functional_ relation, i.e. values on the left are "
"uniquely related with values on the right. It is not however, a _total_ "
"relation (in general, assuming arbitrary build traces). This is discussed in "
"the next section."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:172
msgid "Termination"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:174
msgid ""
"For static derivations graphs, complete resolution is indeed total, because "
"it always terminates for all inputs. (A relation that is both total and "
"functional is a function.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:177
msgid ""
"For [dynamic](../development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-dynamic-"
"derivations) derivation graphs, however, this is not the case — resolution "
"is not guaranteed to terminate. The issue isn't rewriting deriving paths "
"themselves: a single rewrite to normalize an output deriving path to a "
"constant one always exists, and always proceeds in one step. The issue is "
"that dynamic derivations (i.e. those that are filled-in the graph by a "
"previous resolution) may have more transitive dependencies than the original "
"derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:184
msgid "Suppose we have this deriving path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:188
msgid "\"...-foo.drv\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:189
msgid "\"bar.drv\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:194
msgid ""
"and derivation `foo` is already resolved. When we resolve deriving path "
"we'll end up with something like."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:198
msgid "\"...-foo-bar.drv\""
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:202
msgid ""
"So far is just an atomic single rewrite, with no termination issues. But the "
"derivation `foo-bar` may have its _own_ dynamic derivation inputs. "
"Resolution must resolve that derivation first before the above deriving path "
"can finally be normalized to a plain `...-foo-bar-baz` store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:206
msgid ""
"The important thing to notice is that while \"build trace\" _keys_ must be "
"resolved. The _value_ those keys are mapped to have no such constraints. An "
"arbitrary store object has no notion of being resolved or not. But, an "
"arbitrary store object can be read back as a derivation (as will in fact be "
"done in case for dynamic derivations / nested output deriving paths). And "
"those derivations need _not_ be resolved."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/resolution.md:212
msgid ""
"It is those dynamic non-resolved derivations which are the source of non-"
"termination. By the same token, they are also the reason why dynamic "
"derivations offer greater expressive power."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix uses a simplified model of the file system, which consists of file "
"system objects. Every file system object is one of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:6
msgid "\\[**Regular File**\\]{#regular}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:8
msgid "A possibly empty sequence of bytes for contents"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:9
msgid ""
"A single boolean representing the [executable](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/File-system_permissions#Permissions) permission"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:11
msgid "\\[**Directory**\\]{#directory}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:13
msgid "Mapping of names to child file system objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:15
msgid "\\[**Symbolic link**\\]{#symlink}"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:17
msgid "An arbitrary string, known as the _target_ of the symlink."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:19
msgid ""
"In general, Nix does not assign any semantics to symbolic links. Certain "
"operations however, may make additional assumptions and attempt to use the "
"target to find another file system object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:22
msgid ""
"See [the Wikipedia article on symbolic links](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/Symbolic_link) for background information if you are unfamiliar with "
"this Unix concept."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:24
msgid ""
"File system objects and their children form a tree. A bare file or symlink "
"can be a root file system object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:27
msgid ""
"Nix does not encode any other file system notions such as [hard links]"
"(https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_link), [permissions](https://"
"en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/File-system_permissions), timestamps, or other "
"metadata."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:29
msgid "Examples of file system objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:31
msgid "A plain file:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:37
msgid "An executable file:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:43
msgid "A symlink:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:49
msgid "A directory with contents:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object.md:62
msgid "A directory that contains a symlink and other directories:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:1
msgid "Content-Addressing File System Objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:3
msgid ""
"For many operations, Nix needs to calculate [a content addresses](../../"
"glossary.md#gloss-content-address) of [a file system object](../file-system-"
"object.md) (FSO). Usually this is needed as part of [content addressing "
"store objects](../store-object/content-address.md), since store objects "
"always have a root file system object. But some command-line utilities also "
"just work on \"raw\" file system objects, not part of any store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:9
msgid ""
"Every content addressing scheme Nix uses ultimately involves feeding data "
"into a [hash function](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash_function), and "
"getting back an opaque fixed-size digest which is deemed a content address. "
"The various _methods_ of content addressing thus differ in how abstract data "
"(in this case, a file system object and its descendants) are fed into the "
"hash function."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:12
msgid "Serialising File System Objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:14
msgid ""
"The simplest method is to serialise the entire file system object tree into "
"a single binary string, and then hash that binary string, yielding the "
"content address. In this section we describe the currently-supported methods "
"of serialising file system objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:17
msgid "Flat"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:19
msgid ""
"A single file object can just be hashed by its contents. This is not enough "
"information to encode the fact that the file system object is a file, but if "
"we _already_ know that the FSO is a single non-executable file by other "
"means, it is sufficient."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:23
msgid ""
"Because the hashed data is just the raw file, as is, this choice is good for "
"compatibility with other systems. For example, Unix commands like "
"`sha256sum` or `sha1sum` will produce hashes for single files that match "
"this."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:28
msgid ""
"For the other cases of [file system objects](../file-system-object.md), "
"especially directories with arbitrary descendants, we need a more complex "
"serialisation format. Examples of such serialisations are the ZIP and TAR "
"file formats. However, for our purposes these formats have two problems:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:32
msgid ""
"They do not have a canonical serialisation, meaning that given an FSO, there "
"can be many different serialisations. For instance, TAR files can have "
"variable amounts of padding between archive members; and some archive "
"formats leave the order of directory entries undefined. This would be bad "
"because we use serialisation to compute cryptographic hashes over file "
"system objects, and for those hashes to be useful as a content address or "
"for integrity checking, uniqueness is crucial. Otherwise, correct hashes "
"would report false mismatches, and the store would fail to find the content."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:39
msgid ""
"They store more information than we have in our notion of FSOs, such as time "
"stamps. This can cause FSOs that Nix should consider equal to hash to "
"different values on different machines, just because the dates differ."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:42
msgid ""
"As a practical consideration, the TAR format is the only truly universal "
"format in the Unix environment. It has many problems, such as an inability "
"to deal with long file names and files larger than 2^33 bytes. Current "
"implementations such as GNU Tar work around these limitations in various "
"ways."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:46
msgid ""
"For these reasons, Nix has its very own archive format—the Nix Archive (NAR) "
"format, which is carefully designed to avoid the problems described above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:49
msgid ""
"The exact specification of the Nix Archive format is in [specified here]"
"(../../protocols/nix-archive/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:51
msgid ""
"Content addressing File System Objects beyond a single serialisation pass"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:53
msgid ""
"Serialising the entire tree and then hashing that binary string is not the "
"only option for content addressing, however. Another technique is that of a "
"[Merkle graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merkle_tree), where previously "
"computed hashes are included in subsequent byte strings to be hashed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:56
msgid ""
"In particular, the Merkle graphs can match the original graph structure of "
"file system objects: we can first hash (serialised) child file system "
"objects, and then hash parent objects using the hashes of their children in "
"the serialisation (to be hashed) of the parent file system objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:59
msgid ""
"Currently, there is one such Merkle DAG content addressing method supported."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:61
msgid ""
"Git ([experimental](../../development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-"
"git-hashing))"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:65
msgid ""
"This method is part of the [`git-hashing`](../../development/experimental-"
"features.md#xp-feature-git-hashing) experimental feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:67
msgid ""
"Git's file system model is very close to Nix's, and so Git's content "
"addressing method is a pretty good fit. Just as with regular Git, files and "
"symlinks are hashed as git \"blobs\", and directories are hashed as git "
"\"trees\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:70
msgid ""
"However, one difference between Nix's and Git's file system model needs "
"special treatment. Plain files, executable files, and symlinks are not "
"differentiated as distinctly addressable objects, but by their context: by "
"the directory entry that refers to them. That means so long as the root "
"object is a directory, there is no problem: every non-directory object is "
"owned by a parent directory, and the entry that refers to it provides the "
"missing information. However, if the root object is not a directory, then we "
"have no way of knowing which one of an executable file, non-executable file, "
"or symlink it is supposed to be."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:76
msgid ""
"In response to this, we have decided to treat a bare file as non-executable "
"file. This is similar to do what we do with [flat serialisation](#serial-"
"flat), which also lacks this information. To avoid an address collision, "
"attempts to hash a bare executable file or symlink will result in an error "
"(just as would happen for flat serialisation also). Thus, Git can encode "
"some, but not all of Nix's \"File System Objects\", and this sort of content-"
"addressing is likewise partial."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/content-address.md:81
msgid ""
"In the future, we may support a Git-like hash for such file system objects, "
"or we may adopt another Merkle DAG format which is capable of representing "
"all Nix file system objects."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:1
msgid "Exposing File System Objects in real operating system file systems"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix's [file system object](../file-system-object.md) data model is minimal. "
"All the various other bits and pieces of real world filesystem interfaces, "
"such as [extended file attributes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Extended_file_attributes), are specifically ignored to reduce our interface "
"surface and the reproducibility issues associated with a larger interface. "
"In the view of Nix's developers, the types of simple, fine-grained batch "
"jobs (typically, building software) that Nix specializes in simply don't "
"benefit enough from that extra complexity for it to be worth the costs of "
"supporting it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:7
msgid ""
"But to actually be used by software, file system objects need to be made "
"available through the operating system's file system. This is sometimes "
"called \"mounting\" or \"exposing\" the file system object, though do note "
"it may or may not be implemented with what the operating system calls "
"\"mounting\"."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:12
msgid "Metadata normalization"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:14
msgid ""
"File systems typically contain other metadata that is outside Nix's data "
"model. To avoid this other metadata being a side channel and source of "
"nondeterminism, Nix is careful to normalize to fixed values. For example, on "
"Unix, the following metadata normalization occurs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:18
msgid ""
"The creation and last modification timestamps on all files are set to Unix "
"Epoch 1s (00:00:01 1/1/1970 UTC)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:20
msgid ""
"The group is set to the [default group](../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-"
"build-users-group)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:22
msgid ""
"The Unix mode of the file to 0444 or 0555 (i.e., read-only, with execute "
"permission enabled if the file was originally executable)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:24
msgid "Any possible `setuid` and `setgid` bits are cleared."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:28
msgid ""
"`setuid` and `setgid` programs are not currently supported by Nix. These "
"special file system permissions are in general a security footgun, and with "
"data owned by different users in different stores, it would especially be a "
"hazard when copying store objects between stores."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:31
msgid ""
"This restriction has not proved to be onerous in practice. For example, "
"NixOS uses so called setuid-wrappers which are outside the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:36
msgid ""
"As discussed before, Nix essentially shares its file system object data "
"model with other tools like Git. But those tools tend to ignore this "
"metadata in both directions --- when reading files, like Nix, but when "
"writing files, timestamps are set organically, and the user is free to set "
"other special permissions (`setuid`, `setgid`, sticky, etc.) however they "
"like. Normalizing, and not just ignoring, this metadata is therefore what "
"distinguishes Nix from these other tools more than the file system object "
"data model itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/file-system-object/os-file-system.md:40
msgid ""
"Nix's approach is motivated by deterministic building. Whereas Git can "
"assume that humans running commands will simply ignore timestamps etc. as "
"appropriate, understanding they are local and ephemeral, Nix aims to run "
"software that was not necessarily designed with Nix in mind, and is unaware "
"of whatever sandboxing/virtualization is in place."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/secrets.md:1
msgid "Secrets"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/secrets.md:3
msgid ""
"The store is readable to all users on the system. For this reason, it is "
"generally discouraged to allow secrets to make it into the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/secrets.md:6
msgid ""
"Even on a single-user system, separate system users isolate services from "
"each other and having secrets that all local users can read weakens that "
"isolation. When using external store caches the secrets may end up there, "
"and on multi-user systems the secrets will be available to all those users."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/secrets.md:12
msgid ""
"Organize your derivations so that secrets are read from the filesystem (with "
"appropriate access controls) at run time. Place the secrets on the "
"filesystem manually or use a scheme that includes the secret in the store in "
"encrypted form, and decrypts it adding the relevant access control on system "
"activation. Several such schemes for NixOS can in the [comparison of secret "
"managing schemes](https://wiki.nixos.org/wiki/"
"Comparison_of_secret_managing_schemes) on the wiki."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:1
msgid "Store Derivation and Deriving Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:3
msgid ""
"Besides functioning as a [content-addressed store](../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"content-addressed-store), the Nix store layer works as a [build system]"
"(../../glossary.md#gloss-build-system). Other systems (like Git or IPFS) "
"also store and transfer immutable data, but they don't concern themselves "
"with _how_ that data was created."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:6
msgid ""
"This is where Nix distinguishes itself. _Derivations_ represent individual "
"build steps, and _deriving paths_ are needed to refer to the _outputs_ of "
"those build steps before they are built."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:10
msgid "Store Derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:12
msgid ""
"A derivation is a specification for running an executable on precisely "
"defined input to produce one or more [store objects](../../store/store-"
"object.md). These store objects are known as the derivation's _outputs_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:15
msgid ""
"Derivations are _built_, in which case the process is spawned according to "
"the spec, and when it exits, required to leave behind files which will "
"(after post-processing) become the outputs of the derivation. This process "
"is described in detail in [Building](../../store/building.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:23
msgid "A derivation consists of:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:25
msgid "A name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:27
msgid ""
"An [inputs specification](#inputs), a set of [deriving paths](#deriving-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:29
msgid ""
"An [outputs specification](./outputs/index.md), specifying which outputs "
"should be produced, and various metadata about them."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:31
msgid ""
"The [\"system\" type](#system) (e.g. `x86_64-linux`) where the executable is "
"to run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:33
msgid ""
"The [process creation fields](#process-creation-fields): to spawn the "
"arbitrary process which will perform the build step."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:48
msgid "Referencing derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:50
msgid ""
"Derivations are always referred to by the [store path](../../store/store-"
"path.md) of the store object they are encoded to. See the [encoding section]"
"(#derivation-encoding) for more details on how this encoding works, and thus "
"what exactly what store path we would end up with for a given derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:53
msgid ""
"The store path of the store object which encodes a derivation is often "
"called a _derivation path_ for brevity."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:55
msgid "Deriving path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:57
msgid ""
"Deriving paths are a way to refer to [store objects](../../store/store-"
"object.md) that may or may not yet be [realised](../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"realise). There are two forms:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:60
msgid ""
"\\[_constant_\\]{#deriving-path-constant}: just a [store path](../../store/"
"store-path.md). It can be made [valid](../../glossary.md#gloss-validity) by "
"copying it into the store: from the evaluator, command line interface or "
"another store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:63
msgid ""
"\\[_output_\\]{#deriving-path-output}: a pair of a [store path](../../store/"
"store-path.md) to a [store derivation](#store-derivation) and an [output](./"
"outputs/index.md) name."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:65
msgid "In pseudo code:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:82
msgid ""
"Deriving paths are necessary because, in general and particularly for "
"[content-addressing derivations](../../glossary.md#gloss-content-addressing-"
"derivation), the [store path](../../store/store-path.md) of an [output](./"
"outputs/index.md) is not known in advance. We can use an output deriving "
"path to refer to such an output, instead of the store path which we do not "
"yet know."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:88
msgid "Parts of a derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:90
msgid ""
"A derivation is constructed from the parts documented in the following "
"subsections."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:94
msgid ""
"The inputs are a set of [deriving paths](#deriving-path), referring to all "
"store objects needed in order to perform this build step."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:96
msgid ""
"The [process creation fields](#process-creation-fields) will presumably "
"include many [store paths](../../store/store-path.md):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:98
msgid "The path to the executable normally starts with a store path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:99
msgid ""
"The arguments and environment variables likely contain many other store "
"paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:101
msgid ""
"But rather than somehow scanning all the other fields for inputs, Nix "
"requires that all inputs be explicitly collected in the inputs field. It is "
"instead the responsibility of the creator of a derivation (e.g. the "
"evaluator) to ensure that every store object referenced in another field "
"(e.g. referenced by store path) is included in this inputs field."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:103
msgid "System"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:105
msgid ""
"The system type on which the [`builder`](#builder) executable is meant to be "
"run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:107
msgid ""
"A necessary condition for Nix to schedule a given derivation on some [Nix "
"instance](../../glossary.md#gloss-nix-instance) is for the \"system\" of "
"that derivation to match that instance's [`system` configuration option]"
"(../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-system) or [`extra-platforms` "
"configuration option](../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-extra-platforms)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:109
msgid ""
"By putting the `system` in each derivation, Nix allows _heterogeneous_ build "
"plans, where not all steps can be run on the same machine or same sort of "
"machine. Nix can schedule builds such that it automatically builds on other "
"platforms by [forwarding build requests](../../advanced-topics/distributed-"
"builds.md) to other Nix instances."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:120
msgid "Process creation fields"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:122
msgid ""
"These are the three fields which describe how to spawn the process which "
"(along with any of its own child processes) will perform the build. You may "
"note that this has everything needed for an `execve` system call."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:125
msgid "Builder"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:127
msgid ""
"This is the path to an executable that will perform the build and produce "
"the [outputs](./outputs/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:131
msgid ""
"Command-line arguments to be passed to the [`builder`](#builder) executable."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:133
msgid ""
"Note that these are the arguments after the first argument. The first "
"argument passed to the `builder` will be the value of `builder`, as per the "
"usual convention on Unix. See [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Argv) for details."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:139
msgid ""
"Environment variables which will be passed to the [builder](#builder) "
"executable."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:141
msgid ""
"Each environment variable additionally has a \\[_pass as file_\\]{#pass-as-"
"file} flag. When it is set, rather than passing the value directly in the "
"environment, the value is written to a temporary file in the build "
"directory, and an environment variable named like the original but with a "
"`Path` suffix is set to the path of that file. For example, a variable `big` "
"with _pass as file_ set results in an environment variable `bigPath` "
"pointing to a file containing the value of `big`. This is useful for large "
"values, since most operating systems impose a limit on the size of the "
"process environment (typically a few hundred kilobytes)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:146
msgid ""
"This flag has no effect when [structured attributes](#structured-attrs) are "
"used, as attributes are then not passed through the environment in the first "
"place, so there is no size constraint to avoid."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:148
msgid "Structured Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:150
msgid "Nix also has special support for embedding JSON in the derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:152
msgid ""
"Structured attributes obviate the need for the [_pass as file_](#pass-as-"
"file) flag, since JSON files have no size restrictions, unlike process "
"environments. They also allow [options](#options) and [output checks]"
"(outputs/index.md#output-checks) to be expressed in a structured way, rather "
"than string-encoded in environment variables."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:155
msgid ""
"The environment variable `NIX_ATTRS_JSON_FILE` points to the exact location "
"of that file both in a build and a [`nix-shell`](../../command-ref/nix-"
"shell.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:157
msgid ""
"As a convenience to Bash builders, Nix writes a script that initialises "
"shell variables corresponding to all attributes that are representable in "
"Bash. The environment variable `NIX_ATTRS_SH_FILE` points to the exact "
"location of the script, both in a build and a [`nix-shell`](../../command-"
"ref/nix-shell.md). This includes non-nested (associative) arrays. For "
"example, the attribute `hardening.format = true` ends up as the Bash "
"associative array element `${hardening[format]}`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:164
msgid ""
"Derivations carry a number of _options_ influencing how they are built and "
"scheduled. Historically, these were encoded with specially-named "
"[environment variables](#env) or [structured attributes](#structured-attrs); "
"they are, however, fields of the derivation in their own right."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:167
msgid ""
"Options that apply to individual outputs — [output checks](outputs/"
"index.md#output-checks) and [reference scanning](outputs/index.md#reference-"
"scanning) — are documented with [derivation outputs](outputs/index.md). The "
"remaining options apply to the derivation as a whole:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:170
msgid ""
"\\[_export references graph_\\]{#export-references-graph}: A map from file "
"names to sets of [deriving paths](#deriving-path). For each entry, the "
"_references graph_ of the given store objects — each store object in their "
"[closure](../../glossary.md#gloss-closure), along with its references — is "
"made available to the builder: as a file of the given name in the build "
"directory (in the textual format understood by [`nix-store --register-"
"validity`](../../command-ref/nix-store/register-validity.md), with the "
"deriver fields left empty and no hashes), or, with [structured attributes]"
"(#structured-attrs), under the given key in the JSON attributes file (as a "
"list of store object infos)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:178
msgid "This lets a builder do something with the closure of its inputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:180
msgid "**Usage note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:182
msgid ""
"Examples of builders using this include the builders in NixOS that generate "
"the initial ramdisk for booting Linux (a `cpio` archive containing the "
"closure of the boot script) and the ISO-9660 image for the installation CD "
"(which is populated with a Nix store containing the closure of a bootable "
"NixOS configuration)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:185
msgid ""
"\\[_required system features_\\]{#required-system-features}: A set of "
"features that Nix must have among its [`system-features`](../../command-ref/"
"conf-file.md#conf-system-features) for the derivation to be scheduled on it, "
"in addition to the [system](#system) type matching."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:188
msgid ""
"\\[_prefer local build_\\]{#prefer-local-build}: A hint that the derivation "
"is cheapest to build locally: when [distributed building is enabled](../../"
"command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-builders), it will, if possible, be built "
"locally rather than forwarded to a remote machine."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:191
msgid ""
"\\[_allow substitutes_\\]{#allow-substitutes}: Whether the derivation's "
"outputs may be [substituted](../../glossary.md#gloss-substitute). When "
"disabled, the derivation is always built (locally or remotely); this is "
"useful for derivations that are cheaper to build than to substitute. Nix may "
"ignore this option via the [`always-allow-substitutes`](../../command-ref/"
"conf-file.md#conf-always-allow-substitutes) configuration option."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:198
msgid ""
"When disabled, some Nix instance must be able to run the [builder](#builder) "
"on the specified [system](#system) type, since the derivation cannot be "
"substituted."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:200
msgid ""
"\\[_impure environment variables_\\]{#impure-env-vars}: A set of environment "
"variables passed from the environment of the calling user to the builder, "
"which otherwise starts from a cleared environment. This is only allowed for "
"[fixed-output derivations](../../glossary.md#gloss-fixed-output-derivation), "
"where such impurities are tolerable because (the hash of) the output is "
"known in advance. It is ignored for all other derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:207
msgid ""
"The variables are taken from the environment of the building process itself: "
"when building via a daemon, from the daemon's environment, and only "
"otherwise from the environment of the calling command, e.g. `nix-build`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:210
msgid ""
"If the [`configurable-impure-env` experimental feature](../../development/"
"experimental-features.md#xp-feature-configurable-impure-env) is enabled, "
"these environment variables can also be controlled through the [`impure-env`]"
"(../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-impure-env) configuration option."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:213
msgid "Sandbox-related options:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:215
msgid ""
"\\[_no chroot_\\]{#no-chroot}: The derivation asks to be built without "
"[sandboxing](../../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-sandbox). Only under "
"certain configurations will this wish actually be respected, however. Nix "
"may instead simply fail the build, rather than try to perform it without "
"sandboxing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:220
msgid ""
"\\[_additional sandbox profile_\\]{#additional-sandbox-profile}: (Darwin "
"only) Additional sandbox profile text, granting the build extra permissions "
"inside the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:223
msgid ""
"\\[_impure host dependencies_\\]{#impure-host-deps}: (Darwin only) A set of "
"host system paths made available inside the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:226
msgid ""
"\\[_allow local networking_\\]{#allow-local-networking}: (Darwin only) "
"Whether the build may access the local network inside the sandbox."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:232
msgid "Placeholders"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:234
msgid ""
"Placeholders are opaque values used within the [process creation fields]"
"(#process-creation-fields) to \\[store objects\\] for which we don't yet "
"know [store path](../../store/store-path.md)s. They are strings in the form "
"`/<hash>` that are embedded anywhere within the strings of those fields, and "
"we are [considering](https://github.com/NixOS/nix/issues/12361) to add store-"
"path-like placeholders."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:239
msgid ""
"Output Deriving Path exist to solve the same problem as placeholders --- "
"that is, referring to store objects for which we don't yet know a store "
"path. They also have a string syntax with `^`, [described in the encoding "
"section](#deriving-path-encoding). We could use that syntax instead of `/"
"<hash>` for placeholders, but its human-legibility would cause problems."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:243
msgid ""
"There are two types of placeholder, corresponding to the two cases where "
"this problem arises:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:245
msgid "\\[Output placeholder\\]{#output-placeholder}:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:247
msgid "This is a placeholder for a derivation's own output."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:249
msgid "\\[Input placeholder\\]{#input-placeholder}:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:251
msgid ""
"This is a placeholder to a derivation's non-constant [input](#inputs), i.e. "
"an input that is an \\[output derived path\\]."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:256
msgid ""
"In general, we need to [realise](../../glossary.md#gloss-realise) a [store "
"object](../../store/store-object.md) in order to be sure to have a store "
"object for it. But for these two cases this is either impossible or "
"impractical:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:259
msgid "In the output case this is impossible:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:261
msgid ""
"We cannot build the output until we have a correct derivation, and we cannot "
"have a correct derivation (without using placeholders) until we have the "
"output path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:263
msgid "In the input case this is impractical:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:265
msgid ""
"If we always build a dependency first, and then refer to its output by store "
"path, we would lose the ability for a derivation graph to describe an entire "
"build plan consisting of multiple build steps."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:267 source/store/derivation/index.md:376
msgid "Encoding"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:271
msgid "There are two formats, documented separately:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:273
msgid "The legacy [\"ATerm\" format](../../protocols/derivation-aterm.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:275
msgid ""
"The experimental, currently under development and changing [JSON format]"
"(../../protocols/json/derivation/index.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:277
msgid ""
"Every derivation has a canonical choice of encoding used to serialize it to "
"a store object. This ensures that there is a canonical [store path](../../"
"store/store-path.md) used to refer to the derivation, as described in "
"[Referencing derivations](#derivation-path)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:282
msgid ""
"Currently, the canonical encoding for every derivation is the \"ATerm\" "
"format, but this is subject to change for the types of derivations which are "
"not yet stable."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:285
msgid ""
"Regardless of the format used, when serializing a derivation to a store "
"object, that store object will be content-addressed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:287
msgid "In the common case, the inputs to store objects are either:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:289
msgid ""
"[constant deriving paths](#deriving-path-constant) for content-addressed "
"source objects, which are \"initial inputs\" rather than the outputs of some "
"other derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:291
msgid "the outputs of other derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:293
msgid ""
"If those other derivations _also_ abide by this common case (and likewise "
"for transitive inputs), then the entire closure of the serialized derivation "
"will be content-addressed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:297
msgid "_constant_"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:299
msgid ""
"Constant deriving paths are encoded simply as the underlying store path is. "
"Thus, we see that every encoded store path is also a valid encoded "
"(constant) deriving path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:302
msgid "_output_"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:304
msgid "Output deriving paths are encoded by"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:306
msgid "encoding of a store path referring to a derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:308
msgid "a `^` separator (or `!` in some legacy contexts)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:310
msgid "the name of an output of the previously referred derivation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:318
msgid "This parses like so:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:320
msgid ""
"```\n"
"/nix/store/lxrn8v5aamkikg6agxwdqd1jz7746wz4-firefox-98.0.2.drv^out\n"
"|------------------------------------------------------------| |-|\n"
"store path (usual encoding)                                    output name\n"
"                                                          |--|\n"
"                                                          note the \".drv\"\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:328
msgid "Extending the model to be higher-order"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:330
msgid ""
"**Experimental feature**: [`dynamic-derivations`](../../development/"
"experimental-features.md#xp-feature-dynamic-derivations)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:332
msgid ""
"So far, we have used store paths to refer to derivations. That works because "
"we've implicitly assumed that all derivations are created _statically_ --- "
"created by some mechanism out of band, and then manually inserted into the "
"store. But what if derivations could also be created dynamically within Nix? "
"In other words, what if derivations could be the outputs of other "
"derivations?"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:339
msgid ""
"In the parlance of \"Build Systems à la carte\", we are generalizing the Nix "
"store layer to be a \"Monadic\" instead of \"Applicative\" build system."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:341
msgid ""
"How should we refer to such derivations? A deriving path works, the same as "
"how we refer to other derivation outputs. But what about a dynamic "
"derivations output? (i.e. how do we refer to the output of a derivation, "
"which is itself an output of a derivation?) For that we need to generalize "
"the definition of deriving path, replacing the store path used to refer to "
"the derivation with a nested deriving path:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:355
msgid ""
"Now, the `drvPath` field of `OutputPath` is itself a `DerivingPath` instead "
"of a `StorePath`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:357
msgid "With that change, here is updated definition:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:374
msgid ""
"Under this extended model, `DerivingPath`s are thus inductively built up "
"from a root `ConstantPath`, wrapped with zero or more outer `OutputPath`s."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/index.md:378
msgid ""
"The encoding is adjusted in the natural way, encoding the `drv` field "
"recursively using the same deriving path encoding. The result of this is "
"that it is possible to have a chain of `^<output-name>` at the end of the "
"final string, as opposed to just a single one."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:1
msgid "Derivation Outputs and Types of Derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:3
msgid ""
"As stated on the [main pages on derivations](../index.md#store-derivation), "
"a derivation produces [store objects](../../../store/store-object.md), which "
"are known as the _outputs_ of the derivation. Indeed, the entire point of "
"derivations is to produce these outputs, and to reliably and reproducibly "
"produce these derivations each time the derivation is run."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:7
msgid ""
"One of the parts of a derivation is its _outputs specification_, which "
"specifies certain information about the outputs the derivation produces when "
"run. The outputs specification is a map, from names to specifications for "
"individual outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:12
msgid ""
"Output names can be any string which is also a valid [store path name]"
"(../../../store/store-path.md#name). The name mapped to each output "
"specification is not actually the name of the output. In the general case, "
"the output store object has name `derivationName + \"-\" + outputSpecName`, "
"not any other metadata about it. However, an output spec named \"out\" "
"describes and output store object whose name is just the derivation name."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:19
msgid "A derivation is named `hello`, and has two outputs, `out`, and `dev`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:21
msgid "The derivation's path will be: `/nix/store/<hash>-hello.drv`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:23
msgid "The store path of `out` will be: `/nix/store/<hash>-hello`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:25
msgid "The store path of `dev` will be: `/nix/store/<hash>-hello-dev`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:27
msgid ""
"The outputs are the derivations are the [store objects](../../../store/store-"
"object.md) it is obligated to produce."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:31
msgid ""
"The formal terminology here is somewhat at odds with everyday communication "
"in the Nix community today. \"output\" in casual usage tends to refer to "
"either to the actual output store object, or the notional output spec, "
"depending on context."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:34
msgid ""
"For example \"hello's `dev` output\" means the store object referred to by "
"the store path `/nix/store/<hash>-hello-dev`. It is unusual to call this the "
"\"`hello-dev` output\", even though `hello-dev` is the actual name of that "
"store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:37
msgid "Types of output addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:39
msgid ""
"The main information contained in an output specification is how the "
"derivation output is addressed. In particular, the specification decides:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:42
msgid ""
"whether the output is [content-addressed](./content-address.md) or [input-"
"addressed](./input-address.md)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:44
msgid "if the content is content-addressed, how is it content addressed"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:46
msgid ""
"if the content is content-addressed, [what is its content address](./content-"
"address.md#fixed) (and thus what is its \\[store path\\])"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:50
msgid ""
"In addition to the output specification above, a derivation may mandate "
"additional _checks_ on its outputs: properties that must hold for the "
"produced store objects in order for the build to be considered successful. "
"Checks may be specified separately for each output, or once for all outputs "
"of the derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:54
msgid "Reference checks"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:56
msgid ""
"The main checks constrain the [references](../../../glossary.md#gloss-"
"reference) of an output. These checks vary along two axes, yielding 4 "
"possible checks:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:59
msgid ""
"Whether the check applies to the _direct_ references of the output, or to "
"its entire [closure](../../../glossary.md#gloss-closure) via _transitive_ "
"references ([requisites](../../../store/store-object.md#references))."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:61
msgid ""
"Whether the check _allows_ a set of references (every reference must be a "
"member of the set, though not every member needs to be referenced), or "
"_disallows_ a set of references (no reference may be a member of the set)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:63
msgid "The four checks are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:65
msgid ""
"\\[_allowed references_\\]{#allowed-references}: An optional set; the "
"output's references must be a subset of it. When the set is absent the check "
"is skipped, i.e. every reference is allowed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:69
msgid ""
"For example, the empty set enforces that the output has no runtime "
"dependencies at all."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:73
msgid ""
"This is used in NixOS to check that generated files such as initial ramdisks "
"for booting Linux don't have accidental dependencies on other paths in the "
"Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:75
msgid ""
"\\[_allowed requisites_\\]{#allowed-requisites}: Like _allowed references_, "
"but applying to the whole closure of the output: every transitive dependency "
"must be a member of the set."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:79
msgid ""
"\\[_disallowed references_\\]{#disallowed-references}: A set of which no "
"member may be a direct reference of the output. (There is no need for this "
"check to be optional: disallowing nothing is the same as skipping the check.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:83
msgid ""
"\\[_disallowed requisites_\\]{#disallowed-requisites}: Like _disallowed "
"references_, but applying to the whole closure of the output: no member of "
"the set may appear anywhere in the output's transitive dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:87
msgid ""
"The references of a store object are always store paths. However, if every "
"element of these sets had to be a store path, it would be hard-to-impossible "
"to constrain references from outputs _to other outputs_ of the same "
"derivation, because in general the store paths of outputs are not known "
"until the derivation is built. For this reason, an element of these sets may "
"also be an _output name_ of the derivation being checked, standing for the "
"store path of that output, whatever it turns out to be. For example, an "
"output can be permitted to reference itself by including its own name in its "
"_allowed references_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:92
msgid "Size checks"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:94
msgid ""
"\\[_max size_\\]{#max-size}: The [NAR size](../../../store/store-"
"object.md#nar-size) of the output's store object may not exceed the given "
"number of bytes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:97
msgid ""
"\\[_max closure size_\\]{#max-closure-size}: The [closure NAR size](../../../"
"store/store-object.md#closure-nar-size) of the output's store object may not "
"exceed the given number of bytes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:100
msgid "Self-reference handling"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:102
msgid ""
"\\[_ignore self references_\\]{#ignore-self-refs}: Whether references of an "
"output to itself are exempted from the reference checks above."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:105
msgid "Reference scanning"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:107
msgid ""
"Besides the output checks, there is one more per-output setting. It is _not_ "
"a check — nothing is verified about the produced store object — but it is "
"configured in the same per-output manner, so it is documented here:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:110
msgid ""
"\\[_unsafe discard references_\\]{#unsafe-discard-references}: Disables "
"[scanning the output for run-time references](../../building.md#scanning-for-"
"references) altogether. The output is then registered as having no "
"references, regardless of its contents."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:114
msgid ""
"This is useful, for example, when generating self-contained filesystem "
"images with their own embedded Nix store: hashes found inside such an image "
"refer to the embedded store and not to the host's Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:117
msgid ""
"As the name suggests, this is unsafe: discarding references that are in fact "
"needed at run time makes the closure incomplete, so copying the output "
"elsewhere will not bring its dependencies along."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:125
msgid "Types of derivations"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:127
msgid ""
"The sections on each type of derivation output addressing ended up "
"discussing other attributes of the derivation besides its outputs, such as "
"purity, scheduling, determinism, etc. This is no concidence; for the type of "
"a derivation is in fact one-for-one with the type of its outputs:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:130
msgid ""
"A derivation that produces _xyz-addressed_ outputs is an _xyz-addressing_ "
"derivations."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:132
msgid "The rules for this are fairly concise:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:134
msgid "All the outputs must be of the same type / use the same addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:136
msgid "The derivation must have at least one output"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:138
msgid ""
"Additionally, if the outputs are fixed content-addressed, there must be "
"exactly one output, whose specification is mapped from the name `out`. (The "
"name `out` is special, according to the rules described above. Having only "
"one output and calling its specification `out` means the single output is "
"effectively anonymous; the store path just has the derivation name.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:142
msgid "(This is an arbitrary restriction that could be lifted.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:144
msgid ""
"The output is either _fixed_ or _floating_, indicating whether the store "
"path is known prior to building it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:146
msgid "With fixed content-addressing it is fixed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:148
msgid ""
"A _fixed content-addressing_ derivation is also called a _fixed-output "
"derivation_, since that is the only currently-implemented form of fixed-"
"output addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:150
msgid "With floating content-addressing or input-addressing it is floating."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:152
msgid ""
"Thus, historically with Nix, with no experimental features enabled, _all_ "
"outputs are fixed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:154
msgid ""
"The derivation may be _pure_ or _impure_, indicating what read access to the "
"outside world the [builder](../index.md#builder) has."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:156
msgid "An input-addressing derivation _must_ be pure."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:158
msgid ""
"If it is impure, we would have a large problem, because an input-addressed "
"derivation always produces outputs with the same paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:161
msgid "A content-addressing derivation may be pure or impure"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:163
msgid ""
"If it is impure, it may be fixed (typical), or it may be floating if the "
"additional [`impure-derivations`](../../../development/experimental-"
"features.md#xp-feature-impure-derivations) experimental feature is enabled."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:165
msgid "If it is pure, it must be floating."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:167
msgid "Pure, fixed content-addressing derivations are not supported"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/index.md:169
msgid ""
"There is no use for this forth combination. The sole purpose of an output's "
"store path being fixed is to support the derivation being impure."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:1
msgid "Content-addressing derivation outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:3
msgid ""
"The content-addressing of an output only depends on that store object "
"itself, not any other information external (such has how it was made, when "
"it was made, etc.). As a consequence, a store object will be content-"
"addressed the same way regardless of whether it was manually inserted into "
"the store, outputted by some derivation, or outputted by a some other "
"derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:6
msgid ""
"The output spec for a content-addressed output must contains the following "
"field:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:8
msgid ""
"_method_: how the data of the store object is digested into a content address"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:10
msgid ""
"The possible choices of _method_ are described in the [section on content-"
"addressing store objects](../../../store/store-object/content-address.md). "
"Given the method, the output's name (computed from the derivation name and "
"output spec mapping as described above), and the data of the store object, "
"the output's store path will be computed as described in that section."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:13
msgid "Fixed-output content-addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:15
msgid ""
"In this case the content address of the _fixed_ in advanced by the "
"derivation itself. In other words, when the derivation has finished "
"[building](../../../store/building.md), and the provisional output' content-"
"address is computed as part of the process to turn it into a _bona fide_ "
"store object, the calculated content address must match that given in the "
"derivation, or the build of that derivation will be deemed a failure."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:18
msgid ""
"The output spec for an output with a fixed content addresses additionally "
"contains:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:20
msgid ""
"_hash_, the hash expected from digesting the store object's file system "
"objects. This hash may be of a freely-chosen hash algorithm (that Nix "
"supports)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:23
msgid "**Design note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:25
msgid ""
"In principle, the output spec could also specify the references the store "
"object should have, since the references and file system objects are equally "
"parts of a content-addressed store object proper that contribute to its "
"content-addressed. However, at this time, the references are not done "
"because all fixed content-addressed outputs are required to have no "
"references (including no self-reference)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:28
msgid ""
"Also in principle, rather than specifying the references and file system "
"object data with separate hashes, a single hash that constraints both could "
"be used. This could be done with the final store path's digest, or better "
"yet, the hash that will become the store path's digest before it is "
"truncated."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:31
msgid ""
"These possible future extensions are included to elucidate the core property "
"of fixed-output content addressing --- that all parts of the output must be "
"cryptographically fixed with one or more hashes --- separate from the "
"particulars of the currently-supported store object content-addressing "
"schemes."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:33
msgid "Design rationale"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:35
msgid ""
"What is the purpose of fixing an output's content address in advanced? In "
"abstract terms, the answer is carefully controlled impurity. Unlike a "
"regular derivation, the [builder](../../../store/derivation/"
"index.md#builder) executable of a derivation that produced fixed outputs has "
"access to the network. The outputs' guaranteed content-addresses are "
"supposed to mitigate the risk of the builder being given these capabilities; "
"regardless of what the builder does _during_ the build, it cannot influence "
"downstream builds in unanticipated ways because all information it passed "
"downstream flows through the outputs whose content-addresses are fixed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:43
msgid ""
"In concrete terms, the purpose of this feature is fetching fixed input data "
"like source code from the network. For example, consider a family of \"fetch "
"URL\" derivations. These derivations download files from given URL. To "
"ensure that the downloaded file has not been modified, each derivation must "
"also specify a cryptographic hash of the file. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:49
msgid ""
"```jsonc\n"
"{\n"
"  \"outputs: {\n"
"    \"out\": {\n"
"      \"method\": \"nar\",\n"
"      \"hashAlgo\": \"sha256\",\n"
"      \"hash: \"1md7jsfd8pa45z73bz1kszpp01yw6x5ljkjk2hx7wl800any6465\",\n"
"    },\n"
"  },\n"
"  \"env\": {\n"
"    \"url\": \"http://ftp.gnu.org/pub/gnu/hello/hello-2.1.1.tar.gz\"\n"
"    // ...\n"
"  },\n"
"  // ...\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:66
msgid ""
"It sometimes happens that the URL of the file changes, e.g., because servers "
"are reorganised or no longer available. In these cases, we then must update "
"the call to `fetchurl`, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:78
msgid ""
"If a `fetchurl` derivation's outputs were [input-addressed](./input-"
"address.md), the output paths of the derivation and of _all derivations "
"depending on it_ would change. For instance, if we were to change the URL of "
"the Glibc source distribution in Nixpkgs (a package on which almost all "
"other packages depend on Linux) massive rebuilds would be needed. This is "
"unfortunate for a change which we know cannot have a real effect as it "
"propagates upwards through the dependency graph."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:82
msgid ""
"For content-addressed outputs (fixed or floating), on the other hand, the "
"outputs' store path only depends on the derivation's name, data, and the "
"`method` of the outputs' specs. The rest of the derivation is ignored for "
"the purpose of computing the output path."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:85
msgid "**History Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:87
msgid ""
"Fixed content-addressing is especially important both today and historically "
"as the _only_ form of content-addressing that is stabilized. This is why the "
"rationale above contrasts it with [input addressing](./input-address.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:90
msgid "(Floating) Content-Addressing"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:92
msgid ""
"**Warning** This is part of an [experimental feature](../../../development/"
"experimental-features.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:95
msgid ""
"To use this type of output addressing, you must enable the [`ca-derivations`]"
"(../../../development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-ca-derivations) "
"experimental feature. For example, in [nix.conf](../../../command-ref/conf-"
"file.md) you could add:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:103
msgid ""
"With this experimemental feature enabled, derivation outputs can also be "
"content-addressed _without_ fixing in the output spec what the outputs' "
"content address must be."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:105
msgid "Purity"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:107
msgid ""
"Because the derivation output is not fixed (just like with [input addressing]"
"(./input-address.md)), the [builder](../../../store/derivation/"
"index.md#builder) is not given any impure capabilities [^purity]."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:109
msgid "**Configuration note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:111
msgid ""
"Strictly speaking, the extent to which sandboxing and deprivilaging is "
"possible varies with the environment Nix is running in. Nix's configuration "
"settings indicate what level of sandboxing is required or enabled. Builds of "
"derivations will fail if they request an absence of sandboxing which is not "
"allowed. Builds of derivations will also fail if the level of sandboxing "
"specified in the configure exceeds what is possible in the given environment."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:116
msgid ""
"(The \"environment\", in this case, consists of attributes such as the "
"Operating System Nix runs atop, along with the operating-system-specific "
"privileges that Nix has been granted. Because of how conventional operating "
"systems like macos, Linux, etc. work, granting builders _fewer_ privileges "
"may ironically require that Nix be run with _more_ privileges.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:119
msgid ""
"That said, derivations producing floating content-addressed outputs may "
"declare their builders as impure (like the builders of derivations producing "
"fixed outputs). This is provisionally supported as part of the [`impure-"
"derivations`](../../../development/experimental-features.md#xp-feature-"
"impure-derivations) experimental feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:122
msgid "Compatibility negotiation"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:124
msgid ""
"Any derivation producing a floating content-addressed output implicitly "
"requires the `ca-derivations` [system feature](../../../command-ref/conf-"
"file.md#conf-system-features). This prevents scheduling the building of the "
"derivation on a machine without the experimental feature enabled. Even once "
"the experimental feature is stabilized, this is still useful in order to be "
"allow using remote builder running odler versions of Nix, or alternative "
"implementations that do not support floating content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:128
msgid "Determinism"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:130
msgid ""
"In the earlier [discussion of how self-references are handled when content-"
"addressing store objects](../../../store/store-object/content-"
"address.html#self-references), it was pointed out that methods of producing "
"store objects ought to be deterministic regardless of the choice of "
"provisional store path. For store objects produced by manually inserting "
"into the store to create a store object, the \"method of production\" is an "
"informally concept --- formally, Nix has no idea where the store object came "
"from, and content-addressing is crucial in order to ensure that the "
"derivation is _intrinsically_ tamper-proof. But for store objects produced "
"by derivation, the \"method is quite formal\" --- the whole point of "
"derivations is to be a formal notion of building, after all. In this case, "
"we can elevate this informal property to a formal one."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:135
msgid ""
"A _deterministic_ content-addressing derivation should produce outputs with "
"the same content addresses:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:137
msgid "Every time the builder is run"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:139
msgid ""
"This is because either the builder is completely sandboxed, or because all "
"any remaining impurities that leak inside the build sandbox are ignored by "
"the builder and do not influence its behavior."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:141
msgid "Regardless of the choice of any provisional outputs paths"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:143
msgid ""
"Provisional store paths must be chosen for any output that has a self-"
"reference. The choice of provisional store path can be thought of as an "
"impurity, since it is an arbitrary choice."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:146
msgid ""
"If provisional outputs paths are deterministically chosen, we are in the "
"first branch of part (1). The builder the data it produces based on it in "
"arbitrary ways, but this gets us closer to [input addressing](./input-"
"address.md). Deterministically choosing the provisional path may be "
"considered \"complete sandboxing\" by removing an impurity, but this is "
"unsatisfactory"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:157
msgid ""
"If provisional outputs paths are randomly chosen, we are in the second "
"branch of part (1). The builder _must_ not let the random input affect the "
"final outputs it produces, and multiple builds may be performed and the "
"compared in order to ensure that this is in fact the case."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:160
msgid "Floating versus Fixed"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:162
msgid ""
"While the distinction between content- and input-addressing is one of "
"_mechanism_, the distinction between fixed and floating content addressing "
"is more one of _policy_. A fixed output that passes its content address "
"check is just like a floating output. It is only in the potential for that "
"check to fail that they are different."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:166
msgid "**Design Note**"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:168
msgid ""
"In a future world where floating content-addressing is also stable, we in "
"principle no longer need separate [fixed](#fixed) content-addressing. "
"Instead, we could always use floating content-addressing, and separately "
"assert the precise value content address of a given store object to be used "
"as an input (of another derivation). A stand-alone assertion object of this "
"sort is not yet implemented, but its possible creation is tracked in [issue "
"#11955](https://github.com/NixOS/nix/issues/11955)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:172
msgid ""
"In the current version of Nix, fixed outputs which fail their hash check are "
"still registered as valid store objects, just not registered as outputs of "
"the derivation which produced them. This is an optimization that means if "
"the wrong output hash is specified in a derivation, and then the derivation "
"is recreated with the right output hash, derivation does not need to be "
"rebuilt — avoiding downloading potentially large amounts of data twice. This "
"optimisation prefigures the design above: If the output hash assertion was "
"removed outside the derivation itself, Nix could additionally not only "
"register that outputted store object like today, but could also make note "
"that derivation did in fact successfully download some data. For example, "
"for the \"fetch URL\" example above, making such a note is tantamount to "
"recording what data is available at the time of download at the given URL. "
"It would only be when Nix subsequently tries to build something with that "
"(refining our example) downloaded source code that Nix would be forced to "
"check the output hash assertion, preventing it from e.g. building "
"compromised malware."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:179
msgid "Recapping, Nix would"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:181
msgid "successfully download data"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:182
msgid "insert that data into the store"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:183
msgid ""
"associate (presumably with some sort of expiration policy) the downloaded "
"data with the derivation that downloaded it"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:185
msgid ""
"But only use the downloaded store object in subsequent derivations that "
"depended upon the assertion if the assertion passed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/content-address.md:187
msgid ""
"This possible future extension is included to illustrate this distinction:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:1
msgid "Input-addressing derivation outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:5
msgid ""
"\"Input addressing\" means the address the store object by the _way it was "
"made_ rather than _what it is_. That is to say, an input-addressed output's "
"store path is a function not of the output itself, but of the derivation "
"that produced it. Even if two store paths have the same contents, if they "
"are produced in different ways, and one is input-addressed, then they will "
"have different store paths, and thus guaranteed to not be the same store "
"object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:9
msgid "Masking content addressed derivation outputs"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:11
msgid ""
"A naive implementation of an output hash computation for input-addressed "
"outputs would be to hash the derivation hash and output together. This "
"clearly has the uniqueness properties we want for input-addressed outputs, "
"but suffers from an inefficiency. Specifically, new builds would be required "
"whenever a change is made to a fixed-output derivation, despite having "
"provably no differences in the inputs to the new derivation compared to what "
"it used to be. Concretely, this would cause a \"mass rebuild\" whenever any "
"fetching detail changes, including mirror lists, certificate authority "
"certificates, etc."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:16
msgid ""
"To solve this problem, we compute output hashes differently, so that certain "
"output hashes become identical. We do this by _masking_: before hashing, we "
"rewrite the parts of the derivation whose differences we do not want to see."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:19
msgid "There are two maskings, and they are independent:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:21
msgid ""
"_Input masking_ replaces each input derivation's store path with a hash "
"standing for what that input _produces_, rather than for the derivation that "
"produces it. This is what makes different fixed-output derivation inputs "
"that produce the same outputs stop mattering."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:24
msgid ""
"_Output masking_ blanks the derivation's own output paths --- in `outputs`, "
"and in the environment variables named after them. This is needed when those "
"very paths are what we are computing; it is not wanted when the derivation "
"is merely being hashed to stand in for an input to something else, where the "
"paths it already has are exactly what identifies it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:27
msgid ""
"A derivation with only the first applied is an _input-masked derivation_; "
"one with both is a _fully masked derivation_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:29
msgid ""
"Every non-injective function induces an equivalence relation on its domain, "
"by saying two elements are equivalent if they map to the same value. But the "
"equivalence relation induced by input masking is also pleasant to "
"characterize directly, without reference to input masking at all. "
"Intuitively this makes sense, because the details of how exactly inputs are "
"hashed (which hash function, say) shouldn't matter (ignoring hash collisions "
"and the like) --- all that matters is which derivations are deemed "
"equivalent. The [semantic properties](#semantic-properties) section below "
"develops this further."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:34
msgid ""
"So how do we compute the [hash part](../../../store/store-path.md#digest) of "
"the output paths of an input-addressed derivation? This is done by the "
"function `hashInputMaskedDerivation`, shown below, which does the input "
"masking itself; its callers do any output masking."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:37
msgid ""
"First, a word on inputs. `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is only defined on "
"derivations whose [inputs](../../../store/derivation/index.md#inputs) take "
"the first-order form:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:54
msgid ""
"For the algorithm below, we adopt a derivation where the two types of (first "
"order) derived paths are partitioned into two sets, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:55
msgid ""
"```typescript\n"
"type Derivation = {\n"
"  // inputs: Set<FirstOrderDerivingPath>; // replaced\n"
"  inputSrcs: Set<ConstantPath>; // new instead\n"
"  inputDrvOutputs: Set<FirstOrderOutputPath>; // new instead\n"
"  // ...other fields...\n"
"};\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:64
msgid ""
"In the [currently-experimental](../../../development/experimental-"
"features.md#xp-feature-dynamic-derivations) higher-order case where outputs "
"of outputs are allowed as [deriving paths](../../../store/derivation/"
"index.md#deriving-path) and thus derivation inputs, derivations using that "
"generalization are not valid arguments to this function. Those derivations "
"must be (partially) [resolved](../../../store/resolution.md) enough first, "
"to the point where no such higher-order inputs remain. Then, and only then, "
"can input addresses be assigned."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:68
msgid ""
"```\n"
"function hashInputMaskedDerivation(drv) -> Hash:\n"
"    assert(drv.outputs are input-addressed)\n"
"    drv′ ← drv with {\n"
"        inputDrvOutputs = ⋃(\n"
"            assert(drvPath is store path)\n"
"            case hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation(readDrv(drvPath)) of\n"
"                drvHash : Hash →\n"
"                    (drvHash.toBase16(), output)\n"
"                outputHashes : Map[String, Hash] →\n"
"                    (outputHashes[output].toBase16(), \"out\")\n"
"            | (drvPath, output) ∈ drv.inputDrvOutputs\n"
"        )\n"
"    }\n"
"    return hashSHA256(printDrv(drv′))\n"
"\n"
"function hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation(drv) -> Map[String, Hash] | "
"Hash:\n"
"    if drv.outputs are content-addressed:\n"
"        return {\n"
"            outputName ↦ hashSHA256(\n"
"                \"fixed:out:\" + ca.printMethodAlgo() +\n"
"                \":\" + ca.hash.toBase16() +\n"
"                \":\" + ca.makeFixedOutputPath(drv.name, outputName))\n"
"            | (outputName ↦ output) ∈ drv.outputs\n"
"            , ca = output.contentAddress // or get from build trace if "
"floating\n"
"        }\n"
"    else: // drv.outputs are input-addressed\n"
"        return hashInputMaskedDerivation(drv)\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:98
msgid "`hashInputMaskedDerivation`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:100
msgid ""
"We replace each element in the derivation's `inputDrvOutputs` using data "
"from a call to `hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` on the `drvPath` of that "
"element. When `hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` returns a single drv hash "
"(because the input derivation in question is input-addressing), we simply "
"swap out the `drvPath` for that hash, and keep the same output name. When "
"`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` returns a map of content addresses per-"
"output, we look up the output in question, and pair it with the output name "
"`out`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:104
msgid ""
"That rewriting is the input masking. The resulting pseudo-derivation (with "
"hashes instead of store paths in `inputDrvs`) is then printed (in the "
"[\"ATerm\" format](../../../protocols/derivation-aterm.md#input-address-"
"encoding)) and hashed, and this becomes the hash of the input-masked "
"derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:107
msgid ""
"When calculating output hashes, `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is called on an "
"almost-complete input-addressing derivation, which is just missing its input-"
"addressed outputs paths --- that is, one that is already output masked, "
"making it fully masked. The derivation hash is then used to calculate output "
"paths for each output, as the [complete store path calculation](../../../"
"protocols/store-path.md#fingerprint) specifies: it is the digest of the "
"hashed ATerm that specification calls for, under the `\"output:\" id` case, "
"where `id` is the output name. The store object's name is the derivation's "
"name, with `-` and the output name appended --- unless the output is named "
"`out`, in which case the derivation's name is used as-is."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:111
msgid ""
"Note that one derivation hash serves every output; what distinguishes their "
"paths is only the output name, which enters twice over, as both `id` and "
"part of the name."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:113
msgid ""
"Those output paths can then be substituted into the almost-complete input-"
"addressed derivation to complete it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:117
msgid ""
"There may be an unintentional deviation from specification currently "
"implemented in the `(outputHashes[output].toBase16(), \"out\")` case. This "
"is not fatal because the deviation would only apply for content-addressing "
"derivations with more than one output, and that only occurs in the floating "
"case, which is [experimental](../../../development/experimental-"
"features.md#xp-feature-ca-derivations). Once this bug is fixed, this note "
"will be removed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:121
msgid "`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:123
msgid ""
"`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` consists of two main cases, based on "
"whether the outputs of the derivation are to be input-addressed or content-"
"addressed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:125
msgid "Input-addressed outputs case"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:127
msgid ""
"In the input-addressed case, it just calls `hashInputMaskedDerivation`, and "
"returns that derivation hash. This makes `hashInputMaskedDerivation` and "
"`hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` mutually-recursive."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:132
msgid ""
"In this case, `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is being called on a _complete_ "
"input-addressing derivation that already has its output paths calculated: it "
"is not output masked, so it is input-masked only, not fully masked. The "
"input masking takes place anyways."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:135
msgid "Content-addressed outputs case"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:137
msgid ""
"If the outputs are [content-addressed](./content-address.md), then it "
"computes a hash for each output derived from the content-address of that "
"output."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:141
msgid ""
"In the [fixed](./content-address.md#fixed) content-addressing case, the "
"outputs' content addresses are statically specified in advance, so this "
"always just works. (The fixed case is what the pseudo-code shows.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:144
msgid ""
"In the [floating](./content-address.md#floating) case, the content addresses "
"are not specified in advance. This is what the \"or get from [build trace]"
"(../../../store/build-trace.md) if floating\" comment refers to. In this "
"case, the algorithm is _stuck_ until the input in question is built, and we "
"know what the actual contents of the output in question is."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:148
msgid ""
"That is OK however, because there is no problem with delaying the assigning "
"of input addresses (which, remember, is what `hashInputMaskedDerivation` is "
"ultimately for) until all inputs are known."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:150
msgid "Deferring input-addressing when downstream of unknown CA store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:152
msgid ""
"As the previous note says, the algorithm is _stuck_ when an input is a "
"[floating content-addressed](./content-address.md#floating) output that has "
"not been built yet: there is no content address to substitute for it, so the "
"input masking cannot be completed and no input address can be computed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:154
msgid ""
"The derivation is still written to the store, with its outputs _deferred_: "
"an output in this state has no store path at all, and the environment "
"variable named after it is empty. This costs nothing, because a derivation's "
"own store path is [content-addressed](../../../store/store-object/content-"
"address.md#method-text) on the bytes of its serialization, which do not "
"depend on its output paths being known."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:157
msgid ""
"A deferred output does not use a third kind of addressing alongside input- "
"and content-addressing. It is input-addressed; it just does not know its "
"path yet."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:160
msgid ""
"Being stuck is contagious. A derivation with deferred outputs has, for this "
"purpose, unknown outputs of its own, so `hashOutputsOrInputMaskedDerivation` "
"is stuck on it in turn, and anything depending on it must also defer. The "
"condition therefore propagates downstream from the floating output that "
"caused it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:164
msgid ""
"Deferral is resolved by building, not by revisiting the same derivation. "
"Once the offending inputs are built, their content addresses are known, and "
"the derivation can be [resolved](../../../store/resolution.md): its inputs "
"are replaced by the plain store paths they turned out to denote. The "
"resolved derivation is a _different_ derivation, with its own store path --- "
"necessarily so, since it has different bytes. It has no input derivations "
"left to be stuck on, so its input addresses are computable, and it is what "
"actually gets built."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:171
msgid ""
"Nothing gates deferred outputs themselves. They only _arise_ downstream of "
"[floating content-addressed](./content-address.md#floating) outputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:174
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:176
msgid ""
"The recursion in the algorithm is potentially inefficient: it could call "
"itself once for each path by which a subderivation can be reached, i.e., "
"`O(V^k)` times for a derivation graph with `V` derivations and with out-"
"degree of at most `k`. In the actual implementation, [memoisation](https://"
"en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memoization) is used to reduce this cost to be "
"proportional to the total number of `inputDrvOutputs` encountered."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:180
msgid "Semantic properties"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:182
msgid ""
"_See [this chapter's appendix](../../../store/math-notation.md) on grammar "
"and metavariable conventions._"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:184
msgid ""
"Masking is, mathematically, a quotient, in reference to quotient types or "
"sets: what it discards is exactly what we want two derivations to be allowed "
"to differ in. Accordingly, `hashInputMaskedDerivation` partitions input-"
"addressing derivations into equivalence classes: every derivation in that "
"equivalence class is mapped to the same derivation hash. We can characterize "
"this equivalence relation directly, by working bottom up."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:188
msgid ""
"We start by defining an equivalence relation on first-order output deriving "
"paths that refer content-addressed derivation outputs. Two such paths are "
"equivalent if they refer to the same store object:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:190
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$d\\_1$ is content-addressing} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$d\\_2$ is content-addressing} \\\\AxiomC{$ {}^\\*(\\text{path}"
"(d\\_1), o\\_1) = {}^\\*(\\text{path}(d\\_2), o\\_2) $} \\\\TrinaryInfC{$"
"(\\text{path}(d\\_1), o\\_1) \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathrm{CA}}\\\\, (d\\_2, "
"o\\_2)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:203
msgid ""
"where \\\\({}^\\*(s, o)\\\\) denotes the store object that the output "
"deriving path refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:205
msgid ""
"We will also need the following construction to lift any equivalence "
"relation on \\\\(X\\\\) to an equivalence relation on (finite) sets of \\\\"
"(X\\\\) (in short, \\\\(\\\\mathcal{P}(X)\\\\)):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:207
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\forall a \\\\in A. \\\\exists b "
"\\\\in B. a \\\\,\\\\sim\\_X\\\\, b$} \\\\AxiomC{$\\\\forall b \\\\in B. \\"
"\\exists a \\\\in A. b \\\\,\\\\sim\\_X\\\\, a$} \\\\BinaryInfC{$A \\\\,\\"
"\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(X)}\\\\, B$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:215
msgid ""
"Now we can define the equivalence relation \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IA}\\"
"\\) on input-addressed derivation outputs. Two input-addressed outputs are "
"equivalent if their derivations are equivalent (via the yet-to-be-defined \\"
"\\(\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\) relation) and their output names are "
"the same:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:217
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\AxiomC{$d\\_1$ is input-addressing} \\"
"\\AxiomC{$d\\_2$ is input-addressing} \\\\AxiomC{$d\\_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\, d\\_2$} \\\\AxiomC{$o\\_1 = o\\_2$} \\"
"\\QuaternaryInfC{$(\\text{path}(d\\_1), o\\_1) \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathrm{IA}}\\\\, (\\text{path}(d\\_2), o\\_2)$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:227
msgid ""
"And now we can define \\\\(\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\). Two input-"
"addressed derivations are equivalent if their content-addressed inputs are "
"equivalent, their input-addressed inputs are also equivalent, and they are "
"otherwise equal:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:232
msgid ""
"\\\\\\[ \\\\begin{prooftree} \\\\alwaysNoLine \\\\AxiomC{$ \\"
"\\mathrm{caInputs}(d\\_1) \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{CA})}\\"
"\\, \\\\mathrm{caInputs}(d\\_2) $} \\\\AxiomC{$ \\\\mathrm{iaInputs}(d\\_1) "
"\\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\\\mathcal{P}(\\\\mathrm{IA})}\\\\, \\\\mathrm{iaInputs}"
"(d\\_2) $} \\\\BinaryInfC{$ d\\_1\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputDrvOutputs} := \\\\"
"{\\\\}\\right\\] = d\\_2\\left\\[\\\\mathrm{inputDrvOutputs} := \\\\{\\\\}"
"\\right\\] $} \\\\alwaysSingleLine \\\\UnaryInfC{$d\\_1 \\\\,\\\\sim\\_{\\"
"\\mathrm{IADrv}}\\\\, d\\_2$} \\\\end{prooftree} \\\\\\]"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:255
msgid ""
"where \\\\(\\\\mathrm{caInputs}(d)\\\\) returns the content-addressed inputs "
"of \\\\(d\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\mathrm{iaInputs}(d)\\\\) returns the input-"
"addressed inputs."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:259
msgid ""
"An astute reader might notice that that nowhere does `inputSrcs` enter into "
"these definitions. That means that replacing an input derivation with its "
"outputs directly added to `inputSrcs` always results in a derivation in a "
"different equivalence class, despite the resulting input closure (as would "
"be mounted in the store at build time) being the same. [Issue #9259](https://"
"github.com/NixOS/nix/issues/9259) is about creating a coarser equivalence "
"relation to address this."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:263
msgid ""
"\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\) from [derivation resolution](../../../"
"store/resolution.md) is such an equivalence relation. It is coarser than "
"this one: any two derivations which are \"'hash input-masked derivation'-"
"equivalent\" (\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IADrv}\\\\)) are also \"resolution-"
"equivalent\" (\\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\)). It also relates "
"derivations whose `inputDrvOutputs` have been rewritten into `inputSrcs`."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/derivation/outputs/input-address.md:267
msgid ""
"Input-addressing downstream of content addressing, as described above, "
"anticipates this. Since a content-addressed input contributes only its "
"content address, and not the derivation that produced it, derivations "
"differing only in _how_ an identical content-addressed input was produced "
"are already \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IADrv}\\\\)-equivalent. And since a "
"deferred derivation is completely resolved before it is input-addressed, its "
"input-addressed `inputDrvOutputs` are normalized to `inputSrcs` as well --- "
"so derivations differing only in how an identical _input-addressed_ input "
"was produced are \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{IADrv}\\\\)-equivalent too. That "
"is the same \"identify an input by what it is, rather than by how it was "
"made\" move as \\\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\). The difference is that \\"
"\\(\\\\sim\\_\\\\mathrm{Drv}\\\\) makes it unconditionally, whereas here it "
"falls out for content-addressed inputs always, and for the rest only where "
"deferral forced a resolution first."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:3
msgid ""
"A Nix store is a collection of _store objects_ with _references_ between "
"them. A store object consists of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:6
msgid "A [file system object](./file-system-object.md) as data"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:8
msgid "A set of [store paths](./store-path.md) as references to store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:12
msgid ""
"Store objects can refer to both other store objects and themselves. "
"References from a store object to itself are called _self-references_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:15
msgid ""
"Store objects and their references form a directed graph, where the store "
"objects are the vertices, and the references are the edges. In particular, "
"the edge corresponding to a reference is from the store object that contains "
"the reference, and to the store object that the store path (which is the "
"reference) refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:18
msgid ""
"References other than a self-reference must not form a cycle. The graph of "
"references excluding self-references thus forms a [directed acyclic graph]"
"(../glossary.md#gloss-directed-acyclic-graph)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:23
msgid ""
"We can take the [transitive closure](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Transitive_closure) of the references graph, in which any pair of store "
"objects have an edge if a _path_ of one or more references exists from the "
"first to the second object. (A single reference always forms a path which is "
"one reference long, but longer paths may connect objects which have no "
"direct reference between them.) The _requisites_ of a store object are all "
"store objects reachable by paths of references which start with given store "
"object's references."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:29
msgid ""
"We can also take the [transpose graph](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Transpose_graph) of the references graph, where we reverse the orientation "
"of all edges. The _referrers_ of a store object are the store objects that "
"reference it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:34
msgid ""
"One can also combine both concepts: taking the transitive closure of the "
"transposed references graph. The _referrers closure_ of a store object are "
"the store objects that can reach the given store object via paths of "
"references."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:39
msgid ""
"Care must be taken to distinguish between the intrinsic and extrinsic "
"properties of store objects. We can create graphs from the store objects in "
"a store, but the contents of the store is not, in general fixed, and may "
"instead change over time."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:42
msgid ""
"The references of a store object --- the set of store paths called the "
"references --- is a field of a store object, and thus intrinsic by "
"definition. Regardless of what store contains the store object in question, "
"and what else that store may or may not contain, the references are the same."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:45
msgid ""
"The requisites of a store object are almost intrinsic --- some store paths "
"do not precisely refer to a unique single store object. Exactly what store "
"object is being referenced, and what in turn _its_ references are, depends "
"on the store in question. Different stores may disagree on what a given "
"store path refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:49
msgid ""
"The referrers of a store object are completely extrinsic, and depends solely "
"on the store which contains that store object, not the store object itself. "
"Other store objects which refer to the store object in question may be added "
"or removed from the store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:52
msgid "Immutability"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:54
msgid ""
"Store objects are [immutable](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Immutable_object): Once created, they do not change nor can any store object "
"they reference be changed."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:59
msgid ""
"Stores which support atomically deleting multiple store objects allow more "
"flexibility while still upholding this property."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:61
msgid "Closure property"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:63
msgid ""
"A store can only contain a store object if it also contains all the store "
"objects it refers to."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:67
msgid ""
"The \"closure property\" isn't meant to prohibit, for example, [lazy loading]"
"(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lazy_loading) of store objects. However, the "
"\"closure property\" and immutability in conjunction imply that any such "
"lazy loading ought to be deterministic."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:70
msgid "Store Object Metadata"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:72
msgid ""
"[Store implementations](../store/types/index.md) currently associate more "
"information than described above with a store object. Quite arguably some of "
"this information doesn't belong here, because it conflates concerns."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:75
msgid "Two such properties are sizes:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:77
msgid ""
"\\[_NAR size_\\]{#nar-size}: The size in bytes of the store object's [file "
"system object](./file-system-object.md), serialised as a [Nix Archive](./"
"file-system-object/content-address.md#serial-nix-archive)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:80
msgid ""
"This is an intrinsic property of the store object itself, and not of the way "
"any particular store happens to store it. In particular, it is unrelated to "
"the space the store object occupies on disk, which varies with compression, "
"deduplication, and file system overheads."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:83
msgid ""
"\\[_closure NAR size_\\]{#closure-nar-size}: The sum of the [_NAR size_]"
"(#nar-size) of every store object in the [closure](../glossary.md#gloss-"
"closure) of the store object, including itself."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:86
msgid ""
"Unlike the _NAR size_, this is not quite an intrinsic property. As a "
"consequence of the [requisites](#references) being slightly ambiguous as "
"described above, the _closure NAR size_ is also slightly ambiguous; both "
"depend on the store in question and the exact closure in that store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:90
msgid ""
"Because of these issues, implementations currently typically compute this on "
"the fly rather than storing it."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object.md:92
msgid ""
"For details see the [store object info](../protocols/json/store-object-"
"info.md) JSON format or the [narinfo](../protocols/binary-cache/narinfo.md) "
"format."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:1
msgid "Content-Addressing Store Objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:3
msgid ""
"Just [like](../file-system-object/content-address.md) [File System Objects]"
"(../file-system-object.md), [Store Objects](../store-object.md) can also be "
"[content-addressed](../../glossary.md#gloss-content-address), unless they "
"are [input-addressed](../../glossary.md#gloss-input-addressed-store-object)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:7
msgid ""
"For store objects, the content address we produce will take the form of a "
"[Store Path](../store-path.md) rather than regular hash. In particular, the "
"content-addressing scheme will ensure that the digest of the store path is "
"solely computed from the"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:10
msgid "file system object graph (the root one and its children, if it has any)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:11
msgid "references"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:12
msgid "[store directory path](../store-path.md#store-directory-path)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:13
msgid "name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:15
msgid ""
"of the store object, and not any other information, which would not be an "
"intrinsic property of that store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:17
msgid ""
"For the full specification of the algorithms involved, see the "
"[specification of store path digests](../../protocols/store-path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:23
msgid "Content addressing each part of a store object"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:25
msgid "File System Objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:27
msgid ""
"With all currently-supported store object content-addressing methods, the "
"file system object is always [content-addressed](../file-system-object/"
"content-address.md) first, and then that hash is incorporated into content "
"address computation for the store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:31
msgid "References to other store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:33
msgid ""
"With all currently supported store object content addressing methods, other "
"objects are referred to by their regular (string-encoded-) [store paths](../"
"store-path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:36
msgid "Self-references"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:38
msgid ""
"Self-references however cannot be referred to by their path, because we are "
"in the midst of describing how to compute that path!"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:40
msgid ""
"The alternative would require finding as hash function fixed point, i.e. the "
"solution to an equation in the form"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:44
msgid ""
"which is computationally infeasible. As far as we know, this is equivalent "
"to finding a hash collision."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:47
msgid ""
"Instead we have a \"has self-reference\" boolean, which ends up affecting "
"the digest: In all currently-supported store object content-addressing "
"methods, when hashing the file system object data, any occurrence of store "
"object's own store path in the digested data is replaced with a [sentinel "
"value](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentinel_value). The hashes of these "
"modified input streams are used instead."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:51
msgid ""
"When validating the content address of a store object after the fact, the "
"above process works as written. However, when first creating the store "
"object we don't know the store object's store path, as explained just above. "
"We therefore, strictly speaking, do not know what value we will be replacing "
"with the sentinel value in the inputs to hash functions. What instead "
"happens is that the provisional store object --- the data from which we wish "
"to create a store object --- is paired with a provisional \"scratch\" store "
"path (that presumably was chosen when the data was created). That "
"provisional store path is instead what is replaced with the sentinel value, "
"rather than the final store object which we do not yet know."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:59
msgid ""
"It is an informal property of content-addressed store objects that the "
"choice of provisional store path should not matter. In other words, if a "
"provisional store object is prepared in the same way except for the choice "
"of provision store path, the provisional data need not be identical. But, "
"after the sentinel value is substituted in place of each provisional store "
"object's provision store path, the final so-normalized data _should_ be "
"identical."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:63
msgid ""
"If, conversely, the data after this normalization process is still "
"different, we'll compute a different content-address. The method of "
"preparing the provisional self-referenced data has _failed_ to be "
"deterministic in the sense of not _leaking_ the choice of provisional store "
"path --- a choice which is supposed to be arbitrary --- into the final store "
"object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:66
msgid ""
"This property is informal at this stage, because we are just describing "
"store objects, which have no formal notion of their origin. Without that, we "
"cannot rigorously define what it would mean for the process to be "
"insufficiently deterministic. When we cover [derivations](../../store/"
"derivation/index.md), we will be able to make this a formal property — not "
"of content-addressed store objects themselves, but of the derivations that "
"_produce_ them."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:70
msgid "Name and Store Directory"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:72
msgid ""
"These two items affect the digest in a way that is standard for store path "
"digest computations and not specific to content-addressing. Consult the "
"[specification of store path digests](../../protocols/store-path.md) for "
"further details."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:75
msgid "Content addressing Methods"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:77
msgid ""
"For historical reasons, we don't support all features in all combinations. "
"Each currently supported method of content addressing chooses a single "
"method of file system object hashing, and may offer some restrictions on "
"references. The names and store directories are unrestricted however."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:83
msgid ""
"This uses the corresponding [Flat](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-flat) method of file system object content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:85
msgid ""
"References are not supported: store objects with flat hashing _and_ "
"references can not be created."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:87
msgid "Text"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:89
msgid ""
"This also uses the corresponding [Flat](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-flat) method of file system object content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:91
msgid ""
"References to other store objects are supported, but self-references are not."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:93
msgid ""
"This is the only store-object content-addressing method that is not named "
"identically with a corresponding file system object method. It is somewhat "
"obscure, mainly used for \"drv files\" (derivations serialized as store "
"objects in their [\"ATerm\" file format](../../protocols/derivation-"
"aterm.md#use-for-encoding-to-store-object)). Prefer another method if "
"possible."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:98
msgid "Nix Archive"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:100
msgid ""
"This uses the corresponding [Nix Archive](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#serial-nix-archive) method of file system object content "
"addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:102
msgid ""
"References (to other store objects and self-references alike) are supported "
"so long as the hash algorithm is SHA-256, but not (neither kind) otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:104
msgid "Git"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:110
msgid ""
"This uses the corresponding [Git](../file-system-object/content-"
"address.md#git) method of file system object content addressing."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:112
msgid "References are not supported."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-object/content-address.md:114
msgid ""
"Only SHA-1 is supported at this time. If [SHA-256-based Git](https://git-"
"scm.com/docs/hash-function-transition) becomes more widespread, this "
"restriction will be revisited."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:1
msgid "Store Path and Store Directory"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix's [store object](./store-object.md) and [file system object](./file-"
"system-object.md) data models are minimal and abstract. But to actually be "
"used by software, store objects need to be made available through the "
"operating system's file system."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:6
msgid ""
"This is done by exposing all the store objects in a single _[store directory]"
"(#store-directory-path)_. Every entry in that directory is a _[store path "
"base name](#base-name)_ pointing to a store object. Store objects exposed in "
"this way can then be referenced by _[store paths](#store-path)_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:16
msgid "Store Path Base Name"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:18
msgid "Nix implements references to store objects as _store path base names_."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:20
msgid ""
"Think of a store path base name as an [opaque](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/"
"wiki/Opaque_data_type), [unique identifier](https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Unique_identifier): The only way to obtain a store path base name is by "
"adding or building store objects. A store path base name will always "
"reference exactly one store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:27
msgid "Store path base names are pairs of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:29
msgid "A 20-byte \\[digest\\]{#digest} for identification"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:30
msgid "A symbolic \\[name\\]{#name} for people to read"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:34
msgid "Digest: `q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:35
msgid "Name:   `firefox-33.1`"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:37
msgid "A store path base name is rendered to a string as the concatenation of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:39
msgid ""
"Digest rendered in [Nix32](../protocols/nix32.md), a variant of [Base32]"
"(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Base32) (20 hash bytes become 32 ASCII "
"characters)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:40
msgid "Hyphen (`-`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:54
msgid ""
"Exactly how the digest is calculated depends on the type of store object "
"being referenced. Store path digests are _supposed_ to be opaque, and so for "
"most operations, it is not necessary to know the details. That said, the "
"manual has a full [specification of store path digests](../protocols/store-"
"path.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:58
msgid "Store Directory Path"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:60
msgid ""
"Every [Nix store](./index.md) has a store directory path. This is an "
"absolute, lexically canonical (not containing any `..`, `.`, or similar) "
"path which points to the directory where all store objects are to be found."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:67
msgid ""
"The store directory defaults to `/nix/store`, but is in principle arbitrary."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:71
msgid ""
"A store path is the pair of a store directory path and a [store path base "
"name](#base-name). It is rendered to a file system path as the concatenation "
"of"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:74
msgid "[Store directory path](#store-directory-path) (typically `/nix/store`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:75
msgid "Path separator (`/`)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:76
msgid "The [store path base name](#base-name)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:86
msgid ""
"When we have fixed a given store, or given store directory path (that all "
"the stores in use share), the abstract syntax for store paths and the "
"abstract syntax for store path base names coincide: the store directory path "
"is known from context, so only the other two fields vary from one store path "
"to the next."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:88
msgid "Exposing Store Objects in OS File Systems"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:90
msgid ""
"Not every store can be accessed through the file system. But if the store "
"has a file system representation, the following should be true:"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:93
msgid ""
"The store directory path is canonical: no prefix of the path (i.e. path of "
"the first _n_ path segments) points to a symlink. In other words, the store "
"directory can be looked up from the store directory path without following "
"any symlinks. (This condition is a separate condition in addition to the "
"\"lexical canonicity\" described above, which is a property of just the path "
"itself. This (regular) \"canonicity\" is a property about the path and the "
"filesystem it navigates jointly.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:100
msgid ""
"The [`allow-symlinked-store`](../command-ref/conf-file.md#conf-allow-"
"symlinked-store) setting can be used to relax this requirement."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:102
msgid "The store directory path in fact points to a directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:104
msgid ""
"The store directory contains, for every store object in the store, the [file "
"system object](./file-system-object.md) of that store object at the "
"(rendered) [store path base name](#base-name). The permissions and other "
"metadata for these files in the store directory is in the normal form "
"described in [Exposing in OS file systems](./file-system-object/os-file-"
"system.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:107
msgid ""
"The above properties mean that the following file accesses will work. "
"Suppose we have a store available on the file system per the above rules, "
"and `q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq-firefox-33.1` is the store path base "
"name of a store object in that store."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:110
msgid ""
"Suppose that the store directory (path) is `/foo/bar`. Then, `/foo/bar/"
"q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq-firefox-33.1` exists and is the file system "
"object of that store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:113
msgid ""
"Suppose that we don't know what the store directory path of the store is, "
"but we do have a capability `storeDir` to the store directory on the file "
"system. (This would be a \"file descriptor\" on Unix, or a \"file handle\" "
"on Windows.) Then (using the Unix notation for this):"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:116
msgid ""
"```\n"
"openat(storeDir, \"q06x3jll2yfzckz2bzqak089p43ixkkq-firefox-33.1\", "
"O_NOFOLLOW)\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:119
msgid ""
"will succeed (so long as the file system object is not a symlink), and the "
"yielded capability will point to the file system object of that store object."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:121
msgid ""
"(The behavior for symlinks is harder to specify because of limitations in "
"POSIX.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:123
msgid "Relocating store objects"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:125
msgid ""
"The inclusion of the store directory path in the full rendered store path "
"means that the full rendered store path is not just derived from the "
"referenced store object itself, but depends on the store that the store "
"object is in. (And actually, all of the currently-supported ways of "
"computing the digest of a store path also depend on the store directory "
"path, as described in the [specification of store path digests](../protocols/"
"store-path.md). So this is also true even just for store path base names, in "
"general.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:129
msgid ""
"It is therefore important to consider which store a given store object "
"belongs to: Files in the store object can contain store paths, and processes "
"may read these paths. Nix can only guarantee referential integrity if store "
"paths do not cross store boundaries."
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:133
msgid "One can only copy store objects to a different store if"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:135
msgid "The source and target stores' directories match"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:139
msgid ""
"The store object in question has no references, that is, contains no store "
"paths"
msgstr ""

#: source/store/store-path.md:141
msgid ""
"One cannot copy a store object to a store with a different store directory. "
"Instead, it has to be rebuilt, together with all its dependencies. It is in "
"general not enough to replace the store directory string in file contents, "
"as this may render executables unusable by invalidating their internal "
"offsets or checksums."
msgstr ""

