#: source/package-management/index.md:1
msgid ""
"This chapter discusses how to do package management with Nix, i.e., how to "
"obtain, install, upgrade, and erase packages. This is the “user’s” "
"perspective of the Nix system — people who want to _create_ packages should "
"consult the chapter on the [Nix language](../language/index.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:1
msgid "Garbage Collection"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:3
msgid ""
"`nix-env` operations such as upgrades (`-u`) and uninstall (`-e`) never "
"actually delete packages from the system. All they do (as shown above) is to "
"create a new user environment that no longer contains symlinks to the "
"“deleted” packages."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:8
msgid ""
"Of course, since disk space is not infinite, unused packages should be "
"removed at some point. You can do this by running the Nix garbage collector. "
"It will remove from the Nix store any package not used (directly or "
"indirectly) by any generation of any profile."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:13
msgid ""
"Note however that as long as old generations reference a package, it will "
"not be deleted. After all, we wouldn’t be able to do a rollback otherwise. "
"So in order for garbage collection to be effective, you should also delete "
"(some) old generations. Of course, this should only be done if you are "
"certain that you will not need to roll back."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:19
msgid "To delete all old (non-current) generations of your current profile:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:25
msgid "Instead of `old` you can also specify a list of generations, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:31
msgid ""
"To delete all generations older than a specified number of days (except the "
"current generation), use the `d` suffix. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:38
msgid "deletes all generations older than two weeks."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:40
msgid ""
"After removing appropriate old generations you can run the garbage collector "
"as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:47
msgid ""
"The behaviour of the garbage collector is affected by the `keep-derivations` "
"(default: true) and `keep-outputs` (default: false) options in the Nix "
"configuration file. The defaults will ensure that all derivations that are "
"build-time dependencies of garbage collector roots will be kept and that all "
"output paths that are runtime dependencies will be kept as well. All other "
"derivations or paths will be collected. (This is usually what you want, but "
"while you are developing it may make sense to keep outputs to ensure that "
"rebuild times are quick.) If you are feeling uncertain, you can also first "
"view what files would be deleted:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:62
msgid ""
"Likewise, the option `--print-live` will show the paths that _won’t_ be "
"deleted."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:65
msgid ""
"There is also a convenient little utility `nix-collect-garbage`, which when "
"invoked with the `-d` (`--delete-old`) switch deletes all old generations of "
"all profiles in `/nix/var/nix/profiles`. So"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:73
msgid "is a quick and easy way to clean up your system."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collector-roots.md:1
msgid "Garbage Collector Roots"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collector-roots.md:3
msgid ""
"The roots of the garbage collector are all store paths to which there are "
"symlinks in the directory `prefix/nix/var/nix/gcroots`. For instance, the "
"following command makes the path `/nix/store/d718ef...-foo` a root of the "
"collector:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collector-roots.md:12
msgid ""
"That is, after this command, the garbage collector will not remove `/nix/"
"store/d718ef...-foo` or any of its dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collector-roots.md:15
msgid ""
"Subdirectories of `prefix/nix/var/nix/gcroots` are searched recursively. "
"Symlinks to store paths count as roots. Symlinks to non-store paths are "
"ignored, unless the non-store path is itself a symlink to a store path."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:3
msgid ""
"Profiles and user environments are Nix’s mechanism for implementing the "
"ability to allow different users to have different configurations, and to do "
"atomic upgrades and rollbacks. To understand how they work, it’s useful to "
"know a bit about how Nix works. In Nix, packages are stored in unique "
"locations in the _Nix store_ (typically, `/nix/store`). For instance, a "
"particular version of the Subversion package might be stored in a directory "
"`/nix/store/dpmvp969yhdqs7lm2r1a3gng7pyq6vy4-subversion-1.1.3/`, while "
"another version might be stored in `/nix/store/"
"5mq2jcn36ldlmh93yj1n8s9c95pj7c5s-subversion-1.1.2`. The long strings "
"prefixed to the directory names are cryptographic hashes (to be precise, 160-"
"bit truncations of SHA-256 hashes encoded in [Nix32](../protocols/nix32.md)) "
"of _all_ inputs involved in building the package — sources, dependencies, "
"compiler flags, and so on. So if two packages differ in any way, they end up "
"in different locations in the file system, so they don’t interfere with each "
"other. Here is what a part of a typical Nix store looks like:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:20
msgid "![](../figures/user-environments.png)"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:22
msgid "Of course, you wouldn’t want to type"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:28
msgid ""
"every time you want to run Subversion. Of course we could set up the `PATH` "
"environment variable to include the `bin` directory of every package we want "
"to use, but this is not very convenient since changing `PATH` doesn’t take "
"effect for already existing processes. The solution Nix uses is to create "
"directory trees of symlinks to _activated_ packages. These are called _user "
"environments_ and they are packages themselves (though automatically "
"generated by `nix-env`), so they too reside in the Nix store. For instance, "
"in the figure above, the user environment `/nix/store/0c1p5z4kda11...-user-"
"env` contains a symlink to just Subversion 1.1.2 (arrows in the figure "
"indicate symlinks). This would be what we would obtain if we had done"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:44
msgid "on a set of Nix expressions that contained Subversion 1.1.2."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:46
msgid ""
"This doesn’t in itself solve the problem, of course; you wouldn’t want to "
"type `/nix/store/0c1p5z4kda11...-user-env/bin/svn` either. That’s why there "
"are symlinks outside of the store that point to the user environments in the "
"store; for instance, the symlinks `default-42-link` and `default-43-link` in "
"the example. These are called _generations_ since every time you perform a "
"`nix-env` operation, a new user environment is generated based on the "
"current one. For instance, generation 43 was created from generation 42 when "
"we did"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:59
msgid ""
"on a set of Nix expressions that contained Firefox and a new version of "
"Subversion."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:62
msgid ""
"Generations are grouped together into _profiles_ so that different users "
"don’t interfere with each other if they don’t want to. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:73
msgid ""
"This shows a profile called `default`. The file `default` itself is actually "
"a symlink that points to the current generation. When we do a `nix-env` "
"operation, a new user environment and generation link are created based on "
"the current one, and finally the `default` symlink is made to point at the "
"new generation. This last step is atomic on Unix, which explains how we can "
"do atomic upgrades. (Note that the building/installing of new packages "
"doesn’t interfere in any way with old packages, since they are stored in "
"different locations in the Nix store.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:83
msgid ""
"If you find that you want to undo a `nix-env` operation, you can just do"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:89
msgid ""
"which will just make the current generation link point at the previous link. "
"E.g., `default` would be made to point at `default-42-link`. You can also "
"switch to a specific generation:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:97
msgid ""
"which in this example would roll forward to generation 43 again. You can "
"also see all available generations:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:104
msgid ""
"You generally wouldn’t have `/nix/var/nix/profiles/some-profile/bin` in your "
"`PATH`. Rather, there is a symlink `~/.nix-profile` that points to your "
"current profile. This means that you should put `~/.nix-profile/bin` in your "
"`PATH` (and indeed, that’s what the initialisation script `/nix/etc/"
"profile.d/nix.sh` does). This makes it easier to switch to a different "
"profile. You can do that using the command `nix-env --switch-profile`:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:118
msgid ""
"These commands switch to the `my-profile` and default profile, respectively. "
"If the profile doesn’t exist, it will be created automatically. You should "
"be careful about storing a profile in another location than the `profiles` "
"directory, since otherwise it might not be used as a root of the [garbage "
"collector](garbage-collection.md)."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:124
msgid ""
"All `nix-env` operations work on the profile pointed to by `~/.nix-profile`, "
"but you can override this using the `--profile` option (abbreviation `-p`):"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/profiles.md:132
msgid "This will _not_ change the `~/.nix-profile` symlink."
msgstr ""

