#: source/release-notes/index.md:1
msgid "Nix Release Notes"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/index.md:3
msgid "The Nix release cycle is calendar-based as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/index.md:5
msgid ""
"Nix has a release cycle of roughly 6 weeks. Notable changes and additions "
"are announced in the release notes for each version."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/index.md:8
msgid "The supported Nix versions are:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/index.md:9
msgid "The latest release"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/index.md:10
msgid ""
"The version used in the stable NixOS release, which is announced in the "
"[NixOS release notes](https://nixos.org/manual/nixos/stable/release-"
"notes.html#ch-release-notes)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/index.md:12
msgid ""
"Bugfixes and security issues are backported to every supported version. "
"Patch releases are published as needed."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:1
msgid "Release 0.10 (2006-10-06)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:5
msgid ""
"This version of Nix uses Berkeley DB 4.4 instead of 4.3. The database is "
"upgraded automatically, but you should be careful not to use old versions of "
"Nix that still use Berkeley DB 4.3. In particular, if you use a Nix "
"installed through Nix, you should run"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:12
msgid "first."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:16
msgid ""
"Also, the database schema has changed slighted to fix a performance issue "
"(see below). When you run any Nix 0.10 command for the first time, the "
"database will be upgraded automatically. This is irreversible."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:21
msgid "`nix-env` usability improvements:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:23
msgid ""
"An option `--compare-versions` (or `-c`) has been added to `nix-"
"env                                                 --query` to allow you to "
"compare installed versions of packages to available versions, or vice versa. "
"An easy way to see if you are up to date with what’s in your subscribed "
"channels is `nix-env -qc \\*`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:30
msgid ""
"`nix-env --query` now takes as arguments a list of package names about which "
"to show information, just like `--install`, etc.: for example, `nix-env -q "
"gcc`. Note that to show all derivations, you need to specify `\\*`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:35
msgid ""
"`nix-env -i                                                 pkgname` will "
"now install the highest available version of _pkgname_, rather than "
"installing all available versions (which would probably give collisions) "
"(`NIX-31`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:40
msgid ""
"`nix-env (-i|-u) --dry-run` now shows exactly which missing paths will be "
"built or substituted."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:43
msgid ""
"`nix-env -qa --description` shows human-readable descriptions of packages, "
"provided that they have a `meta.description` attribute (which most packages "
"in Nixpkgs don’t have yet)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:47
msgid "New language features:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:49
msgid ""
"Reference scanning (which happens after each build) is much faster and takes "
"a constant amount of memory."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:52
msgid "String interpolation. Expressions like"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:54
msgid ""
"    \"--with-freetype2-library=\" + freetype + \"/lib\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:56
msgid "can now be written as"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:58
msgid ""
"    \"--with-freetype2-library=${freetype}/lib\"\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:60
msgid ""
"You can write arbitrary expressions within `${...}`, not just identifiers."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:63
msgid "Multi-line string literals."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:65
msgid ""
"String concatenations can now involve derivations, as in the example `\"--"
"with-freetype2-library=\"                                                 + "
"freetype + \"/lib\"`. This was not previously possible because we need to "
"register that a derivation that uses such a string is dependent on "
"`freetype`. The evaluator now properly propagates this information. "
"Consequently, the subpath operator (`~`) has been deprecated."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:73
msgid ""
"Default values of function arguments can now refer to other function "
"arguments; that is, all arguments are in scope in the default values "
"(`NIX-45`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:77
msgid ""
"Lots of new built-in primitives, such as functions for list manipulation and "
"integer arithmetic. See the manual for a complete list. All primops are now "
"available in the set `builtins`, allowing one to test for the availability "
"of primop in a backwards-compatible way."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:83
msgid ""
"Real let-expressions: `let x "
"= ...;                                                 ... z = ...; in ...`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:86
msgid ""
"New commands `nix-pack-closure` and `nix-unpack-closure` than can be used to "
"easily transfer a store path with all its dependencies to another machine. "
"Very convenient whenever you have some package on your machine and you want "
"to copy it somewhere else."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:91
msgid "XML support:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:93
msgid ""
"`nix-env -q --xml` prints the installed or available packages in an XML "
"representation for easy processing by other tools."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:96
msgid ""
"`nix-instantiate --eval-only                                                 "
"--xml` prints an XML representation of the resulting term. (The new flag `--"
"strict` forces ‘deep’ evaluation of the result, i.e., list elements and "
"attributes are evaluated recursively.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:101
msgid ""
"In Nix expressions, the primop `builtins.toXML` converts a term to an XML "
"representation. This is primarily useful for passing structured information "
"to builders."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:105
msgid ""
"You can now unambiguously specify which derivation to build or install in "
"`nix-env`, `nix-instantiate` and `nix-build` using the `--attr` / `-A` "
"flags, which takes an attribute name as argument. (Unlike symbolic package "
"names such as `subversion-1.4.0`, attribute names in an attribute set are "
"unique.) For instance, a quick way to perform a test build of a package in "
"Nixpkgs is `nix-build         pkgs/top-level/all-packages.nix -A         "
"foo`. `nix-env -q         --attr` shows the attribute names corresponding to "
"each derivation."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:115
msgid ""
"If the top-level Nix expression used by `nix-env`, `nix-instantiate` or `nix-"
"build` evaluates to a function whose arguments all have default values, the "
"function will be called automatically. Also, the new command-line switch `--"
"arg         name         value` can be used to specify function arguments on "
"the command line."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:123
msgid ""
"`nix-install-package --url         URL` allows a package to be installed "
"directly from the given URL."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:126
msgid ""
"Nix now works behind an HTTP proxy server; just set the standard environment "
"variables `http_proxy`, `https_proxy`, `ftp_proxy` or `all_proxy` "
"appropriately. Functions such as `fetchurl` in Nixpkgs also respect these "
"variables."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:131
msgid ""
"`nix-build -o         symlink` allows the symlink to the build result to be "
"named something other than `result`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:135
msgid "Platform support:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:137
msgid ""
"Support for 64-bit platforms, provided a [suitably patched ATerm library]"
"(http://bugzilla.sen.cwi.nl:8080/show_bug.cgi?id=606) is used. Also, files "
"larger than 2 GiB are now supported."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:141
msgid ""
"Added support for Cygwin (Windows, `i686-cygwin`), Mac OS X on Intel (`i686-"
"darwin`) and Linux on PowerPC (`powerpc-linux`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:144
msgid ""
"Users of SMP and multicore machines will appreciate that the number of "
"builds to be performed in parallel can now be specified in the configuration "
"file in the `build-max-jobs` setting."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:149
msgid "Garbage collector improvements:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:151
msgid ""
"Open files (such as running programs) are now used as roots of the garbage "
"collector. This prevents programs that have been uninstalled from being "
"garbage collected while they are still running. The script that detects "
"these additional runtime roots (`find-runtime-roots.pl`) is inherently "
"system-specific, but it should work on Linux and on all platforms that have "
"the `lsof` utility."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:159
msgid ""
"`nix-store --gc` (a.k.a. `nix-collect-garbage`) prints out the number of "
"bytes freed on standard output. `nix-"
"store                                                 --gc --print-dead` "
"shows how many bytes would be freed by an actual garbage collection."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:164
msgid ""
"`nix-collect-garbage -d` removes all old generations of _all_ profiles "
"before calling the actual garbage collector (`nix-"
"store                                                 --gc`). This is an "
"easy way to get rid of all old packages in the Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:169
msgid ""
"`nix-store` now has an operation `--delete` to delete specific paths from "
"the Nix store. It won’t delete reachable (non-garbage) paths unless `--"
"ignore-liveness` is specified."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:173
msgid ""
"Berkeley DB 4.4’s process registry feature is used to recover from crashed "
"Nix processes."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:176
msgid ""
"A performance issue has been fixed with the `referer` table, which stores "
"the inverse of the `references` table (i.e., it tells you what store paths "
"refer to a given path). Maintaining this table could take a quadratic amount "
"of time, as well as a quadratic amount of Berkeley DB log file space (in "
"particular when running the garbage collector) (`NIX-23`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:183
msgid ""
"Nix now catches the `TERM` and `HUP` signals in addition to the `INT` "
"signal. So you can now do a `killall         nix-store` without triggering a "
"database recovery."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:187
msgid "`bsdiff` updated to version 4.3."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:189
msgid ""
"Substantial performance improvements in expression evaluation and `nix-env "
"-qa`, all thanks to [Valgrind](http://valgrind.org/). Memory use has been "
"reduced by a factor 8 or so. Big speedup by memoisation of path hashing."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:194
msgid "Lots of bug fixes, notably:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:196
msgid ""
"Make sure that the garbage collector can run successfully when the disk is "
"full (`NIX-18`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:199
msgid ""
"`nix-env` now locks the profile to prevent races between concurrent `nix-"
"env` operations on the same profile (`NIX-7`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:202
msgid ""
"Removed misleading messages from `nix-env -i` (e.g., "
"``installing                                                 `foo'`` "
"followed by ``uninstalling                                                 "
"`foo'``) (`NIX-17`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:207
msgid ""
"Nix source distributions are a lot smaller now since we no longer include a "
"full copy of the Berkeley DB source distribution (but only the bits we need)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.md:211
msgid ""
"Header files are now installed so that external programs can use the Nix "
"libraries."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.1.md:1
msgid "Release 0.10.1 (2006-10-11)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.10.1.md:3
msgid ""
"This release fixes two somewhat obscure bugs that occur when evaluating Nix "
"expressions that are stored inside the Nix store (`NIX-67`). These do not "
"affect most users."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:1
msgid "Release 0.11 (2007-12-31)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix 0.11 has many improvements over the previous stable release. The most "
"important improvement is secure multi-user support. It also features many "
"usability enhancements and language extensions, many of them prompted by "
"NixOS, the purely functional Linux distribution based on Nix. Here is an "
"(incomplete) list:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:9
msgid ""
"Secure multi-user support. A single Nix store can now be shared between "
"multiple (possible untrusted) users. This is an important feature for NixOS, "
"where it allows non-root users to install software. The old setuid method "
"for sharing a store between multiple users has been removed. Details for "
"setting up a multi-user store can be found in the manual."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:16
msgid ""
"The new command `nix-copy-closure` gives you an easy and efficient way to "
"exchange software between machines. It copies the missing parts of the "
"closure of a set of store path to or from a remote machine via `ssh`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:21
msgid ""
"A new kind of string literal: strings between double single-quotes (`''`) "
"have indentation “intelligently” removed. This allows large strings (such as "
"shell scripts or configuration file fragments in NixOS) to cleanly follow "
"the indentation of the surrounding expression. It also requires much less "
"escaping, since `''` is less common in most languages than `\"`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:28
msgid ""
"`nix-env` `--set` modifies the current generation of a profile so that it "
"contains exactly the specified derivation, and nothing else. For example, "
"`nix-env -p /nix/var/nix/profiles/browser --set         firefox` lets the "
"profile named `browser` contain just Firefox."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:33
msgid ""
"`nix-env` now maintains meta-information about installed packages in "
"profiles. The meta-information is the contents of the `meta` attribute of "
"derivations, such as `description` or `homepage`. The command `nix-env -q --"
"xml         --meta` shows all meta-information."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:39
msgid ""
"`nix-env` now uses the `meta.priority` attribute of derivations to resolve "
"filename collisions between packages. Lower priority values denote a higher "
"priority. For instance, the GCC wrapper package and the Binutils package in "
"Nixpkgs both have a file `bin/ld`, so previously if you tried to install "
"both you would get a collision. Now, on the other hand, the GCC wrapper "
"declares a higher priority than Binutils, so the former’s `bin/ld` is "
"symlinked in the user environment."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:48
msgid ""
"`nix-env -i / -u`: instead of breaking package ties by version, break them "
"by priority and version number. That is, if there are multiple packages with "
"the same name, then pick the package with the highest priority, and only use "
"the version if there are multiple packages with the same priority."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:54
msgid ""
"This makes it possible to mark specific versions/variant in Nixpkgs more or "
"less desirable than others. A typical example would be a beta version of "
"some package (e.g., `gcc-4.2.0rc1`) which should not be installed even "
"though it is the highest version, except when it is explicitly selected "
"(e.g., `nix-env -i         gcc-4.2.0rc1`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:61
msgid ""
"`nix-env --set-flag` allows meta attributes of installed packages to be "
"modified. There are several attributes that can be usefully modified, "
"because they affect the behaviour of `nix-env` or the user environment build "
"script:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:66
msgid "`meta.priority` can be changed to resolve filename clashes (see above)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:69
msgid ""
"`meta.keep` can be set to `true` to prevent the package from being upgraded "
"or replaced. Useful if you want to hang on to an older version of a package."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:73
msgid ""
"`meta.active` can be set to `false` to “disable” the package. That is, no "
"symlinks will be generated to the files of the package, but it remains part "
"of the profile (so it won’t be garbage-collected). Set it back to `true` to "
"re-enable the package."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:79
msgid ""
"`nix-env -q` now has a flag `--prebuilt-only` (`-b`) that causes `nix-env` "
"to show only those derivations whose output is already in the Nix store or "
"that can be substituted (i.e., downloaded from somewhere). In other words, "
"it shows the packages that can be installed “quickly”, i.e., don’t need to "
"be built from source. The `-b` flag is also available in `nix-env -i` and "
"`nix-env -u` to filter out derivations for which no pre-built binary is "
"available."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:87
msgid ""
"The new option `--argstr` (in `nix-env`, `nix-instantiate` and `nix-build`) "
"is like `--arg`, except that the value is a string. For example, `--argstr "
"system         i686-linux` is equivalent to `--arg system         \\\"i686-"
"linux\\\"` (note that `--argstr` prevents annoying quoting around shell "
"arguments)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:94
msgid ""
"`nix-store` has a new operation `--read-log` (`-l`) `paths` that shows the "
"build log of the given paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:97
msgid ""
"Nix now uses Berkeley DB 4.5. The database is upgraded automatically, but "
"you should be careful not to use old versions of Nix that still use Berkeley "
"DB 4.4."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:101
msgid ""
"The option `--max-silent-time` (corresponding to the configuration setting "
"`build-max-silent-time`) allows you to set a timeout on builds — if a build "
"produces no output on `stdout` or `stderr` for the given number of seconds, "
"it is terminated. This is useful for recovering automatically from builds "
"that are stuck in an infinite loop."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:108
msgid ""
"`nix-channel`: each subscribed channel is its own attribute in the top-level "
"expression generated for the channel. This allows disambiguation (e.g. `nix-"
"env         -i -A nixpkgs_unstable.firefox`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:113
msgid ""
"The substitutes table has been removed from the database. This makes "
"operations such as `nix-pull` and `nix-channel --update` much, much faster."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:117
msgid ""
"`nix-pull` now supports bzip2-compressed manifests. This speeds up channels."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:120
msgid ""
"`nix-prefetch-url` now has a limited form of caching. This is used by `nix-"
"channel` to prevent unnecessary downloads when the channel hasn’t changed."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:124
msgid ""
"`nix-prefetch-url` now by default computes the SHA-256 hash of the file "
"instead of the MD5 hash. In calls to `fetchurl` you should pass the `sha256` "
"attribute instead of `md5`. You can pass either a hexadecimal or a base-32 "
"encoding of the hash."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:129
msgid ""
"Nix can now perform builds in an automatically generated “chroot”. This "
"prevents a builder from accessing stuff outside of the Nix store, and thus "
"helps ensure purity. This is an experimental feature."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:134
msgid ""
"The new command `nix-store         --optimise` reduces Nix store disk space "
"usage by finding identical files in the store and hard-linking them to each "
"other. It typically reduces the size of the store by something like 25-35%."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:139
msgid ""
"`~/.nix-defexpr` can now be a directory, in which case the Nix expressions "
"in that directory are combined into an attribute set, with the file names "
"used as the names of the attributes. The command `nix-env         --import` "
"(which set the `~/.nix-defexpr` symlink) is removed."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:145
msgid ""
"Derivations can specify the new special attribute `allowedReferences` to "
"enforce that the references in the output of a derivation are a subset of a "
"declared set of paths. For example, if `allowedReferences` is an empty list, "
"then the output must not have any references. This is used in NixOS to check "
"that generated files such as initial ramdisks for booting Linux don’t have "
"any dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:153
msgid ""
"The new attribute `exportReferencesGraph` allows builders access to the "
"references graph of their inputs. This is used in NixOS for tasks such as "
"generating ISO-9660 images that contain a Nix store populated with the "
"closure of certain paths."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:158
msgid ""
"Fixed-output derivations (like `fetchurl`) can define the attribute "
"`impureEnvVars` to allow external environment variables to be passed to "
"builders. This is used in Nixpkgs to support proxy configuration, among "
"other things."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.11.md:163
msgid ""
"Several new built-in functions: `builtins.attrNames`, "
"`builtins.filterSource`, `builtins.isAttrs`, `builtins.isFunction`, "
"`builtins.listToAttrs`, `builtins.stringLength`, `builtins.sub`, "
"`builtins.substring`, `throw`, `builtins.trace`, `builtins.readFile`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:1
msgid "Release 0.12 (2008-11-20)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:3
msgid ""
"Nix no longer uses Berkeley DB to store Nix store metadata. The principal "
"advantages of the new storage scheme are: it works properly over decent "
"implementations of NFS (allowing Nix stores to be shared between multiple "
"machines); no recovery is needed when a Nix process crashes; no write access "
"is needed for read-only operations; no more running out of Berkeley DB locks "
"on certain operations."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:11
msgid ""
"You still need to compile Nix with Berkeley DB support if you want Nix to "
"automatically convert your old Nix store to the new schema. If you don’t "
"need this, you can build Nix with the `configure` option `--disable-old-db-"
"compat`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:16
msgid ""
"After the automatic conversion to the new schema, you can delete the old "
"Berkeley DB files:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:22
msgid ""
"The new metadata is stored in the directories `/nix/var/nix/db/info` and `/"
"nix/var/nix/db/referrer`. Though the metadata is stored in human-readable "
"plain-text files, they are not intended to be human-editable, as Nix is "
"rather strict about the format."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:27
msgid ""
"The new storage schema may or may not require less disk space than the "
"Berkeley DB environment, mostly depending on the cluster size of your file "
"system. With 1 KiB clusters (which seems to be the `ext3` default nowadays) "
"it usually takes up much less space."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:32
msgid ""
"There is a new substituter that copies paths directly from other (remote) "
"Nix stores mounted somewhere in the filesystem. For instance, you can speed "
"up an installation by mounting some remote Nix store that already has the "
"packages in question via NFS or `sshfs`. The environment variable "
"`NIX_OTHER_STORES` specifies the locations of the remote Nix directories, "
"e.g. `/mnt/remote-fs/nix`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:39
msgid ""
"New `nix-store` operations `--dump-db` and `--load-db` to dump and reload "
"the Nix database."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:42
msgid ""
"The garbage collector has a number of new options to allow only some of the "
"garbage to be deleted. The option `--max-freed N` tells the collector to "
"stop after at least _N_ bytes have been deleted. The option `--max-"
"links         N` tells it to stop after the link count on `/nix/store` has "
"dropped below _N_. This is useful for very large Nix stores on filesystems "
"with a 32000 subdirectories limit (like `ext3`). The option `--use-atime` "
"causes store paths to be deleted in order of ascending last access time. "
"This allows non-recently used stuff to be deleted. The option `--max-atime "
"time` specifies an upper limit to the last accessed time of paths that may "
"be deleted. For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:54
msgid ""
"```\n"
"    $ nix-store --gc -v --max-atime $(date +%s -d \"2 months ago\")\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:58
msgid "deletes everything that hasn’t been accessed in two months."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:60
msgid ""
"`nix-env` now uses optimistic profile locking when performing an operation "
"like installing or upgrading, instead of setting an exclusive lock on the "
"profile. This allows multiple `nix-env -i / -u / -e` operations on the same "
"profile in parallel. If a `nix-env` operation sees at the end that the "
"profile was changed in the meantime by another process, it will just "
"restart. This is generally cheap because the build results are still in the "
"Nix store."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:68
msgid ""
"The option `--dry-run` is now supported by `nix-store -r` and `nix-build`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:71
msgid ""
"The information previously shown by `--dry-run` (i.e., which derivations "
"will be built and which paths will be substituted) is now always shown by "
"`nix-env`, `nix-store -r` and `nix-build`. The total download size of "
"substitutable paths is now also shown. For instance, a build will show "
"something like"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:86
msgid "Language features:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:88
msgid "@-patterns as in Haskell. For instance, in a function definition"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:92
msgid ""
"`args` refers to the argument as a whole, which is further pattern-matched "
"against the attribute set pattern `{x, y, z}`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:95
msgid ""
"“`...`” (ellipsis) patterns. An attribute set pattern can now say `...` at "
"the end of the attribute name list to specify that the function takes _at "
"least_ the listed attributes, while ignoring additional attributes. For "
"instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:102
msgid ""
"defines a function that accepts any attribute set that includes at least the "
"three listed attributes."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:105
msgid ""
"New primops: `builtins.parseDrvName` (split a package name string like "
"`\"nix-0.12pre12876\"` into its name and version components, e.g. `\"nix\"` "
"and `\"0.12pre12876\"`), `builtins.compareVersions` (compare two version "
"strings using the same algorithm that `nix-env` uses), `builtins.length` "
"(efficiently compute the length of a list), `builtins.mul` (integer "
"multiplication), `builtins.div` (integer division)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:113
msgid ""
"`nix-prefetch-url` now supports `mirror://` URLs, provided that the "
"environment variable `NIXPKGS_ALL` points at a Nixpkgs tree."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:116
msgid ""
"Removed the commands `nix-pack-closure` and `nix-unpack-closure`. You can do "
"almost the same thing but much more efficiently by doing `nix-store --"
"export         $(nix-store -qR paths) > closure` and `nix-store --import "
"<         closure`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.12.md:122
msgid ""
"Lots of bug fixes, including a big performance bug in the handling of "
"`with`\\-expressions."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:1
msgid "Release 0.13 (2009-11-05)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:3
msgid "This is primarily a bug fix release. It has some new features:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:5
msgid "Syntactic sugar for writing nested attribute sets. Instead of"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:7
msgid ""
"    {\n"
"      foo = {\n"
"        bar = 123;\n"
"        xyzzy = true;\n"
"      };\n"
"      a = { b = { c = \"d\"; }; };\n"
"    }\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:15
msgid "you can write"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:17
msgid ""
"    {\n"
"      foo.bar = 123;\n"
"      foo.xyzzy = true;\n"
"      a.b.c = \"d\";\n"
"    }\n"
"    "
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:23
msgid "This is useful, for instance, in NixOS configuration files."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:25
msgid ""
"Support for Nix channels generated by Hydra, the Nix-based continuous build "
"system. (Hydra generates NAR archives on the fly, so the size and hash of "
"these archives isn’t known in advance.)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:29
msgid ""
"Support `i686-linux` builds directly on `x86_64-linux` Nix installations. "
"This is implemented using the `personality()` syscall, which causes `uname` "
"to return `i686` in child processes."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:33
msgid ""
"Various improvements to the `chroot` support. Building in a `chroot` works "
"quite well now."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:36
msgid ""
"Nix no longer blocks if it tries to build a path and another process is "
"already building the same path. Instead it tries to build another buildable "
"path first. This improves parallelism."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:40
msgid "Support for large (\\> 4 GiB) files in NAR archives."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:42
msgid "Various (performance) improvements to the remote build mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:44
msgid ""
"New primops: `builtins.addErrorContext` (to add a string to stack traces — "
"useful for debugging), `builtins.isBool`, `builtins.isString`, "
"`builtins.isInt`, `builtins.intersectAttrs`."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:48
msgid "OpenSolaris support (Sander van der Burg)."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:50
msgid ""
"Stack traces are no longer displayed unless the `--show-trace` option is "
"used."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.13.md:53
msgid ""
"The scoping rules for `inherit                 (e) ...` in recursive "
"attribute sets have changed. The expression _e_ can now refer to the "
"attributes defined in the containing set."
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.14.md:1
msgid "Release 0.14 (2010-02-04)"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.14.md:3
msgid "This release has the following improvements:"
msgstr ""

#: source/release-notes/rl-0.14.md:5
msgid ""
"The garbage collector now starts deleting garbage much faster than before. "
"It no longer determines liveness of all paths in the store, but does so on "
"demand."
